      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>2 FOR DELICIOUS MoDOUGALUS PATENT SELF RAISING FLOUR PANCAKES USE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>1 t.,,:,1e..',</head>
        <p>1 t.,,:,1e..',</p>
        <p>'s Churc</p>
        <p>A very happy arid successful gathering was the annual parochial tea party and concert in connection with St. Peter's Church, which took place in the schools, John-street, on Monday evening. A !arse number of parishioners and friends sat down to ten. The entertainment which followed was presided over by Mr T S Steel who, in a speech on the work of the parish, said they had not sufficient money to enable them to rebuild the Mission, but be hoped the parishioners would make some effort in that direction. They had a new Church Army captain, and he hoped he would endeavour to make the services even more popular and soon find the Mission altogeih4r too small for the work he carried on there. (Hear, hear.) The Sunday school was too crowded to admit all the classes, wil h the result that two or three Lad to be taken in the Mission Room.</p>
        <p>The VICAR (Rev B Percival) acknowledged with gratitude the offer of Mr Hightail, who succeeded the late Alderman John Roberts at Messrs. IVaring and Co.'s Tannery, to supply new surplices for the choir. Mr Clayton, who was Elso con- nected with the firm, had promissd £5 for the purchase of new hymn and prayer books. If they were a living churcit they would always want something, and St Peter's Church was a living church. To the Church Pastoral Aid Society they were sending the largest sum they had ever raised in the pariah, namely, £3B, and last year they sent the highest sum for foreign missions, namely, £2B. He wished someone would present them with a new Church flail, as it aas needed for the extension of the Sunday school, as that room was too crowded with children on the Sunday and the Bible class also was quite full. The Rev J H GARNETT also spoke, and the Chairman and artistes were thanked for their services, on the motion of Mr CLAYTON, seconded by Mr MCQUILLAM. The entertainment was of a most varied ann interest- ing character. It was opened by Miss D Percival and Miss E Padmore, who contributed a pianoforte duet, entitled " Cavalerie Legere." Miss Kershaw sang "The valley of laughter," " Down the vale," and "The magic month of Ma," Mr T Reddish, "I oin Bowling," and Mr E Reddish "Friars of orders prey," "I tritunpn, I triumph," and "Simon the cellarer," for which all the singers were warmly arplauded. Miss D Percival recited " Quaker sinner," very effectively, and the choir boys acquitted themseves well in the song and chorus," Drummer's song," in which t`iety were assisted by Miss Powell. A humorous skelub entitled" Ten little nigger boys," was well enacted and created much amusement. The last item was the familiar sketch Cox and Box," in whioh Mr E Reddish appeared as Cox, Mr E Padmore as Box, and Mr J Greases as Mrs Bouncer. The pianoforte accompanists for the songs were supplied by Mr Padmore (organist), Mr J Greases and Miss Padmore.</p>
        <p>these cases the conversion did not happen by chance, but was the climax of experiences which had gone before. The preacher showed that what applied to the phNsical world was also applicable to the spiritual. Conversion, he said, was a pessing out of a slate of uncertainty into the light of ascertained truth ' • a turnivg from a life of sin to a life of goodness. In conclusion, he demonstrated that all the noblest ideals that could possess 1111111 were exempl.fied in the life of Christ.</p>
