      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening last the Rev W Reid, preaching from Esther, iv., 2, contrasted the attitude towards the sores and griefs of life, symbolised in the sackcloth of the oriental monarch,with that of Christ. The preacher dealt with the policy of closing the eyes and ears against life's problems and troubles its shortsighted- ness, and inevitable result as shown by history, also with the failure of all effort, however sincere, towards redress and reform 'which, ignoring the Christ of the cross seeks to work alone, lie said the only ministry the world had yet witnessed, which had squarely faced life, and at the same time undertaken to not merely better its conditions and surroundings, not merely to deliver from sin's consequences, but to eraclicßte sin to pry every tear, to abolish death, was that of Cluist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday, presided over by Mr. McEachern.—Mr. James Ganld gave the address on "A good name is better than great riches.' It was often asked : " What was in a name ?" Well, some- times a name meant a great deal. He admired those who tried to keep up their family good name. The very memory of a sainted father and mother had often pre- served a young man or woman from doing that which would taint the honour of the parents. Bob Bridges who was picked up in Manchester a hen a baby by a policeman and taken to the Is orichouse, grew up to gain an honoured name in Manchester, although he was given that name in a workhouse. He also related hear- ing a talented singer disguised in the streets of Manchester, who by had habits had fallen from the stage she was a star artiste. She was now ashamed of her name. Let them try and make a good name for purity, honesty and integrity.—The Chairman read the lesson and Mr. A. Healey led the prayer.—Mr. W. H. Payton rendered an organ solo.—Tha President vaiced a vote of sympathy to Mrs. S. Hassall on the death of her husband. The late Mr Hassan bad always proved a friend to the Brotherhood.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church. The Rev .1 A Patten, M.A., of Seacornbe, was the preacher at IVyclifre Congregational Church on Sunday. A portion of toe morning service was devoted to the children, and the preacher related incidents in the life of Saint Isidore. The subject of the sermon was He that believeth on the Son of God bath the witness in himself" (( John, v 10.) He said we lived in a time in which every man was expected to give some reason for the faith that WE S in him. People were wonderfully open-minded and would listen to reason and argument. Christianity was not found in externals but was to be found in snan's own heart. In the middle ages it was thought that the Church was the vehicle of divine truth, and at a later period many rested their faith on the Church's creed. The preacher showed that in neither of these, nor in the Bible alone was truth to be found but in man's own experience The evening discours3 was based on the text "Marvel not that I said unto thee, ye must be born again,' (John 3., 7.) Discussing the question as to whether conversion was a reasonable experience, he showed bow the law of change operated in nature and in human life. In illustration of this fact be gave four instances of notable conversions taken from the lives of Isaac Newton, Florence Nightingale, Dr Bernardo and Thomas Carlyle. He proceeded to show that in all</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan CI arch.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan CI arch.</p>
        <p>The Rev 11 Williamson, B.A , was the preatlier at Bold-street on Sunday niget. In the course of his remarks he said that human history. has its ups and downs, there are times when the note of inspiration is dominant an.] times when it seems to be absent. Times when hope and joy and courage predominate, are often followed by times when fears and doubts abound. The great era of propbetistn in Israel was followed by ihe five hundred years of the dark night of legalism. The drab and uneventful Middle Ages were followed by the glories of the renaissance and the reformation. Our nineteenth century was in many ways a great one but how does it stand with the twentieth century ? Tette, there is ability enough, but no man stands pre-eminent in the public eye, with the sacred fire of gain us burning in his soul. At present, this is not ore of the greatest ages of human history, having regard to its spiritual achievement. Still, it is of little avail to lament the Good Old Time-," and to curse the present. Moreover, in such tam there must be recognised is latent atheism, which would imply that this age is not of God's creation, as were ihe bygone ages. Instead of complaining, we must rather recognise that there is a grave danger of our tecoming imbued with a spirit of unreality slid artificiality—blindly dingle% to creees arid institutions, and forms of religious life of the past, which have now no message for cite soul. It reeds to be seeu that no t)pe of doctrine or of religious life ever came into being became some great rcau so willed it. "pie roots struck far deeper than the mortal mind. They sprang trim the deptha of things, ratter than front the brain of men. Luther's great doctrines were not spun by his brain, but came from Almighty God ' and preacher and hearers were alike inspired by God, who bad spoken to both. It may be, too, that the very fact : that past creeds fail to appeal to-day, is just 83 much God's work in our hearts, as was their efficacy centuries ago, since Gcd is with us still. Therefore, the lesson for the Twentieth Century mind to learn is simply, sincerity throughout the whole being, and throughout the entire life. Better far to have a creed with one article that appeals, rather than one with twenty articles that are merely repeated. In this way we shall best prepare ourselves to welcome the coming golden age. For the golden age is not, as blind tradition has it, in the past, but in the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>"Darwinism and Religion."</head>
        <p>"Darwinism and Religion."</p>
        <p>This was the subject of the Rev Dr Mellor's sermon at Cairo-street Cbripel on Sunday evening. Having expounded the Darwinian theory in a most, interesting way, he said that if heredity and euvironment were the controlling forces, we could only conclude that the picture in the hu man world must be pretty much the same as it was represented to be in the animal world—a struggle to survive, which in itself had no moral and spiritual value, abd to which we sought. to attach moral and spiritual significance, only in error. In the human world, the struggle for existence became, as had been said, simply competition—the survival of the ittest appeared as successful self-seeking, the extinc- tion of the unfit meant just failure, poverty, misery and lead], and, if heredity and environment were still the only controlling forces, all things, as they are, were simply as they ought to be and we had no ground for , complaint and interference. That was the logical con- clusion of the first view of the Darwinian theory taken and applied to the realm of human affairs. Purpose, will, and effort became of no avail, and it was only by sheer self-contradiction that men who held that view strictly ever attempted any sucti thing as reform. Robert 131atcbford was, a case in point. The activity of his whole life was the negation of his theory. Nhat, then, should we say? We did interfere with heredity and environment and we thought we could alter toe course of things, and, even if we did not interfeee, we often complained. We could otily Bey that there must be more in the business than mere force of circumstances. There was surely also con- cerned in it, all that other force which we called personality, which we each one felt when we said "I am I," which carried with it the power of thought and will, the ability to form ideals, to have visions, and to gu'de conduct by those ideals]and v sions. They might say, if they liked, that heredity and environment bad produced that power of personality ; hut did that &amp;illy matter? Was a rose any the less a rose-because it grew on a muck heap ? The thing that muttered was the fact and the force of personality, not its origin. The original ancestor of man might have been an ape, probably was so. Did that mean that men ought to live in trees and eat nuts? Did it, in other words, take away from man's power at this moment ? Surely not. Nor was this to deny the general truth of the theory of evolution. The unity of the world, the ultimate origin of life from one source, the linking up of kind with kind and individual with individual in a totality, the power of circumstance in all that process, might be all true enough. We depended here on the verdict of science. But It was not necessarily the whole truth. Darwinism was not a dogma, but a theory, an hypothesis, which could be enlarged to fit the new facts. And, indeed, in most, modern readings of the tbeory of evolution there had been a departure from the strict Darwinian view. Modern scientific men, in talking of evolution, were generally ready to admit the presence everywhere of other conditioning forces besides environment and heredity. Value and purpose, personality, will and ideals, had all crept into the hypothesis of evolution and seemed likely to remain there, and we could well believe in the force of all those things and still follow Darwin in his great discoveries. There was nothing in the theory of evolu- tion capable of destroying our freedom or taking away from the value of personality. And, that being so, it was clear that real. personal religion had nothing to fear from Darwinism or theories of evolution, Ile theory of evolution seriously effected, indeed, many of the supposedly necessary beliefs of religion, making incredible, for example, the whole system of orthodoxy, but that was another matter. If we regarded religion, as we should, as really a matter of the inward life, a thing of faith and vision and aspiration, an effort to realize) the deepest meaning of life, born and nourished in the individual heart, if we regarded religion in that way, then there was nothing in the theory of evolution, nor in scientific theory ;generally, to take away one jot or tittle from tbe respcnsibility, the value, the sanctity of such religion. A sane, personal religion could never con- flict with science, and it was always a crude and mistaken science that repudiated personal religion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>MINIATURE RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>MINIATURE RIFLE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>APPLETON_ RIFLE CLUB v HALTON RIFLE CLUB</p>
        <p>This match was shot at the Drill Dail on Frieay January 17th. Conditions :—Eight aside, best six to count ; ten shots at a decimal target ; distance, 25 yards. Ilighest possible score, 100. Scores :</p>
        <p>A PPL ETON. HALTON. E Pollard 9B S Rowlinson 97 C P Day (captain) H Whitfield 97 P Bowlinson A Shepherd. F C Bennion J Towers 95 P Barrow</p>
        <p>COUNTED OUT. COUNTED OUT. O ShPpley 95 W Fotster F J Grocott Appleton won by eleven points.</p>
        <p>POET OF THE FARM. DEATH OF MR. WILL CARLETON.</p>
        <p>The death was announced on Saturday by the " Publisher's Circular " of Mr Will Carleton, whose poems " 0 ver Hill to the Poorhouse" and "Betsy and I are out" have been specially popular, and whose collections of poems have been read by more than one generation. These include "City keestivals," " City Ballads and City Legends." " Farm Ballads," "Farm Festivals," "Farm Legends," and other collections. He worked upon a farm when be was a boy, and some of his poems were composed while he was employed in the fields,</p>
        <p>Of his 'Farm Ballads "forty thousand copies were sold in less than eighteen months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>CALL ItIGHTS IZESERVED.I</p>
        <p>"It was quite natural," he said, with calm deliberateness, "that she—my wife—should want to be a .social smcess ; she had such ex- trac.rdinary beauty, suoh distinction, and she pos*essed- brilliant social qualities. I see it all now. I learnt a good deal alone under the stan in the wilds of Africa. Her people were poor. They had lavished their all in giving their daughter every advantage of education vid of travel."</p>
        <p>Isabel noted that Forbes never so much as tnentioned even the Christian name of his wife, and. she • resented it a •little in her secret heart. After all, had not she and Charlie been the closest, of friends for years? "She might have married much better," he went on, with characteristic modesty. "MY income then was comparatively small, and there were two lives between me and a title. I don't think I could properly have made her understand that. For this, and much more, I blame myself now, though ,it was she I blamed at the time. It is astonishing how, when one is in the grip of solitude—real soli- tude-,-One comes to see things in their right proportion." Lady Middleton smiled enigmatically. " I thought her selfish, and all the time it was I. I wanted to condemn her, to wrap up her rare talents in a nankin and bury them, when surely they were given her to use. She Could have g7..T.eed a throne." Lady Middleton recommenced tapping the floor impatiently with her Shoe, while she bit her lips to keep back indignant worlls. She observed, now that she examined, her friend more critically, how very grey his hair had become.</p>
        <p>"And you have never eorrwonded with your wife. or heard of her for all these years? " she asked. "No," he made answer. _ _ . _</p>
        <p>"Then, Charlie, you had best write to her before going to ,Ireland," she told him. "Your wife may he no Ipmeer there. It is no use going on a wild-goose chase, you -know." "Oh ! she will be there with her mother. Did I tell you that I bought a house for her that she had set. her heart on having? Of course she will .be there, and I have my reasons for not wishing to write before going." Lady Middleton had her doubts. The opinion she had .formed of this unknown wife did not lead her to imagine that such a woman would quietly settle down with her mother in Ireland. Her husband had not communicated with her for so long that it did not seem impossible' that she had con- sidered herself free to re-marry. But, even supposing that 'Forbes found her, and that she was willing to live with him again, now that he was wealthy and willing to let her fulfil her craving to be a society personage, Lady Middleton diq not believe that happi- ness (for Charlie) lay that way. Tliere would be a new and cruel disappointment. He, with his Bohemian tastes, would soon find the social chains biting into . his very soul ; nor was his wife likely to give' him anything that would even partially make up for the saerfl.?e he was making. Ali ! Charlie would be much better off big-game hunting ! Yet it was im- possible for her to advise him. The case was too delicate.</p>
        <p>Her .next remark, however, was not without a suspicion of malice: " You spoke of your wife's mother, Charlic,,. is site to come into this wonderful scheme of yours? "</p>
        <p>"And why not?" he asked, innocently. "I have no parents myself; and really, when you come to look at it, it would be rather rough on the old lady to shunt her off the lino after sharing her daughter's home all these years Lady Middleton nodded slowly, as if she hesitated. Then she asked: "Is the mother- ia-law nice?" "I have never seen her," Charlie acknow- ledged, rather unexpectedly. "My Wife was travelling with friends when I met her; and we married rather suddenly—in Paris. It was a quiet, informal affair. But when we agreed to separate my wife said she should have her mother to live with her, which ap- peared to me to be a very right and proper arrangement. I of course increased the al- lowance, knowing that her mother was not well off."</p>
        <p>" You dear, darling old innocent ! " ex- claimed Lady Middleton. "I should like to kiss you, except that I never kiss other women's husbands' Why! How do you come to be such a big brown man, dwarfing my little drawing-room, and yet--and yet be such an infant? "</p>
        <p>Forbes flushed under his tan. He had. been accustomed to Lady Middleton's banter in the old days, but just now it did not altogether please him. It seemed out of place. Her quick instinct detected his thought. " Don't be angry, Charlie," she said, with a tender note in her voice. "I want of all things to see you happy; and somehow "—she spoke a little wistfully—" it does seem a pity that your wife cannot see that happiness can- not be found in a wearisome martyrdom to social success. . She has a chance of an ideal life, free and untrammelled, where she could wear a holland frock and camp out under the stirs, where she could see Many lands, where she could renew her youth every day; and yet —oh, Charlie, why do so many of. Us spend our substance for what is not bread?" It was _Charlie's turn now. " Surely this is rank heresy from the lips of a society favourite, Isabel! You would not really like a gipsy life, or a holland frock?" His eyes wandered over the perfection of her white gown. The .kindly irony in his tones reached her consciousness and wounded her. She answered with some warmth.</p>
        <p>"I would gladly lead such a life," she told him. "I am tired of false glitter, false smiles and false complexions, and all that goes to make up the weariness called—miscalled pleasure! A London season is the hardest, dreariest, and most unsatisfactory thing in the world. And what is the gain? Weariness and vexation of spirit." " Isabel !" he exclaimed, "if you believe all this, why do you not cut your Ponds, and live a life that is life? "</p>
        <p>" Companionless?" she said, bitterly. She rose. "Charlie, you must go," he re- minded him. "I have to dress. Come back at nine o'clock. Muriel will be here by then." He extended his big brown hand, and when hers lay on it for a moment he found it cold. "What a brute I am!" he exclaimed. " I have tired you out." " You never tire me, dear," she said; but she looked tire&amp; all the same. As Charlie Forbes went back to his hotel a thought forced itself upon him, to be in- stantly dismissed. It was his first disloyalty to the woman who had bewitched him and whom he had married.</p>
        <p>In the veiled confidence he had given to Lady Middleton he had spoken no word that should lay blame at his. wife's door. But he knew that she had made him •suffer—suffer! And now he was about to offer himself up—a sacrifice—to her ambition and her vanity. But her beauty and her. charm were like a magnet. She could be irresistible. The thought he had dismissed so deter- minedly from his mind was—" Why did 1 not marry Isabel? What an ideal life we might have had ! "</p>
        <p>The Princess Polenouski's reception was to take place at the Grafton Gallery. Pa.de- rewski was to play. There was a rumour that a thousand pounds had been paid for the pink roses which were to decorate the rooms. Muriel Ranleigh had heard all about this. She had heard, too, that the Princess was to wear the wonderful Polenouski diamonds— such diamonds as no other uoman in London had ever been seen to wear. Muriel was full of girlish excitement when her maid set her down at Prince's Mansions.</p>
        <p>Forbes was already there, and he gave an involuntary sigh as he looked upon Muriel's fresh young face, and thought that she too was a worshipper at society's shrine. He had seen her last in a pinafore, and her hair, he remembered, had been very untidy. Now she wore a white chiffon robe, and her hair was " done " fashionably. But the only orna- ment she wore was a rope of pearls. "Doesn't she look lovely?" demanded Lady Middleton, after a sort of introduction had taken place. " I liked her best in the. pinafore, I think," said Forbes, smiling. " You don't remem- ber, Miss Muriel," he added, " but you were wearing a pinafore when last I saw you!" Muriel Ranleigh looked annoyed. She bad naturally expected a compliment, and in- stead she received an allusion to days not so far distant .a.s to bave grown indistinct—days when the pinafore in question was not always clean. She shot a waspish little glance at Forbes as she reminded him that his taste seemed to have suffered someNthat in the African wilds..</p>
        <p>The .xaro,nicr, Saturday, January 25, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>DECISION.</head>
        <p>THE DECISION.</p>
        <p>By MRS. COULSON KERNAFIAN Author of "A Beautiful Savage," "The Whis- perer," " Tho Sinnings of Seraphine," "An Artist's Model," "No Vindication," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>It was characteristic of Charlie Forbes. that the first person he called upon after an absence of several years in South Africa, was a woman. It. was inconsistent that he, being fond of the society of women, should have voluntarily deprived himself of it for so long.</p>
        <p>Irorbes had had many good women chums— Lady Middleton being chief among them. There had also been a woman who had been bound to him by a tie much closer ' • but who had not been a chum, and who had gone out of his lire.</p>
        <p>When Charlie Forbes was r.nnounced, Lady Middleton rose from her couch in her pretty drawing-room—dim by reason of sun-blinds-- with a little jeyous exclamation. " Charlie! " she cried. " Is it rossible ! Oh! 1.-at lam glad. to see you! I, thought you were in Africa."</p>
        <p>"I am here—very mnch here!" replied Charlie, grasping the two hands she held out to him. " I was sure of a welcome from you, Isabel," he went on ; " you are one of the few people who never give one unpleasant surprises."</p>
        <p>She drew her brows together quizzically, as ! she looked up at him—not quite understanding. He was a big man, and his bronzed face told of exposure to the. elements just as his firm mouth, large chin, and watchful, keen grey eyes indicated power. There w'as some- thing .els,a -in his face which Lady Middle- ton's quick instinct detected, which had not been there when she last saw him, and that she was .sorry to see. Tactfully she .said nothing: about ,this, but freeing her hands• from his grip, touched the electric bell. "We will have tea, Charlie," she said, gaily; " and I won't worry you with questions till you are, refreshed. How dreadfully hot it is—and only. the beginning of June ! Dut I suppose you think nothing of the heat here? " Forbes looked at her, in her flowing white muslin tea-gown, and thought how deliciously cool she looked. She gave him the kind of sensation he remembered to have experi- enced when coming out of tropical sunlight into a cool, marble courtyard, where foun- tains played among palms and tree ferns. A footman came in with the heavy silver tray, on which were the fragile blue • and white cups which Forbes so well remembered. " This feels like civilisation," he said, as . he placed 'Lady Middleton's favourite chair near the tea-table. He noted that she remembered after all these years exactly •Jiow many lumps of sugar he liked, and dropped them into his tea- cup; and for some reason this small indica- tion that she had not forgotten gave him ex- quisite. pleasure. " Do you know, Charlie," she remarked, as she heaped strawberries on a plate for him, ' " I don't think you deserve that I should be so very glad to see you! Never a letter, and never a word all these years! " No, I don't deserve it," he acknowledged, humbly; " and when the taxi stopped oppo- site these mansions, my heart nearly came to a standstill too; for I suddenly bethought myself that. you might be no longer in the familiar flat—that you might have re-married and gone away." A pink flush mounted to her cheek as she answered, with a forced little laugh, "Re- marriage has never entered into my calcula- tions! . And, Charlie, have you married'? " He looked at her straightly, meeting . her clear, honest eyes with eyes as true; and he answered, as was his way, with direct truth : "Yes," he told her, "I married years ago— and we separated—very soon. It was pro- bably my fault. We did not quite hit it off.' I am, as you know, a born bohemian; well, she cared only for the social side of life. She was very -ambitious, and she had, as I now realise, a right to be so; gifted as she was, with superb beauty, and brilliant social quali- ties."</p>
        <p>'And you were very much in love? Lady Middleton asked, with a slight hesitation in her _speech.</p>
        <p>" Yes," lie answered, monosyllabically. " And she—did she love you? "At first, I think," he said. "But, poor girl, it was a temptation for her to—oh, I won't speak of it ! ' "And now you have come back to England, what are you going to do?" she next asked. "I am going to Ireland to see her, and try to bring abopt a reconciliation," he answered, " I am so glad!" said Lady Middleton, with a little catch in her voice. (She refilled Forbes's cup). "Yes, I am so glad," she re- peated._ __</p>
        <p>"I shall tell her that I am now going to try to give her the life she cares for. I have do you know it, Isabel?—come into a big for- tune. It was that that decided me to come back. I settled most of what I then had on her, when we decided to separate. Wander- ing in Africa is not expensive; any way, not as I did it. By the way, I have brought you some skins, Isabel."</p>
        <p>She did not thank him, but just looked into his lined face with eyes full of compassion. To think that Charlie Forbes, with his big, affectionate heart, should count for so little in this unknown woman's eyes, that extra money might buy her back! " Who is. she, Charlie?" she asked. "I will tell you, dear, if my appeal is suc- cessful," he made answer. "If not, then I shall keep my secret." She knew him too well to press the point, and, noting that the discussion was a strain, she began to speak of mutual friends, and give him news. All at once a look of consternation came into her face. "Oh!" she cried, "I am so sorry, but I have just remembered an en- gagement for to-night that I can't nut off. promi3ed to chaperon Muriel Ranleigh to the Princess Polenouski's reception, and I would give anything to have you dine here quietly with me! But Muriel—do you remember Muriel?—she was in the schoolroom when you went away—she would be so dreadfully dis- appointed! Everyone is fighting to get an invitation from the Princess. She gives quite the most sought - after dinners and recep- tions and dances in London. They are fabu- lously rich. He is a Pole, but she is a beauti- ful English woman. But, Charlie, won't you come too? I can easily arrange it, as my brother, Lord Exmouth, was to have gone with us, and has written me that he is not well, and can't go. But have you any clothes?" She laughed softly as she glanced at his rough unconventional suit. MY dear Isabel, I visited a tailor to-day, and got measured for various suits," he told her, and I luckily found a dress suit—a mis- fit for some other fellow. That suit just fitted me. I had best get my hand in, I suppose; and to go with you and little Muriel will be a beginning. My life will be passed in this sort of thing henceforth—if she consents to come back to me." He smiled a little grimly. "Per- haps I shall be allowed a holiday now aud then, if I am a very good boy! Lady Middleton Jeft the tea-table, and elisconsed herself among the cushions in the corner of the big Chesterfield, drawing her skirts towards her, and patting the seat in in- vitation. He came and sat beside her. It was aood to be with her thus intimately again— this best of friend, who never changed! The raon,ss all perf surnseem from partromhe boitg silver personality. Surely those were the silver charmingthis boivlso e red woman's se bowls he had given her for a Wedding-gift, when she was only ".a slip of a girl:'?</p>
        <p>"Poor Charlie'! " she murmured with a sigh, "are you not just . a little afraid 01 kicking over the traces?" He went on talking as if he had not heard her remark. _</p>
        <p>"Not a soul except yourself knows of m 3 marriage," he said. "I went away big-game shooting and to. follow my taste for trav-el and adventure. .No One will ever know l have a wife, unless she consents to come bacii to me. She may—now I, am rich." :Lady Middleton tapped the floor with hei little satin slipper impatiently. " What a selfish, heartless woman this wife must be ! " she thought. She wisely kept her thoughts to herself. But eves sometimes betray what the tongue retuses to utter. Something in Ldy Middle- ton's eyes caused her friend to defend his w 4..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>" See if the Princess does not convert yon to-night, Mr. Forbes," she said. " She is so beautiful, and she will be covered with jewels! I only wish I had her beauty; some Polish Prince might marry me and give me such jewels! " "Don't be a little goose, Muriel!" said Lady Middleton. "The finest jewel a woman can wear is a good man's love! " • • "But of course I want that too ! " cried Muriel. "Prince Polenouski absolutely adores his wife. And site was a- mere noliody, I have heard—American, I think." But Forbes was not listening to Muriel Ranleigh's chatter. He was looking at the tender, womanly face of his friend Isabel, and was thinking what a jewel she would be for ft man to wear. She was not beautiful, accord- ing to critics; but her expression was abso- lutely lovely—so true and loving a soul looked out from her steadfast eyes.</p>
        <p>When Lady Middleton's party reached the top of the grand staircase (which was gar- landed with roses) at the Grafton Gallery, the Prince and Princess were there, receiving their guests. The Princess was wearing a robe of rich cream brocade. She absolutely scintillated with diamonds: A magnificent tiara • sur- mounted the coil of black hair. Her shining black eyes • were proud and haughty. Lady Middleton and Muriel Ranleigh were greeted, and passed on with the crowd. Then came Charlie Forbes_ _</p>
        <p>The Princess, smiling graciously, extended her hand. Their eyes met, and .the smile faded from her lips. A look of fear and of hatred came into her splendid eyes; but she con- trolled herself superbly, and went. on "re- ceiving." Charlie Forbes smiled a queer, crooked smile as he joined his friends. Lady Middleton darted a look at him. " Charlie, aren't you well? " she whispered, anxiously. " I am quite well," he answered; but he was pale under the tan, and great drops of perspiration stood out on his brow. He went on talking. "I am longing to hear I'aderewski play, aren't you, Miss Ran- leigh? " he said, trying bard to make his tones natural. "I would much rather keep looking at the Princess," answered Muriel. "Wasn't she lovely? and oh what diamonds ! " Charlie Forbes did not lister. to Paderew- ski, though lie played Chopin's " Berceuse " exquisitely. Forbes, like Muriel, was think- ing of the Princess: and, to judge from the expression of his ft-ce, his thoughts were not pleasant. On the way back to Prince's Mansions, after Muriel Ranleigh had been dropped at her home, Lady Middleton said: " You have not enjoyed your evening, Charlie. I suppose you were thinking all the time about your wife?" " I was," 11) said, with marked emphasis. . _</p>
        <p>"When are you going to Ireland?" she asked. "I am not going at all," he made answer. "I am goint; back to Africa." Then she knew. They parted at the flat without any expla- nations, Charlie saying that he would call to say good-bye to-morrow. When alone in his room at the " hotel, Charlie sat down to think. His thoughts did not dwell upon his wife, transformed into a Princes—his wife, who had attained her ambition, and whose eyes had looked fear and hatred into his to-night. He was think- ing that there was not another woman in the world who would have forborne to question him as Isabel had done. His conduct must have seemed to her extraordinary in the ex- treme; almost an insult to their long friend- ship. Now he came to think of it, Isabel had always been like that. She had given a ready sympathy; but she had never demanded confidence. The hand-clasp she had given him when they parted on this night told him that whatever the trouble might be she shared it. Oh that he were free! Scales had fallen from his eyes. Next morhing London society was inex- pressably shocked to learn that the Princess Polenouski, who had entertained so royally the night before, had died on reaching home from heart failure. The papers were full of accounts of the reception and of the tragedy. There were portraits of the Prince and Prin- cess in every daily. Much sympathy was ex- pressed for the Prince, who was utterly crushed by the loss of his beautiful wife. "if they only knew! " muttered Forbes, as he flung down his paper. He was strangely excited. Directly after breakfast he got into a taxi and presented himself at Lady Middle- ton's flat. She was finishing her cup of coffee. "So you have come to say good-bye, Charlie? " she said, tremulously. She, too, had read of the death of the Princess. "If you say it must be good-bye," he answered.</p>
        <p>His eyes pleaded. "Stay," she said. ETuE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WHAT DYSPEPTICS SHOULD EAT.</head>
        <p>WHAT DYSPEPTICS SHOULD EAT.</p>
        <p>A PHYSICIAN'S ADVICE.</p>
        <p>"Indigestion and practically all forms of stomach trouble are, nine times of ten, due to acidity; there- fore stomach sufferers should, whenever possible, avoid eating food that is acid in its nature, or which by chemical action iu the stomach develops acidity. Unfortunately, such a iule eliminates most foods which are pleasant to the taste as well as those which are rich in blood, flt-sh and nerve building properties. This is the reason why dyspeptics and stomach sufferers are usually so thin, emaciated and lacking in that vital energy which can only come from a well fed body. For the benefit of those sufferers who have been obliged to exclude from their diet all starcby, swept or fatty food, and are trying to keep up a miserable existence . on gluten products, I would suggest that you should try a meal of any food or foods which you may like in moderate amount, taking immediately afterwards half a teaspoonful of bisurated magnesia in a little hot or cad water. This will neutralise any acid which may be present, or which may be formed, and instead of the usual feeling of uneasiness and fulness, you will find that your food agrees with you perfectly. Bisurated magnesia is doubtless the best food corrective and antacid known. It is not a medicine, and bas no direct action on the stomach; but by neutralising the acidity of the food contents, and thus removing the source of the acid irritation which inflames the delicate stomßch lining, it does more than could possibly be done by any drug or medicine whenever necessary, but I must admit that I cannot see the sense of dosing an inflamed and irritated stomach with drugs instead of getting tid of the acid, the catme of all the trouble. Get a little bisurated magnesia frotn your chemist, eat what you want at your next meal, take some of the bisurated magnesia as directed above, and see if I'm not right."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SHAKSPERE'S VOCABULARY.</head>
        <p>SHAKSPERE'S VOCABULARY.</p>
        <p>In his inaugural address as president of the Man- chester Shakespere Soniety at the Atherzeum on Tuesday night, Sir William H Bailey said that cs it approached completion -Murray's New English Dictionary enipbasised the fact that Sbakspere used more words (many of which he coined himself) than any of his grf at contemporaries. In future we should recognise him, not only as a great poet but also as a great word-inventor and discoverer. In addition to coining now words, he lifted from the common folks, from the soil, many Saxon words that had nearly gone out of use It bad been stated that the new dictionary showed that Shakspere's contemporaries, the poets and writers of Elizabethan age, added the enormous number of 25,000 words to English Literature. Sir Thomas Browne was credited with adding 2,850 words, Bacon with 1,850, Ben Johnson with 1,350, and Shakspere with 9,450. a</p>
        <p>THE BOOTLESS BARONET BRAVELY ATTACKS THE BUD BAD, BUDGET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>By J. W. Graham Peace.</head>
        <p>By J. W. Graham Peace.</p>
        <p>Sir Herevvald Wake, Bart., lord f the Manor of Waltham Holy Cross, owner of Courteenhall, Northampton, and 3,200 broad acres made—at the annual rent audit dinner at Waltham Abbey a day or two ago—a speech which curiously teveals a certain type of Tory mind. "I look forward to coming and seeing you all," he said. "I would come a great deal oftener if I could afford to run a motor-car, bat I can't. . . Indeed, it is very likely I shall have to walk soon, according to the way in which Ministers of the Crown, the guardians of property and iights, are attacking property; and the measures they are taking make toe rather anxious to know whether I shall be able to afford a pair of boots before long. I sympathise with Mr Lloyd George and all gutter politicians. They do not like anybody's skin to be whiter and blood bluer than their own, and they want to g'-t rid of anybody who answers that description. What is left of the feudal system is the old affectionate regard felt by land- lords and tenants for each other, because they' belong to one little class or clique.. The 1906 Budget has done an infinite amount of harm all over the/world. It has created all sorts of dis- putes and given rise to a sort of feeling in other countries and in our Colonies that dishonesty is the best policy. It means absolute ruin. They say the land ought to be equally distributed, and talk about bursting up the feudal system. . . lam not rich enough to stand for Parliament and give Mr Asquith and company a bit of my mind. Every night when I go to bed and when I wake up in the morning I am in a terrible state of anxiety for the future of this country, because of the way in which it is being governed."— " Daily Chronicle," December 7th, 1912. What an amusing old gentleman Sir Herewald Wake must be. how his tenants must regret they are not favoured with his presence amongst them more frequently. Why will they not take the hint so delicately conveyed and present him with a motor car? It would not be too much to do in return for all that he and his ancestors have done for them. Can it be they are ignorant of the fact that but for the kindness of the original Wake in providing the land there would be no Manor of Waltham Holy Cross? I tremble to think of the fate of the tenants of the Manor if Sir Herewald should decide to remove his land to Courteenhall, Northampton, so as to obviate the painful necessity of his walking the distance boot- less, aye and perhaps even without socks (if baronets wear such things), to receive the, no doubt, very inadequate rents they pay him for permission ti use the land so generously provided. Sure'v someone will arouse these tenants to a sense of their "danger ere it be too late.</p>
        <p>I banded the paper from which this report is taken to a junior member of my family and bade him read. He had not finished the first paragraph when I was startled by aloud peal of laughter and upon demand- ing an explanation of such unseemly conduct I found it was occasioned by the picture of a bootless baronet presented to his mental vision. Having reproved him for his levity I turned up the fi!e cf the 'Times and read as follows :</p>
        <p>Lord Robert Cecil, speaking at the Mansion House yesterday on the poverty of many people in the West Ham and East Ram districts, men- tioned two cases of extreme hardship which had been brought to his notice Recently a man got up at 3 o'clock in the morning, and, after eating a piece of bread, %valked from the Weat Ham die-- trict to Willesden for a day's work. When he got there he found it was a night shift, so he w.i.ted. Be worked throughout the night, and got 55. for it. He walked back home, having had nothing to eat since he ate the bread on the previous morning, and gave the 53. to his wife, who had to pay 43. 6d. out of it for rent, leaving only Gd. for food. Another man walked from the same district to North Hampstead on the promise of a job, and then had to walk all the way back. (It is over fifteen miles Loin West Ham to Willesden.) I turned again to the report and read of the "terrible anxiety for the future of the country (?the 3,200 broad acres) which assailed the worthy baronet and wondered if it was anything like tha. which affected the men of whom Lord R Cecil spoke I wondered, too, if there might be any connection between the tribute of rent —variously estimated at between £150,000,000 and £2oo,ooo,ooo—which the workers of this country are compelled annually to render, and for which they get no return whatever, and the fact that so many of these same workers have to pass their lives upon the verge of the poverty-line over which they are all too easily driven by the first adverse wind.</p>
        <p>Were this huge tribute paid into the National Exchequer and used to defray elle cost of national and local services, how great would be the relief to industry thus made possible. The present crushing burden of rating and taxation could be entirely removed, and every inducement ta improve and develop would then be given. True, Sir Herewald Wake and other landowners would find their occupation gone, and it would no doubt be a matter of painful surprise to many such to learn that, as mere rent receivers, society had no use for them, but there is consolation in the thought that, in the widened opportunit;es for labour then offering, Sir Herewald would be given the chance of honourably earning his boots instead of, as at present, having to depend upon his tenants to provide him with these and all other necessaries.</p>
        <p>That this would be good for his peace of mind cannot be doubted, for his time and attention being fully occupied in useful labour, be would be much less likely to harbour those groundless fears which now give occasion for the "terrible anxiety,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>PARRS BANK MEETING.</head>
        <p>PARRS BANK MEETING.</p>
        <p>THE TRADE BOOM.</p>
        <p>Presiding at the meeting of Parr's Bank on Thursday,, Mr Cecil Is. Parr said that, in spite of the strikes of the. year, the trade and commerce of the country had swept. forward in an ever-increasing volume, and he was pleased to say that Parr's Bank had tad its full share in that increase of business. Moreover, there were in many quarters signs ot even greater activity in. , connection with most of the trades in the. year now beginning. Coal, iron and cotton showed considerable signs of improvement, but there was one dark spot, to he considered and that was the present position of agriculture, in connection with which competition grew keener from abroad, and the prospects of which were not so good. There was a belief in some quarters that joint-stock banks were averse to advancing money to agriculturists, out Parr's Bank was just as ready to lend money to farmers as it was to other sections of the nom:amity. Rates during the year had been favourable to bankers, atot there was a growing concensus of opinion at the present time openly expressed in favour of increasing gold reserves by both joint-stock banns and national institutions. Whilst he cordially agreed with this policy, he felt it should be carried out with the greatest caution and deliberation, otherwise a general and simultaneous demand for gold might Culminate in such extreme stringency as to brifig *about the crisis which the policy was designed to avoid. Another dark spot was the further depreciation of their investments. Consols, written down to 76 at the beginning of the year, dropped at one time to 72i, stud the present price , was 731. In the near future the conditions pointed to the fact that there would be a very real demand for money. He mentioned in this connf.‘..btion 10:6 PX.l7l's_ Bank was one of the five English banks interested in the proposed Chinese loan. Dealing with the balance sheet, he said there had been satisfactory increase in the number of accounts opened at their various branches The bank, in the opinion of the directors, was never in a sounder position than it was to-day.</p>
        <p>DON'T Loos OLD—Keep the ravages of time at bay by darkening the grey streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPHUR HAIR RESTORER,which deepensto a natural colour ma few days.Lockyer's maintains a youthful ampearance.Large bets. verywhere. Post free I/6. Bedfori Laboratory, Londc,r. S. E.</p>
      </div>
