      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</p>
        <p>Warrington's cup team is practically selected, and iif my readers take the thirteen players who are doing duty at Wigan to-day as the first choice, they will not be far wrong. There are DO last minute " captures' to announce, and none likely. Players eligible for the 'Cup Competition have to be registered by February Ist (next Saturday), and Warrington's list will be the existing one. The loss of gate money in the Wakefield and St. Helens matches " put the lid on" the Wire pullers' speculations for players. Yot cannot go to market with an empty pocket!</p>
        <p>On the whole, Warrington should be just a bit 2stronger than they were during last season's tourna- ment, in which they played a by no means inglorious part. In fact their feat of drawing each of the three tirst rounds and playing each opponent twice estab lished a record in the Northern Union. The defence was stronger than the attack, and it is so to-day. Probably the forwards are better than for some years past, and as forceful scrummage work has a lot to do with cup-tie success, Warrington only need to come out of the hat first to be pretty sure of maintaining a well-won reputation for cup-tie prowess.</p>
        <p>The loss of that .9.200 against Wakefield and St.' nelens haa &amp;finitely settled the question o Dickenson's benefit It cannot be given this season. With the club owing money to the Bank, it is impossible to give any player a benefit. One must be just before being generous. • Had these two matches not been ruined by the weather, Dickenson would have got his benefit, The Committee has decided to recommend ta the General Meeting that Dickenson have a benefit not later than October of next season, when the club will be in funds from subscriptions. Dickenson appeared before the committee at a recent meeting, and expressed himself as perfectly satisfied when the position was explained. Meanwhile he is playing particularly well. Good luck to him.</p>
        <p>Warrington have recently been enjoying a com- paratively exalted position in the League table, as a resu:t of good form shown in bad weather. The percentage has topped the " 50 " mark for the first time this season, and, now we are faced with the prospect of another return to the wilderness. Out of the next four, Warrington have only one match at home. Wigan away to-day, Leeds away next week, then Widnes home. and Wakefield and Salford away.</p>
        <p>The team for to-day includes Dickenson at centre, -end Ranwick on the wing—an experiment which should strengthen the middle line: Jolley, full back; Renwick, Tranter, Dickenson and Williams, three- 'quarters; Nicholas and Daintith, half backs; G Thomas, Cheaters, Skelhorne, R Thomas, Fearbley -and Cox, forwards.</p>
        <p>Wigan smothered Widnes on Wednesday by over 40 points, but I think they will find the Wirepullers tougher opponents. The match between the two clubs -at Warrington early in the season ended in a lucky win for Wigan, who had very little superiority to boast of. Warrington ought to get within ten points of the Lancashire champions.</p>
        <p>The League table up-to-date is as follows : For. Agst. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c'nege. flud'fleld -.20 19 1 0... 63112 462...21 24 114... 95130 Wigan .20 17 3 0... 67 96 422...27 33 153... 8500 Broughton 18 12 3 3... 29 40 178...12 18 78... 7500 R.H 22 15 6 1... 61 69 329...37 44 206... 7045 Huns let 91 14 6 1... 50 69 307...34 29 155... 69.04 'Salford l9 13 6 0... 25 37 161-14 27 109... 6842 Dewsbury 21 13 7 1... 44 80 398...31 36 170... 6428 Wakefield T 22 13 7 2._ 36 63 261...34 50 218... 6363 td.harn lB 10 7 1... 37 50 224...33 32 162.. 5833 ,lint°,l l9 10 8 1... 30 40 180...17 32 130... 5526 19 10 9 0... 34 48 212...25 31 143... 5263 tirrington 20 10 9 1... 23 29 137...24 32 144... 5250 ,Leeds' .21 10 10 I._, 40 40 200.. 34 47 209... 5000 :Bradford 2l 10 10 1... 28 30 146...25 48 191... 50 00 .....null 21 9 11 1... 25 36 158...33 61 249... 4523 Hvalifax 2O 810 2... 23 42 172...35 50 220... 45430 lA'adnes l9 810 1... 96 43 181...34 43 197... 44.73 •R'hdale HlB 7' 9 2 26 44 184...21 30 132... 4444 ,St Helens 21 3 12 33 47 907...36 47 213... 40.47 Leigh 19 7 12 0_ 13 97 107...27 33 168... 3684 York lB 5 13 0... 20 35 145-40 64 272... 27.77 Runcorn -20 5 15 0... 24 29 135...43 68 290... 25.00 Bramley I9 415 0... 21. 26 120...45 90 360... 21.05 Keighley -16 3 13 0... 16 20 92...38 53 235... 18 75 gßarrow 19 3 15 1... 15 29 117...40 60 260... 1842 Coventry l7 016 1... 17 23 103...68117 487... 2.94</p>
        <p>The " A " teams contest at Wilderspool should be well worth fieing. Warrington "A" gained a notable 'victory at Wigan, and the visitors will be keen on 'Wiping out their only home defeat. It is expected that Wigan's new Colonial, Curran, will appear at Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>The home team will be: Mclntyre, full back ; 'Davies, 'Judson, Jordan and Clare, three-quarters; Brown and Douglas, half backs; Naylor, Arnold, Harmer, Bevan, CI itchley and Gibbs, forwards.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES. The following is a list of the fixtures together with the results of tbe corresponding games last season : Bradford Hunslet ll Salford Reighley Huddersfield Batley 'York 8 Dewsbury .It°oldale Hornets 5 Wakefield Trinity Barrow 5 Runcorn 7 : 13r°ughton Rangers ...... 6 Hull 8 Wigan St Helens 2O Swinton 5 'Widnes — Coventry _</p>
