      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>lllllmm—_ .. ~ I . . . .. , , • . " 'N- " '''--, ' ' , ';'' . -t, .et.".61;!..!'"-,,% ~, – , 4.•*; , .* .4' !,', ......% ' f'' ',II, ~..;.-1,. ~. At; C . i'.....4 •., ~r " '` -- –</head>
        <p>lllllmm—_ .. ~ I . . . .. , , • . " 'N- " '''--, ' ' , ';'' . -t, .et.".61;!..!'"-,,% ~, - , 4.•*; , .* .4' !,', ......% ' f'' ',II, ~..;.-1,. ~. At; C . i'.....4 •., ~r " '` -- -</p>
        <p>WIGAN'S FINE FINISH AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In this match at Runcorn it was evident that Wigan had held their opponents too cheaply, and at half-time they could only claim a two points lead. Bradley opened the scoring for Wigan, wriggling over the line after being three times held, and before half-time Sbarrock added a penalty goal. Runcorn scored a fine try through Padbury, ha burst through and forced his way over, no goal resulting. Early on in the second half Runcorn equalised through a penalty goal by Massey. Play was very even right up to threequarter time, when the pace had evidently teen too hot for Runcorn, and the superior stamina ot Wigan enabled them to run in four more tries through Walford ;2), Todd, and Coldrick, four goals being kicked by Sharrock (2) and Thomas (2). Result :—Wigan, 5 goals, 5 tries 125 paints) ; Runcorn, 1 goal, 1 try (5 points). Teams and officials : _ _</p>
        <p>Runcorn—Shaw, back; Yond, A Hughes, Creevey and Deakin, three-quarter backs ; B Hughes and Toole, half hacks ; Padbury,' Peacock, Inman, Stevensou, Massey and Donoghue, forwards. Wisan—Sharrocs, back ; Bradley, Jenkins, Wilford and Todd, three-quarter baclis ; Gleave and Thomas, half backs; Seeling, Ramsdale, Frances, Coldrick, Silcock and William, iorwards. Referee, W H Wood, iludderstle:d. Touch judges, A Cross, St. Helens, and J Dutton, ‘Varrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>THE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A's " FOURTEEN POINTS VICTORY.— Owing to the heavy. state of the ground, play in the match between Warrington " A " and Leigh Shamrocks at Wilderspool un Saturday, was somewhat scrappy. The game was, however, keenly fought, but Warrington completely outpl.ayed the visitors and won by 14 points to nil. For a time the visitors held their own, but eventually Warrington took advantage of rather weak clearances by Goulding, and Brown, at half-bank, com- pletely bluffing the opposition, rushed over the line for the opening try. Maintaining the pressure, Warrington were almost always besieging the visitors' quarters and for a glaring infringement of the offside rule Ciitchley placed a penalty goal. Warrington soon snored another try through the medium of Clare, Mclntyre, distinguishing himself with a line run before serving Clare. The result was that the wing three- quarter put on the finishing touch in clever style. At half time Warrington led by one goal two tries (eight points) to Leigh Shamrocks nil. . The opening stages of the second half were character- ised by even play. The Warrington backs, however, !bowed their superiority, and Davies, taking up a pass- ing movement,dashed over and scored a fine try. For the. second time the Leigh touch-judge protested against a fair try by a 4Varrington player and the referee now suspended him from further acting. Near the close the visitors were always on the defence, Dickenion showing rare skill. Brown scored from a forward rush. Result : Warrington, one goal four tries (14 points) ; Leigh Shainrocka, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>TO-DAY'S TEAM.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S TEAM.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' REDS V CADISH EA D.-At Gorsey-lane Full back. F Horrocks ; three-quarter backs, J Griffiths W Leah, A Bramhall, G Warren ; half backs, G Floyd, J Gallop; forwards, A Stretford, J Brandwood, Morris, F Mason, A Booth, J Broadhurst.</p>
