      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>--- ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>--- ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM AMATEUR CUP. FIRST ROUND. MERSEY ATHLETIC V ALTRINCHAM LADS.</p>
        <p>At Sale on Saturday. The visitors gained a narrow victory with scores: Altrincham Lads, 3; Mersey Athletic, 2. BARNTON VILLA V BOWDON VALE.</p>
        <p>At l3arnton on Saturday. Final: Barnton Villa, 2 goals; Bowdon Vale, nil.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD RECREATION V BOLLINGTON.</p>
        <p>At Latchford on 6aturday. The Recreation were easily the better team in te first half, and held the commanding lead of four goals to one at the interval. In the second half the game was far more even, each side scoring twice. The scorers were Woodbead, Welsby (2), Rigby, Cartwright, and Harrison for the Race , and Vost, Hargreaves and Lawson for Bollington. Final: Latchford Recreation, 6; Bollington, 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LATCHFORD V. OUGHTRINGTON AMATEURS.</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD V. OUGHTRINGTON AMATEURS.</p>
        <p>At Latchford on Saturday. The Amateurs had Wood in goal in place of Kite. The game was a keenly con- tesieu one all through, the home team (Latchford), just gaining the verdict by the only goal of the game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>CADISHEAD WESLEYANS v LYAIM.</head>
        <p>CADISHEAD WESLEYANS v LYAIM.</p>
        <p>At Cadishead on Saturday. The game opened fast, neither side claiming much advantage for some time. The defence at each side was good. The result was in doubt right up to the close, and when time was called the senior members had gained a narrow victory with the score ; Lymm, 3; Cddishead Wesleyans, 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>OUGHTRINGTON PARK v. STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON PARK v. STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>At Heatiey. This was another meeting of First and SeJond Division clubs. Although Stockton Heath put up a plucky fight, they were no match for the senior orgAnization, and had to admit defeat by the score : Oughtrington Park, 4; Stockton Heath, 1. NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. SECOND DIVISION. _ _</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON C.L.B V. LA—T-C-H—Fa. RD RESERVE. At Grappenhall on Saturday, the Brigade escaping defeat tor the first time this season. The first halt was very even, eich side displaying a good defence, with the result that at the interval the score sheet wits blank. Within five minutes of the resumption the Reserves took the lead with a fine shot. The Brigadsrs infused more life into their play, and deserved 4 equalized. Once on level terms, the Brigade improved and had the best of matters for the remainder of the game, but could not get through, W Starkey (Latchford) being the chief stumbling block. Final: Warrington C.L.8., 1; Latchford, 1. FRIENDLY MATCH. WARRINGTON EXCELSIoRS_y BASIC QUAY REEREA-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>TION.</head>
        <p>TION.</p>
        <p>This match was played at—i3ewsey on Saturday and resulted in a win for Warrington Eacelsiors by 3 goals to 2. The first half of the game was of an even character and the score of one goal each at the interval, fully represented the play. The game was one of the best witnessed at Bewsey this season. The home team deserved their victory by the odd goal in three. When the whistle sounded for time the score was Excelsiors, 3 goals; Recg, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ANGLING ASSOCIATION. (N.A. CENTRE) The conditions for our local anglers are not yet favourable enough for them to turn out in numbers. One member visited the Ciowy with poor result, only one roach falling to his lot and that was taken in the first ten minutes. Not another stir did he get all day. Several members visited water nearer home with better results. One or two nice roach were creeled on Sunday last, and one member took a nice lot of roach on the Monday. We hear of members getting their baits ready for the time when the weather will allow them to visit the Gowy, the popular bait being stone, stick or leaf baits. There is not the least doubt that more fish are taken with these baits than any other about this time of the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>GOAT'S%</head>
        <p>THE GOAT'S%</p>
        <p>This is a description- of the Gowy given by an Angler from up North on his first visit. " Many Bolton anglers are puzzled as to why their brethren in Chester and Warrington have been so anxious to secure the privilege of fishing what is known only as the Brook by the natives, considering that they, or at any rate the Chester people, have the beautiful Dee 'tinning past their doors. Some descrip- tion of the river and the fishes it holds will therefore be of service. The fishery may be approached either from Dunham Hill or Mickle Trafford stations, on the , London an North-Western Warrington to Chester Railway. The river passes under Chester-road shout half-way between the stations; we shall be able to travel down stream to where a sign post is put up, then two meadows at the big Withens, and two meadows near the Shako/. Bridge. There are some deep pools in this section containing decent pike, flue roach, and very fair dace. Gudgeon of large size are also to he found. I Looking up stream as we stand on Chester-road Bridge, we shall see distinct streams which meet together close to the bridge, forming one river before passing under. The stream to the left is the Black Brook, and we may fish one side only to the London and North-Western Railway, the middle stream is the Barrow Brook and our priviledge extends to a signpost, Men one side only to the pontoon. The main stream is on the right, and we may fish it up to the mill pool, where shoals of dace may be seen, hut are difficult to catch because the dace can see the fishermen, us there is no covet. We may travel up the mill stream up to the church, and on to Little Barrow village, with the exception of four fields where signboards will be set up.' "Roach exceeding two pounds weight, which were taken from the Gowy, are on view in Warrington, also dace exceeding 13 ounces. Higher up then our fishery the river is preserved and stored with trout but they don't all stop there. The chief charms of the fishery, however, are the abundance of fish and the clearness of the water, and the frequent changes from rapid stream to deep pool. In some puts the waterway is almost chocked with weeds trailing and waving in the rapid stream, where dace rise to the well placed fly at every cast. In other parts where the channel is very deep and often wide, the bottom fisher has a better chance, but everywhere the clearness of the water requires the tactics of a Red Indian or, at least, those of 'their imitators, the Boy Scout. Stoat waterproof boots should be worn, for unless there has been a long spell of dry weather the ill-shod angler will fare badly in many places where the land is trequently flooded and holds water like a sponge. I have frequently passed on the railway, and never without keenly scrutinising the landscape and noting the places where flab may be found or get " witcher in the pursuit."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Examiner Saturday January 25, 1913</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday January 25, 1913</p>
        <p>THE BAY HORSE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>A general meeting was held here on Monday night last, to form a society to affiliate to the Northern Anglers. A fair muster put in appearance to form the club. The centre secretary read out the rules of the N.A.A. and the advantage of affiliating with the War- rington Centre. The first ,item was thF: election of officers :—President, Mr Herbert Hayes; treasurer, Mr T Roberts ; secretary, Mr J Potter ; committee, Messrs %V Bailey, R Burrows, J Sudlow, H Jolley, J Bryers. Joseph Graham, R Archer, H Richards, J Green and B Dobson.—After the Secretary elected took his place at the table, the amount of subscriptions per year was dealt with, ono finally arranged at ss, the date of matches and other items being left over until next general meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>NOTICES.</head>
        <p>NOTICES.</p>
        <p>The club will be open at headquarters to take subscriptior s and issue cards if they arrive in time. The secretary will be at Bay Horse to receive subscriptions on Saturday and Monday night between 8 and 9p.m. Subscriptions will also be taken at the Coopers Arms en Monday night trom 8 p.m. by Secre- tary R Bradshaw. members who take cards out from headquarters need not take them out of affiliated clubs. Only one set required. A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven kept up their improved form and beet Manchester University by 0-3, being at full strength, whereas Manchester had three of their best men away through injuries. lhe play in the first half was even, and the scoge at the interval was 4-3. Then Latchford went ahead and scoring five times won easily. Manchester could not get through again, as the Latch- ford defence was good all round; Fletcher at rih back playing a most skilful game. Bruhl in goal mane several clover saves, one from a terrific shot by Hutchin- son being absolutely crest. Hutchinson at inside right for Matr;liester was the best forwa:d on the field and MacGlill played hard. The Manchester goalkeeper defended soundly, and had little chance with the scoring shots. Nlinards, Travis and Pearson were a strotg forward trio, and got eight of the goals between them, Minards scoring four times, and Travis and Pearson twice each. The other goal came from a good individual effort by Isherwood near the eud. _ _</p>
        <p>The Second Eleven's match with Alderley Edge had to be cancelied by the latter club in consequence of players being off ill and injured. The First Eleven v South Manchester at Alexandra Park this afternoon is :—A Bruhl, goal; W Fletcher, C Billington, backs ; J B Wilkinson, A E Bradshaw, A Knight, halves ; It A Dale, .1 E Pearson, W H Minards, A Starkey, W Isherwood, forwards. Train from Central 1.50. The Second Elvens meet at Latchford and the home eleven is :—H Wright, goal ' • W T Eccles' H Fletcher, backs ; C 1-1 Morgan. T Gibbs, T Banks. halves ; Laver, F Hobin, E !links, A CI Miracles, H If Colton, forwards. Bully-oil '2 15 p.in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>WARhINGTON'S DUAL SUCCESS AT ST. HELENS</p>
        <p>On Saturday last, from Matto Heath, the Rev C M Chavasse started CO runners, representing 10 teams of the St Helens C.C.A. for a novice team race over a course of approximately five miles of fairly stiff country, in the vicinity of the toll bit-, the cew golf links, and Eccleston-lane ends with the finish at the corner of Thatto Heath and Crotsley-roads. Result:—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>INDIVIDUAL PLACINGS.</head>
        <p>INDIVIDUAL PLACINGS.</p>
        <p>- Time. Min. Sec. 1 W Stafford, Warrington A C 2 'l' Maleedy, Sacred Heart E 1 29 53 3 J Wutsun A C _</p>
        <p>1 .1 Carey, Sacred Heart H ...... .. 30 5 5 F Hoard, St Heleas Parish Church 30 20 6 A Wright, Ravenhead TEAM PLACINGS. _</p>
        <p>I Warrington A.C. :-N Stafford 1, J Watson 3, G Wakefield 9, J 'Moroiley 11, 24 points; also finished F Price 21 and H Payton 42. 2 Sacred Heart "A" :-T Maleedy 2, J Carey 4, T Hull 14, J Caldwell 19, 39 points. 3 St Helens Parish Church :-F Howard 5, E Hilton 7, J Heaton 15, H Phillips 17, 44 points. 4 Runcorn :-10, 12, 16, 27- 65; 5 Kavenhead "A " : 6, 13, 21, 25-65 ; 6 Prescot : 20, 23, 26, 42-111; 7 Sutton : 8, 22, 39, 45-114 ; 8 St Helens Rees: 18, 33, 35, 38-124; 9 Sacred Heart " B " : 36, 37, 43, 48-164; 10 Ravenhead "B" : 29, 40, 50, 52-171 point. The conditions were six to run, and every team finished the necessary four counters to entitle them to be placed. Great interest was taken in the race by St. Helens people, who had voted the incividnal honour to Maleedy. It was also antisipated that the Sacred Heart "A " team had most to fear from the Parish Church team, and the only disturbing factor to these profinostications was that the Warrington lads did nut miss their train.</p>
        <p>The six runners, in charge of J Battersby, were a quiet and unassuming little party which returned very proudly with all the honours that were to be Lad and St. Hele”s comment on the Warringtonians may be summed up in one word " great." Only five weeks ago Warrington A.C. won the dual honours of the West Lancashire C.C.A. novice race and a search of the records of similar events shows that the local club have established a record whir.h wiil take some beating as it means a fresh batch each time. Nelson Stufford and Jeffrey Watson were well placed right from start to finish and the local club are justifiably proud of them and their four club mates who helped them manfully. k corn the local headquarters two packs ran over the home trail. The Juniors were headed home by Hanson and Gregson after covering a little over 5 miles. Holbroo.k, Turton and 1. Antrobus were in close com- pany when leading the senior lot home after running about 9 miles and W Antrobus, J Parsonage and NN Bolton were close enough to suggest that they are finding their form.</p>
        <p>_ TO-DAY'S FIXTURE. Meet at Headquarters at 3 p.m—important.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Eccentric tuml.ling, boisterous comedy and a strong element of originality are predominant features in the" turn" provided by the Three Alvarettas who have delighted large audiences at the Hippodrome during the week. From the commennement to the finish, the audience is kept rolling with laughter, for besides being expert and daring acrobats, the trio are capable comedy merchants and are excruciatingly funny in a skit "I can't shut my mouth." One member is supposed to be lock-jawed and the other two jump on him and pull him about in a most discon- certing manner, at the same time performing sk,me hair-raising acrobatic feats. Blake and Granby are a smart couple of comedy artistes and cause much laughter in a short farce "The system." The husband cannot by any means be termed a "home bird" and in trying to evade his wife and repair to the haunts of his boon companions he invents, a system by which on the slightest remonstrance from his wife, he affects a temper and threatens suicide. The wife is willing to let him do anything if he will not take his life, and "the system "of the husband has worked. However, through his generosity to a friend his scheme is discovered by the trustful wife who eventually turns the tables on her husband. A strong element of humour prevails throughout, and this is acceptable to everyone because it is of the clean, smart and witty order. Joe Almasio and Miss Yamina appear in a juggling and tumbling act which is far above the ordinary. Almasio is a clever, eccentric, comedy juggler, whilst his partner is a tricky "tumbler." The Four Astors are highly successful in a ragtime dancing and singing arena. A daring performance is given by one of the male members who dances the most intricate steps on skates. The other artiste worthy of mention in the quartette is Baby VI. who well deserves the title of "The phenomenal child artiste." There are few child artistes possessed of the stamina which she displays in her dancing and the audience is unstinted in its approbation. La Freya "The charming Parisian artiste" appears in her original and artistic visions and is accorded a hearty reception from the " popular" parts of the house. The artistic, was, however, some-. what obscured in some of the visions. Arthur Ferris gives a number of "comedy character studies" in song form. Although a little more discretion might be exercised in his choice of song, he causes much merr;ment. Anna Dorothy, "The Ginger Girl," carries everything before her in her song "I don't care" and' from the way she gave-it one could not help thinking that she didn't care. Her best character was that of the policeman, her gags and witticisms being smart and up-to-dste. Webb and Braoston are capable operatic vocalists and in their new military song scena "On the 'Veldt" score a striking sucoesa. The. picture "The interrupted elopement" is highly entertaining.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RECITAL BY MR. ALFRED PERRIS:</head>
        <p>RECITAL BY MR. ALFRED PERRIS:</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the wintry weather, a large audi once assembled in the Cairo-street schools on ‘Vednes- day evening to hear a dramatic and poetic recital by Mr Alfred Perris. The programme, while chiefly cast in the more serious and reflective vein, had also some happy lighter touches of humour, and the thoughts suggested by the pieces, as well as the manner of their interpretation, were such as t r compel appreciative interest throughout. There were two selections from Shakespeare ; the rest of the items may be classed as modern, and the works of American poets and authors were freely drawn upon. The fact that Mr. Perris was suffering f.om a severe cold made his task the more difficult, but one could not but admire the indomitable way in which he rose superior to the drawback. Fortunately his vocal powers were not impaired and every word received due emphasis and! could be distinctly heard While merging himself to a great extent in his subjects, Mr Perris does not resort to exaggera ion. His purpose—as set forth in "Hamlet's advice to the players "—itith which he fittingly opened his recital—is "to hold the mirror up to nature" And to rely upon the exp essiveness of the sto•en word rather than upol an elaboration of gesture. Perhaps it was the desire to be strictly na ural and legitimate in his methods which caused Mr Perris to present what we regarded as a too uniformly sombre vereion of" The Diver " (Bulwyer Lytton's translatioe from the German of Schiller.) A little more warmth and variety of colour might well have been imparted, without exposing the reciter to the charge of exaggeration. \'e were particularly impressed with George Meredith's 'touching and suggestive little work "Martin's Puzzle." Mr Perris was undoubtedly at his best in this item, embodying the reflections of a large-hearted sceptic on the cruel misf, rtunes which befall an innocent and uncom- plaining girl. " Elow can an all-loving Providence allow such things to be ? " is the question which agitates old Martin's mind, and he argues the pros and cons with a homely, penetrating directness which is arresting in its power. In the end, however, he appears to bs led to a submissive frame of mind. Mr Perris realized to the full the pathetic forcefulness of this work and brought out every point so convinc- ingly that the audience was held as by a spell arid at the cicse rewarded his efforts with an exceptionally cordial round of applause. Jaques' immortal dis- course on "The Seven Ages of man "from " As you like it" was very well interpreted, Mr Perris's conc,ption of the lines being earnest and solemn, though not so excessively lugubri- ous as they are sometimes made to appear. "The Sisters" (Whittier) was given with awesome dramatic effect. As a study in contrasts of emotion Bret Harte's "Jimn "stood quite on a plane of its own, and Mr Perris was eminently successful in conjuring up the alternating mods of despair and delighted relief. "In the tunnel," by the same poet, was also deeply impressive and "Our little Jim" (G. I?. k.-ims) was a truly human trifle, expressing a depth of feeling in a few words. T. B. Aldrich's "Baby Bell," dominated by tender affection combined with a sense of the idyllic and mysterious, was one of the choicest items on the programme. The humorous side was represented by "Altruism " R Trowbridge's piquant story of the ostensibly disinterested lines on which two ship-wrecked travellers argue for possession of a solitary life- preserver —and " Aurelia's unfortunate young man "—Mark Twain's far-fetched narrative of the chain of c damities which conspired to mutilate the prospective bridegroom and repeatedly defer the nuptials. Of the recital as a whole, we can only say that it afforded a most enjoy- able and profitable evening's entertainment and showed that Mr. Perris is possessed of real talent as an elocutionist as well as poetic insight. The programme was acceptably varied by the inclusion of the songs " Life's Eiptome "(Kenneth Rae). "Along the Golden Sands " (J L Roeckel) and "Beloved, it is morn " (Florence Aylward) by Miss May Perris.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>WHAT THE SPIRITUALIST SAID.</head>
        <p>WHAT THE SPIRITUALIST SAID.</p>
        <p>Peculiar Slander Action at Warrington.</p>
        <p>A curious case in which a slanderous statetneut was alleged to have been prompted by a Spiritualist's " second sight" professions, was heard at the Warring- ton County Court on Thursday, by his Honour Judge Stanger, N.C. The action, which had been remitted from the High Court, was brought by Mary Moors, wife of James Moors, 21, Duke-street, Harlestown, to recover £5O damages for slander trom Elinbeth Stanway, wife of James Stanway, 11, Duke-street, Earlestown. Mr H J Longtou was tor the plaintiff and Mr A Browne for the defendant.</p>
        <p>The plaintiff alleged that defenlant came un to her door and said she would he obliged if plaintiff would give her back the ring which she had stolen trom her house. Asked for proof of her accusation, defendant said a Spiritualist had told her that the ring had been stolen by a P person who had cleaned her house and whose name was Mary. His.Honour : Had you cleaned her house ?</p>
        <p>Plaintiff : Yes sir, several times. Continuing she said that when defendant had made the statement about the culprit's name baing Mary, she added "fhat's you and you are a big thief." She called plaintiff a thief several times,although plaintiff said it was untrue. Defendant said it was quite true and she believed it. Plaintiff then commented: "We shall need no detectives if Spiritualists can tell people who have stolen things." Defendant repeated the allegation in the hearing of passers-by and when her mother came upon the scene and was about to speak to plaintiff, the defendant took her by the shoulders, turned her away and said" Don't talk to a thief." The slander had since • been circulated by many other people. Mr Longton : Is there any truth in the allegation about stealing the ring? _</p>
        <p>Plaidtiff : No, sir.- I have never seen the ring. Mr Longton said there was some ill-feeling between the parties previously—at least there was on the part of defendant, in consequence of the plaintiff's daughter having discontinued giving her sewing to do. Air Browne, in answer to the Judge, said there was no intention on the part of the defendant to plead justification. She did not impute anything agiinst the character or honesty of the plaintiff. Alr Longton : lam only sorry Mr Browne has not said that before.</p>
        <p>His Hononr : Well he his said it now publicly. There is, I understand, no imputation on the honesty of this lady ? Mr Browne: No, sir.</p>
        <p>Mr Longton lhat is the first time, we have heard it. Mr Browne contended that the action %as a frivolous one and an abuse of the Court. 'Air Longton wrote the defendant stating that legal proceedings would he taken if the defendant did not sign a written apology and pay his costs (55.) Both parties to the action had nothing whatever. They were the wives of respectable workingmen and it would be idle to proceed with the CSSe.</p>
        <p>His Honour, in suggesting that a settlement of the case was desirable, remarkeci that the fact that the ground on which the alleged slander rested was an absolutely silly one did not prevent its bqug an imputation.</p>
        <p>Mr Browne said his client went to make inquiries at the plaintiff's house, in consequence of that" silly Spiritualistic business." No-one was present but the two parties. . _ _</p>
        <p>In view of the public wlth6rawal of all imputations, Mr Longton agreed to withdraw the action on the defendint pAying a small amount tpwards the plaintiff's costs.</p>
        <p>His Honour said that if the case had been fought out to the bitter end it might have inflamed the unfriendly feelings. He was glad the matter had been settled in an amicable manner. If the defendant did say what the plaintiff alleged, the action had been very properly brought. She appeared to have rested the imputation on the most ridiculous grounds and one could not help feeling that it would have been rather a good thing to have joined the Spiritualist as a co-defendant in the action and made her liable for the mischief she had done by her silly talk. The great object of the plaintiff—the clearing of her character— had been achieved. He hoped nobody from that day forward would attach the slightest importance to the silly tittle-tattle about a Spiritualist having said that a person named Mary had stolen a ring. There was not a particle of foundation for the imputation of dishonesty and he could understand the plaintiff feeling it very keenly.</p>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CLUB.</p>
        <p>Tne monthly bogey competition was played on Satur- day. Very few cards were returned, aud the following qualified :—Mr J G Birtles, five Clown in the first division ; and Mr E A White, 12 down in the second</p>
        <p>Other r eturns were :—First division, Mr T B Handley six down ; Mr llairey, twelve down.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>ROYALTY AT CINEMA HALL.</head>
        <p>ROYALTY AT CINEMA HALL.</p>
        <p>A special cinematograph performance, attended b Prince and Princess Christian, Prince Albert, an Princesses Victoria and Marie Louise of Schleswig Holstein, end the houselvild of Frogmore House, w a given at the PeopleN Electric Theatre, Windsor, on Wednesday.</p>
      </div>
