      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>LANCASHIRE INQUIRY INTO ALLEGED</head>
        <p>A LANCASHIRE INQUIRY INTO ALLEGED</p>
        <p>DETERIORATION.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Education Committee met et Preston on Monday, Sir Henry F Hiboert presiding. In submitting the scholarships report, Sir Henry mentioned that 73 exhibitions had be,.:4a resigned, which was regrettable seeing the sqcrifices that were being made to give RII opportunity to young people to further their education.</p>
        <p>With regard to the qusstion of educational progress of children in Council schools, Alderman S Taylor (Luriesdale). referred to the two resolutions received and discussed at the last meeting relating to the curriculum in elementary schools. The committee, he continued, were seldom able to deal with resl educa- tional questions. No doubt the teaching of reading, writing, and arithmetic, lying at the bottom of this resolution, might be, a simple matter, but a very great deal underlay the proposal before them which did not appear on the surface. It really raised the question wnich had been agitated in all ages— whetuer education was to be strictly utilitarian, or other considerations were to enter. He did not think they could get out of their responsibility by formu- lating a model time-table and saying that so many hours per \sees should be devoted to arithmetic or any other subjsct. Since the committee last met a good many pronouncements had been made throughout tie country, \\elite several had bent made in Laucashiie, dealing with the question. It was a very important matter and one not to be dealt with lightly. He agreed that the country wanted an inquiry. It would be a long time before any material cifference was made in the curriculum, if any was made. Mr Kinch said it was common knowledge that the new inspection was not generally considered satisfactory. One inspector would recommend certain things,and the man who succeeded him would recommend other things. Mr Kinch suggested that the Committee should have its own inspectors. The teachers would haven periodical visit from inspectors who had perhaps previously been teachers. The following recommendation was adopted : " That this Committee, in common with most teachers and educationists, frankly acknowledges that the results in the three essentials of instruction in our elementary schools have materialiy deteriorated of late years, but at the same time, believes most strongly that the greatest obstacle ta true educational progress in this county is the multiplicity of subjects required to be taught to children under fourteen years of age, during which period it is of the greatest importance that a sure foundation should he laid, and a desire created in the minds of the children for nuttier studies by a fair mastery of a few rather than a smattering of many sabjects. This Committee, therefore, earnestly expresses the hope that the Board of Education and local education committees will take steps to bring about such a modification of the curriculum and revision of the methods of instruction in our elemen- tary schools as will best promote reel educational advancement." It was decided to ask each local committee to return answers to eleven questions, including the following : —Are the local committee of opinion that the results in the three essentials of instruction in our elementary schools have materially deteriorated of late years ? Are the committee of opinion that the deterioration is due to the multiplicity of subjects required to be taught in county schools to children under 14 years of age ? Does the deterioration affect both boys and girls; if not, which ? What additional subjects have been required since the "appointed day " to be taught in your area? What are the subjects (if any) which, in the opinion of the committee, should be omitted from the curriculum ? In what way should the mothids of instruction in schools in the committee's mei be modified ?</p>
      </div>
