        <p>Medical.</p>
        <p>;IPLEN DM Whooping Cough remedy sent post free I=3 for 1/Id. Watts, 8, Christmps St., Bristol.</p>
        <p>Spiritualism. Mr. Aaron Wilkinson conducted the meetings at the Druids' Hall last week-end. He is a capable exponent of the philosophy of Spiritualism (write a corres- pondent), and has many times given startling demonstrations of the nearne-s of the world of spirits and the possibility of communication between the two worlds. On Sunday Mr. Wilkinson gave two instruc- tive addresses on " Mediumship " and " The value of spiritual evidence," these being followed by clair- voyance. On Monday evening, be, after a very brief introduction, devoted the whole of the time to clairvoyance and psychic readings. There were good congregations.</p>
        <p>There is no trace of OUR LEGAL EXPERT apathy to-day in regard AND INSURANCE, to national insurance. Those people who have already qualified for " benefit " are singing the praises of the measure, while others are tumbling over one another with anxiety as to their real position under the Act, Our legal expert is being inundated with queries as to the various benefits and what constitutes eligibility. He finds that there is a midely-prevailing impression, which he explicitly contradicts, that where husband and wife are both insured persons the maternity benefit is 60s. instead of 30s. The maternity benefit is not more than 30s. under any circumstances, but in the case quoted the wife is also entitled to 7s. 6d. a week for one month, and if she suffers after that month from any other sickness not due to maternity, the 7s. 6d. is paid for a further period not exceeding six months. These advantges are so obvious as to obviate any extolling here. But there are numerous points on which insured persons are still anxious, and if they will only- send in their queries to our legal correspondent a prompt explanation will be forthcoming.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the THE PROTECTIONIST City of London Free POISON. Trade Committee, almost every aspect of Tariff Reform was adequately dealt with. One point, however, may be selected for especial notice. The defeat of the food-tax side of the Tariff Reform propaganda was caused by the refusal of the massive common sense of the community as a whole to accept the Tariff Reform fallacy that the consumer does not pay the tax. That fallacy has to be reckoned with equally in every metamorphosis which the Protean nature of Tariff Reform is prepared to assume. The consumer pays the duties. Every one is a consumer. It may be a seductive proposition to some producers that they should be allowed to raise prices on the community by the exclusion of that foreign competition which is the one safeguard against monopoly. But so long as the community thinks in terms of the community, Protection in any form or disguise will be shunned like poison,</p>
        <p>A protest, so belated as A!,BELATED BLEAT. to be ridiculous, against the theory that the rich man must have many more votes than the poor man, was raised at the Canon- street Hotel on Tuesday, with Mr. Balfour as the chief speaker. That protest, if raised at all, should have been made when Lord Lansdowne praised the Referendum as an instrument of gauging public opinion which eliminated the plural voter. How hard pressed for any logical argument the pro- testers are, can be gathered from Mr. Balfour's eminently sophistic reasoning. The franchise, he contends, is based, not on the right of individuals, but of localities to be represented. City magnates must be allowed to choose representatives for the locality in which they have their offices. They might just as reasonably contend that they have a right to choose the representa- tives for the localities /in which they play golf. Whatever basis of franchise is adopted, great wealth will always be able in some way or other to exert great influence, That the State should refuse to make the right of citizenship a more genuine thing lest the influence of great wealth should be impaired, is a doctrine which Mr. Balfour would hardly venture to maintain if he was appealing, as the leader of a Party,to a democratic verdict.</p>
      </div>
