      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY JANUARY 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>The difficulties which A CONSUMPTION were bound to arise in PROBLEM. getting the Insurance</p>
        <p>Act into working order are being overcome, and the release of the doctors from their pledge not to serve clears the pith in the most auxious of all directions. There remain, however, a num- ber of big problems still to be faced, and one of the most important of them is the extension of sanatorium treatment to the dependents of insured persons. There are as is pointed out in the interesting report issued by the County Councils Association after their interview with the Chancellor of the Exchequer, obvious reasons why this extension should be made if it can be found possible at all. The situation as it stands at present is very clearly put in the report. Such an extension would pro- bably double the number of persons requiring treatment, and though the additional cost for dispensary treatment might not be increased in proportion to the numbers, the number of beds in the sanatoria would be at least doubled, and expensive arrangements would have to be made for tuberculous children. As regards funds, many insurance committees might have some surplus available for depen- dents, but in almost all counties the inclusion of dependents would produce a considerable deficit. Of this the Government have pro- mised to pay one-half, and the claim of the County Councils is that they should be re- couped not less than 75 per cent. of the cost. The Councils appear to have faced the ques- tion is a spirit of impartiality which is • very satisfactory. Their careful calculations sug- gest that the deficit to be borne by the county rates would probably be between 1/2d. and Id. in the ,E. This, the tone of their report suggests, is a heavier burden than they are inclined to shoulder, and a further consultation with the Chancellor is asked for. It is earnestly to be hoped that some arrange- ment will ,be made which will enable the proposed extension of the sanatorium benefits to be made. It would increase the strength of the new force called into being to fight the dread scourge by 50 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>As every one who A HIGH LEVEL realises the attitude of DEBATE. the Welsh people_ con-</p>
        <p>- fidently anticipated, the bye-election in the Flint Burghs proves that there is no slackening in their demand for Disestablishment and the reclamation of national endowments for national purposes. The technical pre-occupations of the discus- sion in Committee were relieved on Monday oy a speech from Lord Hugh Cecil, on an amendment to use the funds released by the Bill for the concurrent endow- ment of religious work in Wales. Mr. Walter Roch, in a speech which breathed the 'same spirit, made an effective reply, in which .he quoted the pregnant words of Bishop Thirlwall, the most statesmanlike and :intellectual prelate who ever administered a Welsh See. "No material offerings," this Bishop of St,David's declared," are so accept- able to the Almighty as those which are beneficial to men." In a single sentence, the plea that it is "robbing God" to devote some of the endowments of a long-denationalised "Church to the purpose of elevating the ,condition of the people, was convincingly ans wered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>We hear there is every likelihood of prac- tically the whole of the medical profession in Warrington, eventually enrolling themselves .on the panel to administer the benefits of the Insurance Act.</p>
        <p>- • - The solemn giant of Printing House Square ,(the "Times ") in its annual financial review -says _ _</p>
        <p>" Warrington and the district may be described as the centre of the Northern tanning trade for heavy sole leather ; within a radius of a few miles there are over 25 heavy-sole tanneries,using on an average from 8,000 to 10,000 hides per week."</p>
        <p>This means 13,000,000 heavy hides year. As the whole of the hides tanned in the 'United Kingdom are only about .3 millions it is difficult to see how in the Warrington district we can tan more than four times the total tanned. Our local modesty compels us to call attention to this exaggeration, which Probably occurs through the introduction of a superfluous nought.</p>
        <p>41, Spiritualism may have its good points, but as an aid to the detection of crime it falls far behind Sherlock Holmes, though both favour The deductive method. In a local slander 'case on Thursday some curious facts were disclosed. One woman had lost a ring,and on being told by a Spiritualist that a person whose name was Mary had taken the ring, She immediately called on a neighbour bear- ing that name and said -"You area big thief." Mary was naturally aggrieved, and as the Slander was repeated at her expense, she s_ought to right her wrongs in the County Court. The Judge sided with her view of the slander, and expressed regret that the Spiritualist was not a co-defendant in the ease. We are sure every Mary in the land Will agree with him.</p>
        <p>* Lord Roberts, the veteran head of the National Service League, in acknowledging a birthday present from members of that °rganization, in London this week, paid a 'Pecial tribute to Captain Crosfield's energies an the compulsory training campaign. He „,„salley owed much to the captain who had -°1.4--eci indefatigably for the League, its progress in the North of England being largely due to his devotion and industry. Further by his great generosity he had helped the League on several occasions to surmount its difficulties.—Captain Crosfield's energies in the cause are unquestioned, but, in spite of all the strenuous advocacy of the last few years, the British workingman is as disinclined as ever to take upon himself the Yoke of Conscription. * *</p>
        <p>Liverpool councillors who are now going seriously into the housing conditions of the .it3r, have an example of what is possible by a Public spirited movement in their own area. The Marquis of Salisbury whose Rural }lousing Bill was before the House of Lords a few evenings ago is interested in OF Liverpool Garden Suburb at Thingwali ,I.A.Thich is being developed on co-partnership lines by a society of which Mr Henry Vivian ex-M.P. for Birkenhead is the Chairman. Associated with him are Sir Benjamin John- 50n, Mr J F L Brunner, M.P., Lord Howick, J Moon, J.P. Mr F Litchfield, and others 'ldentified with the movement for better hous- -1.11. About 180 acres are being developed and itt. is intended to erect houses in the propor- .2" of ten to the acre—an object lesson in town planning that should prove of consider- ableinterest to northern towns.</p>
        <p>* - Councillor Roberts, in characteristically I:llphatic style, told the Junior Liberals in his address on Town Council affairs on Thursday evening, that the type of man who was now offering himself for service on ~,, socially ieiPal bodies was neither educattonally,, the intellectually nor in any other way equal of his predecessors of twenty years t,h i —g°' We are afraid there is a great deal of rut in the contention, but the difficulty s induce men ot outstanding ability and Tfluence to come forward. Councillor .IRobert s advanced as the chief reasons for he deterioration the facts that the great 113111mercial and ratepaying concerns, as such, lave no direct representation, and that Political considerations enter far too largely Into the selection of candidates for the 9,.°1-incil• The first obstacle might easily be ,•,ernoved • by leglislation, if a sufficiently Tong. public opinion were created, but, not- withstanding the admitted faults of the ,Present system, it seems hopeless ever to Pect the elimination of the party political 'ketnent from municipal contests. Two t 1 * eatrical and musical stars who 'ere great favourites with Warrington ,itcliences have passed away gince our last Idssue• The death was announced on Satur- tay of Mr J W Turner, the famous operatic cen°r, who, along with his English Opera ic?inPanY, which he formed so far back as "185, had frequently appeared at the Royal T ourt Theatre and the Parr Hall. Mr urner, who was born in 1845, spent his early iife as a farmer's boy and even in later years he pade a hobby Of farming. To hear him ?king the tenor lead in such operas as girl " Maritana," and "The Bohemian was a sheer delight and at one time there was no more popular figure- on the English operatic stage. On Thurs- day night Mr. Auguste Van Biene, the well- known actor-musician, died suddenly while • .1-arforming at the Brighton Hippodrome. Many Warringtonians will recall with plea- _lare the performances of " The Broken Mel,)dy " which this great actor-'cellest gave at the Parr Hall. He stood supreme as the _-xploiter of sentiment and few instrumenta- _lists could so work upon the emotions of an a_udience. It will be remembered that Mr. Yan Biene was appearing at the Parr Hall lri January 1901 when the news of the death of Queen Victoria was received and further Performances during that week had to be .cancelled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"An Indian Curse."</p>
        <p>As the title suggests, this romantic drama deals largely, with life in the East. Of its many novel featuress,the chief is the unorthodox nature of the plot. Briefly, it is, that on the invitation of the hero, John Waring, the Prince of Barona, an Indian potentate, conies to England and stays with Waring as a guest. The latter is engaged to marry the unscrupulous Muriel Dugarde who, however, is overcome by the plausible tongue, and fawning ways of the Prince. She is not, however, entirely decided in her favour until she discovers that he is immensely rich and he promises her all that she desires in the way of dress and social life. She wishes the death of her affianced Englishman, hut the Prince's plans are frus- trated. Muriel goes to the East, where her Indian admirer assures her that " there is no sweeter fi ,wer in the garden of Allah than the English rose." Waring also goes East and meets with his true love Alzeda, whereupon the Princess is consumed with jealousy. Waring scorns her and because of her infidelity, the Prince, after the pleasant manner of Eastern Princes, dooms her to a most torturing death, whilst Waring and his Eastern fiancee enter upon married bliss. In its orgetrus Eastet,n setting with its well trained dancers and chorus the play is decir'edly attractive and, as presented by Arthur Hinton and Leila illwoods Co, cannot fail to prove highly successful. Mr Herbert Evelyn is decidedly clever in his representation of John Waring and infuses real life into the part. His acting is very forceful, being especially worthy of commendation in the scene where he learns that the woman he has trusted, loves wealth better than anything, and where he is transformed from the ardent lover into a scorning, mocking, jeering and revengeful man. Mr Arthur Hinton is responsible for a realistic delineation ef the Prince of Barons. He is the typical, sauve and salaaming oriental and expresses his lines clearly and definitely. Miss Florence Zillw oAI soon earns the execratiorts of the audience for the telling manner in which she presents Muriel Du,v,arde. Mr Frank McClellan appears with great success as Mehenet Ali, the servile and faithful follower of the Prince. Mr Arthur Keancl is highly successful as Mirza, the blind father of Alzetia. The latter character could not be in more than able hands than those of Miss Annette Howard, who, by her graceful manner and personal charm, enlists the sympathies of the audience. Mr Alfred G Raymond and Miss Bertha Frlsing as Tony Smart and Donna, the principal Salome dancer, add the humour which is necessary to relieve the tragedy of the play. Mr Fred W Mann is an accomplished musical director</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Pantomime will again hold sway at the Itylands- Street house next week when Mr Gar Kiddie's com- pany will present" Mother Goose" twice nightly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>ROYAL ALBERT INSTITUTION.</head>
        <p>ROYAL ALBERT INSTITUTION.</p>
        <p>Next Week's Concert in the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>•We would remind our readers that the concert which is being organised by Miss Mary Gornall and promoted by the committee of the Warrington Dis- trict Ladies' Association, of which Mrs. Aylmer Frost is president, will take place on Thursday next in the Parr Hall at 7.45. The proceeds will be given to the ab we Institution at Lancaster (which was built and is supported by voluntavy contribu- tions), towards the maintenance of the six children from Warrington district, at present undergoing training there. Only ;OS was forwarded from Warrir,gton last year for this purpose, the subscriptions having gone do an considerably.prohably owing to ti-e strikes in the early part of the year. The perhaps novel form of admittance to the concert will he by invitation card. During the course of the evening a silver collection will be made by 28 ladies very well-known in the neighb3urhood of kVarrington. Four of the artistes performing are professionals who are giving their service for nominal fees. We hope there will be a large and appreciative audience to repay them. for their generous assistance. It is to be hoped that all those who have not yet answered will still find they are able to accept the invitation. People whose names have inadvertently been over- , looked may on application obtain invitation cards . from Miss Mary Gornall, llibblesdale, Grappenhall, who has a limited number to dispose of. The pro- gramme, which will be a very interesting one, will include recitations by Mrs Ridgway, the well- known authress cf whom Warrington is justly proud, and violin solos by Miss Amethe Leadbetter, of whom Wilhelmy, the celebrated violinist wrote " Miss Amethe Leadbetter is born to be a violin player, and I consider her one of the greatest of the day." Miss Hilda Cragg James, wh,se singing called forth such great enthusiasm at Miss Gornall's matinee concert, will contribute three items to the programme. There will also be a trio for piano and strings, a duet for two pianos, and a pian 'forte quartette. The other artistes to appear are the Misses G S Stewart, M Fairclough, J Fairclough, M Reynolds, M Goruall aral Mr Bernard Drewsen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>LADY AWARDED £5OO DAMAGES.</head>
        <p>LADY AWARDED £5OO DAMAGES.</p>
        <p>Mrs Alice Crosby, wife of Major 3 G Crosby, con- nected with the War Office, Vas on Wednesday, awarded £5OO agreed damages against the London General Omnibus Co., Ltd. Mrs Crosby alleged that she was getting off a 'bus in Earl's Court-road when the driver started the bus suddenly. She fell and broke s leg. A stay of execution was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>~•, 2 , , _</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>Mr. Masterman has presented a return of the amounts paid in fees by the Treasury in the year 1912 to barristers who are members of Parliament. The sum received by Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., was £l2 2s. 6d.</p>
        <p>A superior young curate who is visiting a young lady: "No, dear lady, frankly. I can not persuade myself that it would be safe to let women vote." "And what, may I ask, is your reason for opposing it ?" "Well, dear lady, I can not but think that if women were to vote it would make them, let us say—a little—er—er—masculine." Oh. I don't know. It has never had that effect upon the clergy." *</p>
        <p>Many local opponents of the Welsh Church Bill are wondering at the conspicuous silence of Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., on the subject during he debates in the House of Commons. While the member for the neighbouring constituency of Newton (Lord Wolmer) has been actively espousing the cause of the Establishment in Wales, Mr Smith has so far not seen fit to intervene. Those local Church- men who find the Bill unpalatable have emphasised their views in no uncertain fashion and Mr Smith's apparent indifference is therefore all the more surprising.</p>
        <p>The doctors of Warrington have had an exceedingly busy time during the past week signing the medical benefit tickets of insured workers and it may safely be assumed that the bulk of the tickets have now been endorsed. In conversation with a medical man last evening, we learned that there has been a decided turning of opinion in favour of the Act in quarters which, by the benefits came into operation, were rather sceptical or openly hostile. People who, although in the doctors hands, are no longer under the necessity of paying doctors' bills, have now begun to discover that there some virtue in the Act—in fact the only grumblers are those who see no immediate prospect of requiring their money's worth of medicine and attendance. * *</p>
        <p>Easter this year falls on almost the earliest date possible, March 23rd, the earliest day for fifty-seven years. As is generally known, the date is fixed by Act of Parlia- ment, which prescribes that Easter Day shall always fall upon the first Sunday after the full moon which happens upon or next after March 21st. So it comes about that Good Friday this year is on March 21st and Easter Sunday on the 23rd. In other words, Lent is over almost before spring, according to the calendar, has begun. Shrove Tuesday is on the 4th and Ash Wednesday the sth of February. This early date has once more raised the question of having a fixed Easter, but the innate cons,n-vatism of the British people on questions of this kind would appear to place such a reform of the calendar out of the question. *</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Grant-Duff, a Local Government Board Inspector, has been tellirg the Maccles- field Board of Guardians how ill-adapted the institutional atmosphere in which Work- house children are brought up, often is to the after-conditions of home life. At one of the workhouses in his district, where everything else was very well done for the children, he asked where the children's bath was. "Oh," replied the master, "we haven't got a bath, but we have got a big room which is cemented all over. We turn the youngsters in there and turn the hose on them, and I tell you, our children are celebrated for their cleanli- ness." (Laughter.) His reply was that that was all very fine, but when the children went out into the world they were not going to find a room and a hose, with somebody to hold the hose. Children taught in that way would not know how to clean themselves with a bit of soap and a bucket of water. * * *</p>
        <p>The importance of nursing as a form of medical benefit was recognised by the framers oe enabling at bh lei nga lnsuranceorov Act,approved societiesand t he o r irs a provision Insurance Committees to grant subscriptions or dona- tainopnosinfotr the support to ort aotftdend district nurses pesesarnsdontso. Hitherto the arrangements with the doctors have absorbed attention to the exclusion of this and some cognate matters, but now that these arrangements are completed Sir Archibald Williamson, who takes a keen practical interest in the work of district nursing, is inviting the Insurance Com- missioners to give the question their serious consideration. He desires to know whether the Commissioners are themselves prepared to make grants for the support of nurses. It is at least doubtful (says a London corres- pondent) whether under the Act they have this power. They may, however, be able to suggest some machinery by which the various approved societies in a district can jointly effect an arrangement with the local nursing association. It will be greatly to the advantage of insured persons if adequate and efficient nursing can, wherever neces- sary, be given as part of the benefit provided by the Act. * * *</p>
        <p>Mr Augustine Birrell, who will address a meeting at Warrington next month, was sixty-three years of age on Sunday. This distinguished son of Liverpool is by profession a barrister, and was sufficiently successful as to gain "silk " when quite a comparatively young man at the bar. He is a bencher of the Inner Temple, and for a time was the Quain Professor of Law at London University. Mr Birrell has written many books, of which his " Obiter Dicta " (which may 'oe translated as "Sayings by the Way") are the most famous. He is essentially a true son of literature, and his personal appearance gives the right keynote to his character. A parallel between Mr. Birrell (the literary Mr. Birrell, not the Radical politician) and Charles Lamb is sufficiently obvious to the man in the street to be frequently drawn by passing scribes, and has been emphasised by Mr Birrell's own avowed appreciation at all times for the author of the" Essays of Elia." It is not often given to a man (says the "Liverpool Post and Mercury ")to add a new word to his country's vocabulary, but "to Birrell" and a " Birrellism " have become necessary to be included in every standard English dictionary. Mr. Birrell's association with the long robe has not prevented him giving us !even some Birrelisms on the law. Not so very long ago he assured us that "the law may be an ass; but it is also a gentleman " !</p>
        <p>The Cambridge University Local Examinations pass list for December iNcludes the name of Keith Rymer Young in the First Class Honours senior division, with distinctions in mathematics and spoken French. Our Poultry Articles for the coming season will commence on Saturday, February Bth. " Redcap" specially invites suggestions and queries from readers.</p>
        <p>Important to Secretaries of Approved Societies.</p>
        <p>We wish to inform all secretaries of approved societies under the National Insurance Act that we have in type the necessary forms for the disbursement of benefits und,?r the Act. We shall he glad if secre- taries will call and see us about their requirements. Press Club Dinner.</p>
        <p>A highly enjoyable evening was spent on Siturdp.y, at the Hotel St. George, Liverpool, by the members of the Liverpool Press Club and their friends, the occasion being the club's annual dinner. Mr. Caleb Rees (president) was in the chair. The speeches were brief but exceedingly felicitous in tone. "The Press Club " was proposed by Councillor J Hawthorn Warrington) and acknowledged by the President. Picturedrome. On Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, the manage- ment is showing" The Massacre." This picture has been obtained by special request of numerous patrons, and is said to be the most expensive moving picture- drama yet produced. It is an example of brilliant photography and perfect acting. The star picture for Thursday, Friday and Saturday is entitled " The Usurer's Grip," a pathetic drama showing the ordeal of a man with a family in the clutches of a vicious kan shark. Patrons should look out for " Les Miserables." The date will be announced shortly. " Sunday."</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Drama tic Society are entitled to the warmest congratulations upcia the notable triumph they have achieved in their produc- tion of "Sunday." At the final performance on Saturday evening the theatre was crowded in every part, and the artistes entered into their respective parts with the utmost enthusiasm, Miss Nellie Mullins being the prime favourite in the difficult title role. At the close the curtain was again rut g up and Miss Mullins, Miss Dorothy Browne and Mrs F Griffiths were presented with handaome bouquets and other tokens of appreciation. Poe gentlemen's services were also fittingly recognized.</p>
        <p>FOR MODERN FIREPLACES and Cooking Ringes COM hining economy with simplicity try J. S. GARLAND Ironmonger, Bridge et., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Church Tea Party. The annual Congregational tea party in connection with Emmanuel Reformed Episcopal Church, was held on Thnrsday in Emmanuel Institute, when a large number of parishioners sat down to tea. After tea a most enjoyable entertainment was provided, thanks to the untiring efforts of Mr W Jowett, the church organist, who was also the accompanist for the even- ing. The choir ably rendered two glees, Mrs A Landon proved very skilful as a violinist, and also very entertaining in a musical monologue. Aliss Whitmore and Miss Davy ably acquitted themselves, Mr Warburton sang "Trooper Johnny Ludlow" and " Lighterman Tom," while the members of the choir concluded with a humourous dialogue. During the evening the Rev W Jaye briefly addressed the company, and expressed his satisfaction at seeing the hall so well filled with friends of Emmanuel Church. The usual votes of thanks were passed with great enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>Employers and Insurance. The first annual meeting of the Employers' Parlia- mentary Association was held on Friday of last week in the Church House, Manchester, Sir Charles Macara in the chair.—Mr W P Ryl ands (Warrington) in moving a resolution dealing with the constitution of the association, said the large employer unfortunately was often so much concerned with his own affairs that he was ill-adapted to organization and co-operation with other employers. Burdens were placed upon his shoulders, and he felt that he was only one of a number sharing the infliction, and he growled about it but did nothing. That association desired to ensure that all important trades and dis- tricts should be able, through their representatives, to make their views felt when any legislation affecting employers was proposed. (Applause.)—Mr R I Wynne-Jones (Warrington) said the important matter was to increase the membership of the association which was much indebted to the members of the committee, who hail worked so hard to bring it to a state of such good organization as it was now in. (Hear, hear.)—The resulution was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>Persona'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>LONDON WOMAN FOUND DROWNED.</head>
        <p>LONDON WOMAN FOUND DROWNED.</p>
        <p>Believed to ipive fallen from the cliffs at East Luiworth, Dorsetshire, Mrs A Hatton, aged 28, the wife of a London hotel proprietor, has been found dead in the sea at Son hbourne, Bournemouth. She had been missing for a fortnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>PICKPOCKETS' TERROR RETIRES.</head>
        <p>PICKPOCKETS' TERROR RETIRES.</p>
        <p>Known as the " Masher King." and feared by almost every profeasiowil pickpocket in London, ex-Detective Sergeant Henry Hancock was presented on Wednesday with a gold watch by the police officers and men of the Woolwich district on his retirement after 25 years' service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Funeral of the Late Colour-Sergeant Daniels</head>
        <p>Funeral of the Late Colour-Sergeant Daniels</p>
        <p>Honour was done to the memory of an old Terri- torial on Sunday afternoon, when members of the local battalion attended the funeral at the Warring- ton Cemetery of Colour-Sergeant Abel Daniels, who passed away last week, at the age of 73 years, at the home of his. daughter, Mrs Hinchclifte, 166, Battersby- lone. For over fifty years the deceased had been connected with the Volunteers and Territorials and was a popular figure at the annual enca-npment, his knowledge of the Volunteer movement and his fund of good stories enabling him to interest and entertain his comrades. During the latter part of his career at the Drill Hall be was in charge of the sergeants' mess. He was previously in business as a shopkeeper in Tilley-street. A devoted member of the Church of England, he had latterly attended the services held in St. Peter's Church. He leaves three sons and one daughter. The funeral was c mducted by the Rector (Rev Canon Willis). The deceased's three sons and diughter and other relatives were present as well as the following past non-commissioned officers of the battalion: Sergeant- Major Sullivan. Bandmasters T Armstrong and Joseph Richardson, Quartermaster T Watmough, Staff- Sergeant Robinson (A S.O ), Colour-Sergeants Lloyd, Russell, Walker, G Watmough, Cliffe and Sergeants Bramball, Taylor, Kertnode, Dagnall, Rooke, Elliott, Appleton, Gill and Houghton. Police Ball.</p>
        <p>The members of our local Police Force are not only " terrors to evil doers" but are men of many parts, whose energies find expression in various directions. The Constabulary, too, have a happy knack of carry- ing their undertakings to a successful issue, and the result of their most recent effort has been no exception to the rule. We refer to the first annual Police Ball, which was held in Parr Hall on Thursday evening last and which was a highly successful and enjoyable function. There was an attendance of close upon two hundred, including a fair proportion of p &gt;Boo officers with their lady friends. His Worship., the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph, Chairman of the Watch Committee) and the Mayoress, Mrs Neild, attended the ball, as did also the Chief Constable, Mr Martin Nicholls. Dancing commeneed at 8 p.m. and was kept up until 2 a.w. to the excellent music of Mr Tom Boothroyd's band. Toe catering was in the hands of the Warrington Cafes Co. and, needless to say, gave every satisfaction. A pleasing feature, which added greatly to the enjoyment of those pres•mt, was the handsome scheme of decoration of Parr Hall. This work had been carried out by Mr Ralph Johnson, Bridge Street, who very kindly allowed the Police the use of it free. The duties of M.C. were efficiently carried out by Constables Bennett, Hayes, Coombes and Norridge. The object in view in promoting the ball was the raising of funds for the Police Recreation Club, and it is gratifying to learn that a satisfactory sum is likely to have been realisad. " Prudential " Staff Dinner. This new recognised annual event given by Mr S Brocklehurst, superintendent, and his assistants took place at the Winmarleizli Cafe, Sankey-street, on Thursday evening, covers being set for 50. Since the last dinner the National Health Act had come into operation, and in a speech of some length Mr Brockle- hurst took the opportunity to enlighten those present on a few points relative to the payment of the benefits. He further pointed out the startling fact that out of the 14 millions of persoas insured under this Act only about 8 million cards had been'collected for the first quart T., and of these numbers the Prudential, who claimed the magnificent number of :3 million insured persons, had collected almost their full number of cards showing their wonderful organisation The Wan ington dis- trict had seem ed well over 5,000 members, and fifty claims had been paid in the first week. Further speeches followed by the Assistant Superintendents and several agents, and much enthusiasm prevailed in the good pia spects for progress during ihr.3 present year. The proceedir.gs were appreciably enlivened. by a splendid programme of music to which the following contributed :—The Misses Brough and Brocklehurst, with Messrs Brocklehurst, Jack, Walker, Whitehouse, and Whitfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Next Week at the Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>Next Week at the Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>The smallest womanin the world—Lady Little— heads a big bill at the Hippodrome next week and her visit to Warrington will no doubt arouse great interest She will he supported by the following artistes : Graham, the human ;marionette; Conway George in vocal and character impres-ions ; Cavalotte Brothers, all-round instrumentalists; Frank and Beryl Harmer, vocal comedy duo; Hal Bryan, vocal comedian ; Ragotio, in a sparkling unique novelty, and Little Connie Edmonde, juvenile serio comedienne. National Health Week. The Agenda Club, which last year inaugurated National Health Week. is looking this year for a celebration on a more National scale. The experiment of 1912 was more than justified and the experience then gained will be useful for future celebrations. For 1913 the week is fixed rather earlier viz., from Gth to 12th April. Already our own Health Com- mittee has expressed its willingness to take part in the proceedings, so that a move of some kind is likr ly to be made shortly. Meantime preachers and teachers and societies interested will do well to make a note of the date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Reading Circle on the Administration of</head>
        <p>The Reading Circle on the Administration of</p>
        <p>Charities.</p>
        <p>The Reading Circle held its monthly discussion meeting, on Monday, at the Guild of Help Offices under the presidency of Mrs Hill. A paper was read by Mr C A Littler,based upon two chapters of Rogers' " Charitable Relief." It dealt with standards of life and character arid with State and private relief. In the discussion which followed, attention was drawn to the decay of the apprenticeship system and the probable effects of raising the school age. Other topics were briefly touched -upon, such as family co-operation in digress, the economic va:tre of the housewife, causes of unemployment and reasons for the extension of out-relief. The Reading Circle is proving most useful for its study of theory combined with practical work.</p>
        <p>CUTLERY.—Joseph Rodgers &amp; Sons' celebrated Knives n d Forks, &amp;c.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, Bridge St., NVarrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Death of Mrs. Benjamin Bennett.</head>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Benjamin Bennett.</p>
        <p>Many Warringtonirms, as well as numerous people engaged in agricultural pursuits on the Cheshire side of the town, will learn with deep regret of the death of Mrs Benjamin Bennett,formerly ot Hatton and more recently of Clap Gates farm, Lively-lane, Warrington. .Mrs Bennett, who belonged to a well-known farming family, was in her SO:h year and had lived in retirement for the last ten ye.irs with her husband and daughter at 15, Victoria-road, Stockton Heath. Her husband survives her and is in his 84th year. There are ten children, six daughters and four sons. Mrs. Bennett will he remembered as a lady of homely habits and lovable disposition. She and her family worshipped at Bold-street Wesleyan Church and there, as elsewhere, she was held in high esteem. The funeral will take place at the Stockton Heath Church on Monday at three o'clock, when the Vicar will officiate. LAMPS, Oil Stoves, Coek Stoves.—J.S. GARLAND Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. N.S P.C.C. Bail.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall was the scene of another fashionable solial function on Tuesday evening, the occasion being the annual ball in aid of the Warrington branch of that excellent institution—the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children. About 120 ladies and gentlemen were present and a capital pro- gramme of dance music was provided by Mr W P Bayley's band, Liverpool. The hall, with its artistic decorations, presented a strikingly beautiful appear- ance. The ptatform was a perfect picture of choice blooms, plants and ferns, which had been lent by Lady Greenall. At the opposite end of the room was an effective panel arrangement of light draperies, embellished with long shoats of creepers, and at the base of the mirrored centre was a fine collection of exotics. Indian-worked cloth curtained the walls. A special floor had been pre- pared and in every respect the comfort of the guests was studied. The arrangements were in the capable hands of Mrs F Aylmer Frost as hon secretary and bon 'treasurer, who was ably assisted by a committee comprising Miss H A Armitage, Mrs Broadbent, Mrs John Coxon, Mrs C Shepherd-Cross, Mrs Elgood, Mrs V B Davies, Mrs Greenall, Mrs P Mortimer, Mrs W Peter Rylands and Mrs Watts. The ladies of the committee acted as stewardesses. Supper was well served by the Winmarleigh Cafe Co. Ltd. Donations were received for the ball fund from Lady Greenall, Captain Crosfield, Mr W Peter Rylands, Mrs John Cox.m, Mrs Long, Mr Leyton,Mrs Mortimer and others, and the ladies of the committee supplied most, of the decorative material. ANTHRACITE STOVES, clean, cheerful, smolielenF.4, no fumes.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND. 51, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Mr. Piercy lies la 1 a varied exte ierc3 in municipal work, and prior to taking up his preszwt position at Stoke-on-Trent he was for seven years with the Middlesex County Council as assistant engineer. His other appointments have been : -Two years with the Sunderland Corporation ; two years at Sheffield, and two at Richmond - in each case as assistant engineer. He served his articles with the Borough Surveyor of Hanley and remained for two years as his assistant. Mr. Piercy was recently succPssful in obtaining the second premium of fifty guineas in an open COW petition promoted by the county borough of Huddersfield for town-planning designs for four areas in that municipality, He is thirty-six years of age and was educated at Kinver Grammar School, Worcestershire. He is a member of the Institution of Municipal and County Engineers and of the Royal Sanitary Institute.</p>
        <p>,••• f e „ . , :</p>
        <p>Mk. Ai. A. The New Deputy Borough Surveyor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Late Mr. Joseph Pennington.</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. Joseph Pennington.</p>
        <p>In the presence of many sorrowing relatives and friends, the funeral tlok plAce at the Warrington Cemetery on Tuesday afternoon of the late Mr. Joseph Pennington, who passed away at his residence, 42. Folly-lane, last Friday. Mr Pennington was a native of Newton-le-Willows, hut most of his life had been spent in Warrington. For close upon fifty years he bad been in the service of the L. &amp; N.-W. Railway Company—for a long period as engine driver. Be was a regular worshipper at St. Paul's Church, and had held the once of sidesrnan for about fourte.-n years. Mr. Pennington was a member of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants and the National Indep9ndent Order of Ocldfellows, and was held in great esteem by all associated witn the local branches of these organiza- tions. Prior to the interment, a service was held In St. Paul's Church, the Revs W Bracecamp and E E Peters efficiating. In the course of a short address, the Rev W Bracecamp said the late Mr Pennington's comrades respected him as one whose word was his bond. By his death his family had lost a good and faithful father and St. Paul's Church, a willing helper. The nymns "A few more years shall roll " and "0 God our help in ages past" were sung. In addition to members of the family there were present the following representives of the L. and N.-W. Railway (loco shed) :—Messrs Cresswell, Jeffreys, Gardner, Brandwood, Mulvey, and W Hill. The bearers were Messrs .t 1 Gleave, 11 Roberts, H Shaw, T Potter, F Price and J Jones. Floral tributes were sent by the following :—"Mamma and Mabel," "Joe, Polly and* kiddies," " Harold and Vera," " Walter and Clara," " Will and Flettie," "Jim and Mary," Mr and Mrs Hodgkinson and family, Vicar and officers at St Paul's Church, Workmates (Loco shed), " Neighbours," "Ida," " Louie, Hettie and Ada," "Torn and Lily," Mr and Mrs Small (Aylesbury) Mr Lowden Wells had charge of the funeral arrange- ments.</p>
        <p>Firemen at Dinner. Our local fire fighters had a much more congenial task than responding to the warning of the fire bell on Saturday evening, when they assembled at the Lion Hotel for their annual dinner. It was a jovial gathering characterised by a true spirit of, comrade- ship, and the evening will be long and pleasantly remembered. Supt. Turner occupied the chair at the postprandial proceedings and was supported by Alderman Burton (chairman of the Fire Engine Com- mittee) and Councillor Archer.—ln propesing the toast of the " Fire Engine Committee:" the Deputy. Superintendent referred to the great improvements which have been effected in the equipment of the brigade during the year, and said the members were deeply thankful for the kindly considera- tion which had always been shown to them —Alderman Burton explained that it was the earnest desire of the Fire Brigade Committee to make the brigade, if not superior, at least the equal to any brigade in Lancashire. He c )ntended that they had had splendid results for the amount which was levied on the rates, and he attributed this to the loyalty of the men, who worked so harmaniously to the orders of their veteran Superintendent. Councillor Archer said there was a tendency at one period to decry the brigade. Happily, all this had now passed away, and instead of hypercritical comments, one heard on all sides praise extended to the brigade for their loyalty and devotion.—The toast of " The Superintendent was proposed by Alderman Lurtoti, who spoke highly of the self-sacrificin;,, work which Superintendent Turner had done during his 50 years' connection with the brigade —Councillor Archer followed in a similar strain and added that the superintendent "did good by stealth and blushed to find it fame." (Applause.) The Superintendent thanked all the members of the brigade for the way they had carried out their duties and intimated that no leader could have a better set of men under his command.—ln replying to the toast of his health, the Deputy-Superintendent gave an interesting resume of the year's conflagrations. A capital musical programme was gone thr. ugh, songs being rendered by Messrs G Vernon, R Cook, W Dalton, J Archer, H Spencer, A Blackshaw,J Johnson, S Iriam, J Wakefield, F Galrnore and E Gleave. Mr J Gore. the blind pianist, was the accompanist, and also sang several songs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>QUERIES OF ANSWERS.</head>
        <p>QUERIES OF ANSWERS.</p>
        <p>OUR READERS are invited to send legal queries to be answered week by week. Inquiries must be short, concise, and plainly written, and the name and address of the writer given to show that the inquiry is genuine. The replies are by a member of the profession, and every effort is made to secure accuracy, but we cannot assume any responsibility for the opinion or advice given. Mark all envelopes containing queries " Legal," end address them to the EDITOR," EXAMINER,' WARRINGTON. If you do not wish initials to be used in the reply please suggest a nom-de-plume.</p>
        <p>SICKNESS. Where b ;fir husband and wife are insured the maternity benefit is still only 305., not, Os. But in that came the wife gets also 7s. 6d. a week for one month, and if she suffers after that month from any other sickness not due to maternity ' The 7s. Gd. is paid for a further period not exceed- ing six months. A single wcrnan is entitled to the 305., but nothing further unless suffering from some other disease not connected with child birth in which case she gets 7s. 6d. a week for a month. ENQUIRE.—No person over 65 years of age is required or authorized to become an insured person under time Act with one exception, namely, a person between the age of 65 and 70 who at the commence- ment of the Act was an employed person. Persons so excepted are put on special terms as to payment and benefit, for particulars of which apply to the Approved Society or the Insurance Committee. P.W.—A certificate of any duly qualified medical man or of any certificated midwife is sufficient authority for payment of maternity benefits. In the case of medical benefits the doctor must be on the local panel. In the case of sick benefits the doctor need not be on the panel. X.Y.—lf the husband is the insured person the local Insurance Authority or Approved Society may require in doubtful cases some proof of the marriage. If the 'woman is herself insured she is the person entitled to the money and no proof of marriage could be demanded unless the wife claimed "additional benefits." A marriage certificate is only one means of proof, but where obtained it roust be paid for at the usual rate. IGNORAMUS —"Land and Property" is rather vague. Was the land of freehold or leasehold tenure ? What was the nature of time" property"? We assume that by "land "you mean fret hold land, and by " pro- perty " you meam personal property. In that case : (1) B's widow should have received during her life an equal fourth part of the income of the "property," but nothing from the land; on her death intestate her next of kin took her place. (2) on A's death C had two equal fourths of the land and property. On his death intestate his son would succeed to the land (subject to a certain right called " dower " of the widow) and the widow and son (the widow one-third the son two-thirds) to the property. On the death of both intestate the relatives would take according to the law of descent, of real and personal property respectively. It is obvious therefore that D could be made to account by the relatives of B and C. If the definitions above given of the respective meanings of the words " land " and "property" are wrong our correspondent had better write us again. KNOTS.—The husband must provide his wife with such necessaries as are requited by persons in her rank of life; if he does not she can pledge his credit to obtain them. If he does, the wife cannot pledge his credit unless he authorizes her to do so either (1) expressly, or (2) constructively, i.e., by his practice of p tying bills for goods ordered by her. To end cmstructive agency he must give all persona who have previously given his wife credit notice not to do so in future. In the case of a first trans- action it is different, for there the husband can repudiate a debt unless expressly authorized by him, or unless for necessaries (subject to what has been said above). LEGACY.—The validity of the Will is not affected if it is witnessed by relatives, but if a relative or any other witness so attesting (or the wife or husband of such relative or other witness) is given a legacy, the legacy is void. WINDFALL.—The Trustees of the Will should pay the interest earned by the legacy to the child's parents if the child is living with his parents— assuming the parents need and desire such payment. If under such circumstances the Trustees refuse they can be compelled to pay the income to the parents or other the child's guardians. If the income is larger than is needed for the child's support, then a sufficient portion should be so paid and the rest retained by the Trustees on behalf of the child. If the income is small and insufficient for the legatee's support and further help is necessary the will or the Court may authorize recourse to the principal by way of supplement.</p>
        <p>.• lett- ..„,</p>
      </div>
