      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <p>- . .</p>
        <p>"We are continually receiving circulars from the -Home Office asking us not to send people to prison." such was the statement made by Alderman Smethurst at the Warrington Police Court on Monday during the hearing of a case in which Ellen Barrett, a stranger to the town, was charged with having been drunk and dis- orderly. When the woman was cilled, the Chief Con- stable said it might be desirable for her to be remanded for twenty four hours, because of a complaint against the prisoner that she had stolen a skirt. The prosecutrix in the other charge which might he pre- ferred against the prisoner was within the precincts of the Court, but apparently horrified at having to appear j at such a place as the Police Court, she wa not in a fit. •condition to be put in the box. In any c se, even if the prosecutrix was ell right. ttie police w re not ready to proceed with the charge. If the woman was remanded he would mention those facts to the magistrates who .had to deal with the prisoner. The Mayor: It would be rather hard lines on the prisoner if she were not guilty, to he kept in for twenty-four hours. The Chief Constable agreed that it would, but said that the Home Office authorities were anxious that people should not be re-arrested immediately they were set free, if that could be avoided by taking all possible charges together. Alderman Smethurst: And they are quite the other way abnut, too. They are anxious that we should not lend people to prison if we can possibly hell) it. We are continually receiving circulars from the Home Office .asking us not to send people to prison. The Chief Constable: We don't ask you to send any- body to prison. We merely bring them here if they ,have done wrong for you to deal with them as you think fit. It was decided to proceed with the charge of -drunkenness and Barrett was fined ss.</p>
        <p>• • In the 2-N 011 [ITS t!" s ALDERMAN SMETHURST AND THE HOME OFFICE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Borough Police Court •SATURDAY-13pfore Alderman Tinnion (in the chair), Councillor T Locker, Messrs A J Jolley and T</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court •SATURDAY-13pfore Alderman Tinnion (in the chair), Councillor T Locker, Messrs A J Jolley and T</p>
        <p>Pemberton. WOULD NOT WORK.—Edward Bentley, 78, School- 'brow, pleaded guilty to a charge of persistent cruelty to his wite.—Mr Steel, who represented the complain- ant, stated that the man should have appeared the previous day, but he did not turn up and the magistrates granted a warrant. Soon after the rising of the Court he was arrested, helplessly drunk. Tho parties were married 32 years ago and they had had 17 children. For about 15 years prisoner had not kept himself and for three and a half years he had not worked. He was con- stantly drunk and begged money from different persons. He took money from his children and had been cruel to his wife for the last eight or nine years. His ,language was mos:, disgusting and disgraceful. One of sons was a cripple and he had threatened end ill-treated him in a shameful way. He had called him a "hump- backed swine." He (Mr Steel) was not asking for a large order to be made against the defendant because all the wife wanted was quietness and she would accept -a nominal order of 5s per week —An order for 5s a week was made against the defendant who said he thought he had done a certain amount of work.—The 'Chief Constable: A certain amount. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair) Alder- men Smethurst and Bolton, Messrs S Wright, W Roberts, G Pilling and T Locker. ACTORS' WILFUL DAMAGE.—A somewhat interesting case was heard when Percy Braithwtite, a well-dressed actor, lodging at 27 Golborne-street was charged with having wilfully broken a plate glass window on Saturday, doing damage to the extent of £4 to the property of Herbert Braysbaw 27. King-street.—Prisoner pleaded not guilty and when witnesses had given evidence he cross- examined each at some length.—Several witnesses gave -evidence to the effect that prisoner was seen at 10.55, standing close to the window. A woman came up to him and after a short conversation she walked on about three yards. The prisoner then hurled a bottle through Mr Brayshaw's window. He went to Golborne-street afterwards and one witness Mrs Patrick Price, informed the police. Each witness said the prisoner had a trilby hat on.He, prisoner, denied this and said he was wearing cap.—lnspector Forster doposed to arresting prisoner at his lodgings at 11.20 p in. He told him that he was making inquiries regarding the breaking of the window in King-street. Braithwaite denied knowledge of King-street or Cambria-place. He also pretended to be drunk. When charged he repli.d " I know perfectly well what you are charging me with and there need be no mistake about that."—Mr Braystia w proved the extent of the damage and said he had no desire that the prisoner should go to prison. He would be willing to accept £2 to cover the damage. In answer to the prisoner he admitted that he bad never met him before and so prisoner could not hive had anything against him to cause him to do such a thing. —Prisoner submitted that the case was one of mistaken identity. He admitted in answer to a magistrate that he had bad five hot rums. He had taken them for a •cold. They would not affect him so much as to make him do such a "wild suffragette trick."—After prisoner's wife had given eviience as to his wearing a •cap and not a trilby and also that he had a muffler round the lower part of the face, the Bench ordered Braith- waite to pay the costs and £2 towards the damage—s total of £2 183 60.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. Before Coon H Roberts (in the chair) Messrs W ft Richardson, C Jackson Holmes and T Locker.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFTS.—On two charges of theft, Daniel 'Taylor, of no settled address, was remanded for a week in order that inquiries might be msee. It was alleged that he had stolen a vest, the property of Park and Bowden, Buttermarket-street, and a barrel of bone grease, value £7, the property of the Warrington Produce Co., Winwick-road.—The Chief Constable said that considerable strategy must have been used to get the barrel of grease away. The prisoner was seen rolling it along Winwick-road. When spoken to, he .said that a motor-lurry had broken down on the way to Earlestown, and the bArrel had to go to Earlestown. It was desirable that further inquiries should be made regarding the prisoner. Prisoner was remanded for a week.</p>
        <p>EMBEZZLEMENT CHARGE AGAINST TRAM CON- DUCTOR.— Nesley Horton, train conductor, 182,Padgete- lane, was charged on a warrant with having embezzled the sum of 2d, the monies of the Warrington 'Corporation. Mr A T liAllaway, deputy town clerk, prosecuted and Mr T S Steel defended.—Mr Hallaway stated that on Friday evening two olerks were instructed to travel by the tram which left Rylands- street at 8.20 for Sankey-Bridges. They boarded the tram at The%lis-streer, and purchased two tickets .numbered 0384 and 0385. In addition to making a note of the numbers they marked the tickets with pin pricks. On leaving the car at the terminus they placed the tickets on the seat. When the car returned to Warrington a checker examined the waybill and looked at the tickets which corresponded, but it wzis found that two tickets which had been issued on the outward journey had been used for the return journey. He understood that Mr Steel desired an adjournment, to which the prosecution offered no objection.—Detective Holland proved the arrest of Horton, who, he said. denied the cbarge.—Mr Steel applied for a week's .adjournment. He said Horton was only arrested the previous night, and he had not had an opportunity of going into the question of the defence. He also applied for bail.—Horton was remanded for a week, bail being allowed in a personal surety of £l.O and another surety for a similar amount.</p>
        <p>URSDAY.—Before Mr A J Jolley (in the chair) Mr J Upson and Councillor Locker. ALLEGED THEFT OF BOOTS.—John Gordon, 15, Stanley-street, was remanded for a week in connection with a charge of stealing from outside Messrs Briggs' shop, Market Gate, a pair of gents' boots, value 33. To THE DANGER OF THE PUBLlC.—William Roberts, 57, Wilderspool-road, was fined ss. for having driven a horse and float to the danger of the public at Bridge- foot on January Gth.—P.C. Corbett proved the case.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Smethurst and Bolton, Messrs S Wright, A J Jolley, Gi Pilling, P Locker and W Furness. WAS IT DESERTION ?—Eliza Rafter, 22, Barry.street, summoned her hus'nand Peter for desertion, and she applied for a separation order.—Mr Steel, who appeared for the complainant, said the parties were married 22 years ago, and there were five children, two of whom were earning wages. Defenchnt was employed by Messrs Fletcher, Russell and Co., and at the beginning</p>
        <p>best for old folk.</p>
        <p>Horlick's is Malted Barley, Wheat and Milk in Powder form—so rich in nourishment that invalids and the aged can live on it. Sustains through the day. Ensures sound sleep at night. Easily digested, economical and prepared in a moment. Recommended by Doc- tors, Nurses and Food Experts.</p>
        <p>A.</p>
        <p>116, 2/6 &amp; otall Chemists. Grocers &amp; Stores Write De/'t.. 98 Yor Free SamPle and Booklet HORLICK'S MALTED MILK Co., Slough, Bucks</p>
        <p>of November he left home to go to work for the firm at South Shield 9. Whilst away he sent home sums amount- ing to £3 10s. For the past six weeks the wife had received no money whatever from him.—Com plainant bore out this statement in cross-examination by Mr A Browne (who appeired for the defendant) admitted that she had sent scurrilous postcards to his employers at South Shields. She, however, denied reiusing defendant admittance to the nome when he came home in January.—P.C. Barr stated that the defendant came to him and asked him to go with him to the house as his wife had refused him admittance. . She opened the door in answer to the knock and a bundle of clothing was handed to the defendant. The door was then closed again.—Mr Browne submitted that there was no intention of desertion on the part. of the defendant. He went away by appointment and com- plainant saw him off at the station.—The magistrates dismissed the case and said the charge of desertion had not been proved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>LTED,MILR</head>
        <p>LTED,MILR</p>
        <p>111</p>
        <p>EXTENSIVE WORKS THEFTS.</p>
        <p>SMART SENTENCES AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>RECEIVERS PUNISHED.</p>
        <p>Three respectably dressed workmen appeared before Alderman Tinnion (in the chair) and other magistrates on Saturday morning, one in answer to a charge of stealing and the other two on charges of receiving goods which had been stolen from Messrs Crostleld's works. Their names were Tomas Lawton, labourer, 243, Knutsford-road, James Marshall, lithographer, 11, Alexander Terrace, Caester-road and Thomas Oswald, lithographer, 85, Ridgway-street, Manchester. Lawton was charged with stealing on the 17th January four bottles of perfume and a quantity of soap value ka 45., the property of Messrs Joseph Crosfield and Sons Ltd., and with stealing during the past six months a large quantity of st,ap, shaving sticks and perfume, value £2 7s. Marshall was charged with receiving the goods enumerated in the second charge and Oswald with receiving goods to the value of £1 45., knowing them to have been stolen. Lawton and Oswald pleaded guilty, but Marshall,who was represented by Mr T S Steel, pleaded not guilty—a plea which the Chief Cohstable characterised as wicked He (the Chief) wondered how ou'earth he could plead not guilty after the statemenai he had made to the police. Marshall was allowed to stand back and the case against the other two was proceeded with. The Chief Constable said that for some time past Messrs Crosfleld bad been missing scent and other articles, with the result that the watchmen bad been instructed to keep spicial observations. Dennis Hughes was instructed by the senior watchman, Flood, to keep watch in the Packing room the previous night. He concealed himself in the room and about 9.30 he saw the prisoner Lawton there, and asked him what he was doing. Lawton did not reply and he was taken to Mr Layton's office, where, on being searched,the bottles of scent were found in his possession. He readily admitted that be had taken them. He was taken into custody and from information he gave the police, Inspector Foster kept observation on the 7.2 a.m. train that morning from Manchester to Warrington, and Oswald was arrested in Winwick-street. He was told that Lawton was in custody and he straight ay admitted that he had received property from him. He said he knew perfectly well it must have been stolen,as the price he paid for it could not have led him to think anything else. It was a most serious case,indeed. It was monstrous for firms to be victimised like_ that. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- Oswald, who said he had only taken four articles, was fioed 303., including costs or 14 days' imprisonment. Lawton, who bad admitted at the police station that he had been stealing ever since he went to the firm, was mulcted in a fine of 50s. and ccsts in each case or in default two months' imprisonment. The case against Marshall was then proceeded with. After the case bad been outlined by the Chief Con- stable, Sergeant Reddington corroborated and stated that in consequence of Abet Lawton said the previous night they went to 'Marshall's house close on midnight and knocked him up. Witness cautioned him and told him Lawton was in custody for stealing from Messrs Crosfield's' and that he bad made a statement to the effect that he (Marshall) had been receiving some of the goods. Marshall repeatedly denied knowledge of the goods, and as the officers were going to searco the house, the wife came on the scene and said "Jim, shall I tell the truth ?" whereupon prisoner replied Keep your mouth shut." They advised him to tell the truth and his wife said "1 will tell." Marshall then said "1 paid for all I had." The wife took them to a wardrobe in the front bedroom, and there was found a great deal of the property. Other goods were discovered in diffsrent parts of the house in drawers. Some soap was concealed in a clothes box and one of the bottles of scent on the dressing table had et idently been used. At the police station Lawton made the following statement in Marshall's presence: "Yes I took the soap and other things because I have been short of money and because Mr. Marshall has been good to me. I tried to return it by getting him these things. Mr Marshall asked me to get him some soap. I did, and he gave me 2s. for it at Christmas. I also got him some scent for 2s. He has been a friend to me and he has been asking me to get soap and I thought that would be one way of paying him back, by letting him have it cheap. Several times I would not have taken the soap or scent unless I had been asked to. I have been doing it for years, ever since I have been at the soapery, and lam sorry for it." Marshall did not contradict that statement and when charged he replied : "rhe only thing I can say is that he came to me when he was short of money and I helpsd him as much as I could by giving him money before I got the goods. He brought them outside the works before he gave them to me." Lawton said" That is true, I took the goods twice to his house."</p>
        <p>Mr Steel for the defence slid the prisoner bad been employed for 14 years in the lithographing department at the works. It was a well-known fact that Mesirs Cradleld did let the workpeople have goods at a reduied price, when they were returned. It was not vnusull for soap to be found amongst clothes or in the wardrobe— in fact he (Mr Steel) had placed soap of his own along with clothes.</p>
        <p>The Chief: But you got yours in another way. They do not generally put "Perfection" with the clothes, but rather perfumed toilet soap. Continuing, Mr Steel said that Lawton had been borrowing money from Marshall from time t 3 time, and Marshall received the goods under the impression that Lawton had obtained them at a time when the employees were allowed to purchase them at a reduced rate.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable here pointed out that the bottles were not properly finished for sending out of the works.</p>
        <p>Mr Steel admitted that, like a good many more men, Marshall had denied knowledge to the police at first, but he was awakened late at night and if it had been in the daytime he would not have s%id the things he did.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that the prisoner had a wage of £2. 43 per week. The Ch.irman said the case was a very bad one indeed. They thouaht it high time that employers should be protected from such men as the prisoner. They would fine him £lO including costs, or in default two months' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>THEFTS .OF FLOUR.</head>
        <p>THEFTS .OF FLOUR.</p>
        <p>More cases of works thefts were hesrd at the Police Court on Wednesday when the presiding justices were Mr J W Hayward (in the caair) and Mr C Jackson Holmes.</p>
        <p>John William Jackson (labourer), 60, Hume-street ; Samuel Miilington (labourer), 7, Fountain-street ; Joseph Horace Barlow (labourer), Mottrarn's-yard, Scotland-road, and Jonathan Broanhurst, greengrocer, 61, Bostock-street, were charged with stealing on the 16th January from the Bank Quay Four Mills two bags of thirds flour, value 255, the property of Messrs James Fairclough and Sons Ltd. AU the prisoners pleaded guilty, Millington and Barlow to one sack each. Chief Inspector Williamson stated tbat the three men were employed as mill hands and the defendant Broad- hurst was a greengrocei in Bestock-street. For some considerable time Messrs Faiiclough or their agents had been victimised by extensive thefts through their workpeople. Some weess ago the police obtained information as to the methods by which those thefts had been carried out.</p>
        <p>The prisoner Barlow, at this juncture fainted in the dock and after a short delay be was removed to a seat near the defendants box, two officers being in attend- ance all the time. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Continuing the Chief Inspector said the police were successful in obtaining definite information as regarded the methods by which the thefts were carried out and Detectives Holland, Summers and Craig, were employed watching the mills and the comings and goings of carts to the mills. Between 12 o'clock and 1 o'clock on the day in question, Broadhurst ordered from the manager five bags of husks at is. per bag„ and one hag of bran at ss, making a total sum of 10s. He paid this sum and got a receipt for it. He promised to came for the sacks at 4 o'clock with his cart. Detectives Summers and Craig kept observa- tions and saw Broadhurit arrive, load his cart, come out again and drive away. He had never left the cart and Summers followed the cart, which was travelling rather rapidly, and saw in it, not six bags but seven Broadhurst whipped up the horse but eventually* Summers caught up to him. Broadhurst said he had six bags on the cart and added that, if there were seven, there had been a mistake. On examination it was found that the cart contained not five bags of husks and one of bran, but one bag of bran, four of husks and two of thirds, the latter being valued at 12s 6d each, whereas the total value of goods in the cart should only have been 108. Broadhurst was captioned and said he knew nothing about it. He said be was in the Ship Inn when the cart was being loaded. It was pointed out to him that that was not so, but he still persisted that he had been in the Ship Inn all the time. He Slid he had 103 t the receipt for the 10s., which he had paid for husks and bran. It was</p>
        <p>discovered that the men Millington and Jackson had loaded the cart hut they said they had put five bags of husks and one of bran into it. Tney were charged and Jackson replied "I say I was not aware of the first bag" (that was the bag of thirds). Millington answered the charge with' both of us shoved thirds in instead of five hags of husks ana one of bran which were ordered." Broadhurst replied "I am saying nothing." On the way to the cells Millington said' I expected to het my share of 53. from Jackson." As a result of further informatioa,Detectives Reddington and Holland visited Littons Mill and saw the prisoner Barlow. They cautioned him arid told him the/ bad information about him having aided in the stealing of two sacks of thirds. He denied said be was chaining waggons at the other end of the mill. He was .brought up to the Police Station and on being confronted with the other men, admitted that. he had put one sack on the lorry, When charged he said "I am innocent of one bast. 1 put one on and I expected to get my share out of the money." Millington said be was ignorant of one bag. 'ln concluding his statement the Inspector said they were bound to point out that the thefts had been exten- sive and very serious indeed,wita the result that Messrs Fairclough had suffered very severely. The police had had to be especially alert. as the thefts were carried out in such an adroit manner. Ihe firm wanted the case to he a warning and a lesson. Mr Daintith, vho appeared as a representative of the company, said he bad been in charge of the mill for 20 years and during the whole of that time,men had always taken goods and it had caused the firm a treat deal of anxiety. The prisoners had been very good workmen, but they had to overlook that fact because the robberies had been so extensive that it was about time a stop was put to them. Jackson bad been witts the firm 'two years, Millington five years and Barlow 12 months. He bad to dismiss a man not long ago for thieving, but evidently the prisoners did not take notice Jackson said he was sorry it had happened, Millington said the same and appealed for the mercy of the court, as he was married and had five caildren. Barlow murmured that the Bench could do as they liked with him.</p>
        <p>Mr T S Steel, who appeared for Broadhurst pointed out that the mmthdds ad3pted by the workmen did not affect Broadhurst. The defendant was 53 years of age and he was not a very strong man. He had been willing to help the police and he had promised that for the future that sort of work would be ended, lie also undertook that his business should be conducted on straight and proper lines. The Chief Int.p,.ctor said Broadhurst had been before the court eigut times for cruelty to a horse and driving vehicles without lights. In August 1911 he appeareloti two charges of receiving stolen br tss and was sent to prison for three months with hard labour on each charge,. _ - -</p>
        <p>Tlie Chief Constable said the police raised no objec- tion to a fine, so long as it was th-) maximum. Broad- hurst hsd giveu the police informstion which had been of use to them.</p>
        <p>The Bench retired and on their return Broadhurst was fined £2O or two rnlnths imprisonment, and toe other defendants were fined 693 each, or one ci uth imprison- ment.</p>
        <p>At the same court EiPnry Tra'arer. 55, Aiken-3treet, was, along with Jackson and Birlo 07 charged with stealing some time during December, one bag of thins value 12s 6d. the property of Messrs Fairclough. The Cuief Constahlo imimatei to the bench that in view of Barlow's condition in Court, Messrs Fairclough were desirous tbat the charge against him should he withdrawn.</p>
        <p>The bench agreed to this curse. Chief Inspector Williamson stated that it clme to the knowledge of the police whilst they were making inquiries in regard to the first charge that this theft had been committed. Tranter had paid 7s for the bag and when brought t) the Police Station stated that he had paid for wnat he had had. When confronted with the other man he said he was sorry he had had the flour. He w.s charged along with Jackson and th-, latter replied" We were both together and received 7s from Tranter. We shared the money." Tranter said "I did not steal it but I did receive one bag." Jackson said that if the Bench w mid give him another chance it would be better. Birlow was as much in it as he was, and he (the pris) r) had a wife and child to keep. la Mr Steel appeared for Tranter and said that prisoner had often been asked before to have the bags, but he had always refused. Unfortunately he had no tongue, and it was difficult to understand all be said. He had given way to this temptation because it had been pat to him over and over again. He was 63 years of age, and struggled through life with a few pigs with which he had to maintain himself, his wife and family. The Caief Cliastable pointed out that Messrs Fairclough had only witlicTrawn the charge against Barlow on account of his condition in curt, not that the offence should be minimised in the least. The Chairman : We quite understand. Jackson was fined 20s or 14 days imprisonment, and Tranter was mulcted in a fine of £5, or in default one month's imprisonment. Jackson made application for time in which to pay the fines, but before he got down the status of the dock he said, "I will not have to see thechief. I have got money to pay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BURDENED NV/OMEN.</head>
        <p>BURDENED NV/OMEN.</p>
        <p>Lack-lustre ues. with dark rings, sallow cheeks, wrinkled brow and tatight-d, awn lips that seldom relax, are signs which brand women as sufferers from those ailments which in a greater or lesser degree trouble most women from girlhood to middle age. Yet there is no reason why any woman should continue to suffer. Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Com- pound, a woman's medicine fr women's ailments, acts in harmony with Nature, restoring and preserving health. When those awful pains come in the back and sides, with agonising headaches and a sense of fore- boding, this herbal remedy is worth its weight in gold. A woman, once a great sufferer, has written "I have been in so much pain that I have not known where to put myself. I used to think I should gds out of my mind. One night, when I was very bad, my husband went to the chemist's, and bought me a bottle of Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound. After three doses I felt an improvement, I have only taken three bottles, and I feel all right now." The Lady Manager, The Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a, Hampstead Road, London, will send Mrs. Lydia E. Pinkbam's Vegetable Compound for 2s. 9d., or a very large bottle for 4s. 6d. She will also send tree a private book to women.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>LAY DEAD IN A PLAT FOR A McNTII.</head>
        <p>LAY DEAD IN A PLAT FOR A McNTII.</p>
        <p>Evidence at the inquest on Mr Thomas G Carvill, aged 38, on Wednesday, showed that he had probably lain dead in the flat at Cio,mwell-avenue,Hammersmitb, in which he lived alone,since Christmas Eve. This was the date of a newspaper found in his pocket, and the earliest date among the pile of letters on the doormat. His father, Mr P G Carvill, a barrister, of 105, Pall Mall, said his son had been invalided from the consular service. The jury found that death was due to rupture of a blood vessel in the brain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>DAY ON A MAN-OF-WAB</head>
        <p>A DAY ON A MAN-OF-WAB</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGHT.I</p>
        <p>The modern warship is a triumph of nava! Architecture. With the impregnability of a • floating ~steel fort to the terrible projectiles of to-day has to be combined the mobility and the carrying of a huge cargo of coals for .the powerful machinery. And although the machinery is of the most varied and ingenious description, applied as it is not only to the pro- pulsion and steering of the huge fabric, but also to the heating, lighting, and ventilating; to the moving of the big guns, and the hoisting of the projectiles; and to a number of other useful and labour-saving purposes, yet over and above all this, the personnel of the ship is as numerous and of a far more varied description than in the aivs of the good old wooden walls of England. While it is with the officers and crew of a man-of-war that we are now more directly co.n- cerned, it is necessary to premise any descrip- tion of the daily routine with some account of the discipline and organisation which exists on a modern ship of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>YESTERDAY AND TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>YESTERDAY AND TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>In the good old days when a ship was put intc commission the captain was placed in charge of the bare hull and masts, with officers and war- rant officers. He had to enlist the crew, and rig and equip, clean and paint the ship—a process, which while it was excellent training, often occupied a period of three months. Such leisurely methods are not possible nowadays. Most of ie navies of the great Powers have fleet yeserves, consisting of ships prepared and ready for Service, but usually laid up in one or other of the naval dockyards. With the excep- tion of ammunition and perishable victuals they are fully equipped. They are in charge of skeleton crews, and are kept clean and in good trim, needing only their full complement of men, etc., to be ready for sea. To-day when a man-of-war is commissioned, the senior officers on half-pay usually come direct from their homes, the- younger officers from the school-ships, etc. The trained men are drafted from the gunnery ships, and the torpedo men from the school-ships. The "steam re- serves" of the port supply the engine-room artificers an stokers. Other bluejackets come from the guardship or from barracks. The marines are sent on board in a body from their divisions. As a result of this detailed organisa- tion a warship can be got ready for sea nowadays in twenty-four hours.</p>
        <p>• Nor does this highly organised discipline exist only before commissioning. On the contrary, it may be said only to have begun—the modern war vessel in its personnel is a model of organisa. tion and discipline.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>WATCH.</head>
        <p>THE WATCH.</p>
        <p>In the British Navy every vessel, from a second-class cruiser upw'ards, has a staff con- sisting of at least a captain, executive officer, navigating lieutenant, gunnery lieutenant, and torpedo expert. The executive.officer points out to every man his post, 'and instructs him as to his duties. This is done by the distribution of cards. What is known as the "Watch Bill " forms the basis for the organisation of service. The crew is, of course, divided, as of old, into starboard and port watches, who work four hours on deck by turns. To obviate the same men having the same watches every day, a daily alternation is effected by dividing the day into seven watches by breaking up the time between four and eight p.m. into the "first dog watch" from four to six and the "second dog watch " from six to eight p.m. Each member of the crew is assigned a number—the starboard watch being odd and the port even numbers. By these numbers they are consigned to their posts, their sleeping place and their hammocks, and their places at the mess tables. Each watch is divided into halves and quarters, so that if any work has to be clone, all, or part, can be called on deck. Marines, artificers, stokers and cooks remain in compact bodies in the watch bills, have separate messes and sleeping places, and are exempt from watches. The numbers of the sleeping places are written up near the hammock hooks, which are fixed in the beams. Every square foot of space has to be utilised, and " 'tween decks" the hammocks are very crowded—only 15 to 18 inches each of space being allowed. The place gets very close in bad weather when hatches are bat- tened down—albeit one watch remains on deck.' Boatswain's hammocks are slung near the hatch- ways so that they can rouse out their men quickly and get up on deck. THE DAY BEGINS..</p>
        <p>The. bluejacket's day may be said to begin at 4-30 a.m., when, according to regulations, the watch on deck scrub and wash the decks to that state of spotlessness usually so much remarked by the fair sex when they visit an ironclad. At six o'clock the watch below and the "day men" —i.e., the members of the ship's complement who are not on the watch bill—lash or tie up the hammocks. The latter are stowed away, and the mess tables, which are movable and are ti-iced up or suspended between the beams, are lowered. The tables are also occupied as far as possible in accordance with the watch bill. The men may, within certain limits, choose their own mess, but each mess must be equally divided between the two watches, and the day men must be equally distributed as directed by the master- at-arms,</p>
        <p>The first meal of the day is breakfast at-6-3Q Nominally it 'consists of cocoa and bread, but this scanty fare is amplified both from the men's allowance under the scale of victualling and from delicacies, purchased from the common fund of the mess,' The mess, it may be ex- plained, consists .of from 12 to 18 men. Every mess has its caterer and its "cook," who is really equivalent to an orderly in the army. The caterer receives ,the- savings 3f the mess monthly from the pay master, and either spends it in delicacies or extras, or saves it all up and distributes the total amount when the ship is -paid off. Tialialiv. 'however_ there is great</p>
        <p>From Reynolds' Newspaper.</p>
        <p>emulation among the differerit messes, and the men take great pride in keeping their mess in fine style. After breakfast the men are piped to quarters to clean guns, wood, brass, and so forth. The watch below clean 'tween decks— particularly where the men have breakfasted.</p>
        <p>THE DAY'S WORK.</p>
        <p>At eight o'clock the colours are hoisted and 1 toe crew paraded, that is, they all stand to at- tention and salute. Half-an-hour later the members of the crew change their clothes for those used in the daytime, and at nine o'clock they are mustered for prayers, which are .con- ducted by the captain in the presence of the whole crew. Then the watch falls in for exer- cise and 'morning drills. Dinner comes along at noon precisely. While in port the daily menu consists of fresh meat, soup and vegetables, and once a week, by way of a change, salt pork and pea soup are provided. At sea, on the other hand, the changes have •to be rung on the dietary. Salt beef and plum pudding are alternated with salt pork and pea soup, except on one day when preserved meat and preserved potatoes are served. Post-prandial satisfaction is increased by the subsequent issue of grog, which consists of half of ruin and half of water, and permission to smoke and general "stand easy" with pipes and smoking lanterns. Work is resumed at 1-15. Guns are cleaned and mess decks cleared up, after which the watch fall in for afternoon drill. Then at 4-30 comes tea or "supper," as it is called in the Navy, consisting of tea and bread. This is the last meal of the day, and the long fast till next morning was for long a grievance with the Jack Tar, but it has latterly been remedied. After tea and a smoke the men clean arms, and at 5-10 are mustered to coil down ropes and hoist boats. Soon afterwards clothes are changed and stowed away, hammocks are let down, and after a final smoke the watch below turn in before rounds at nine o'clock. In the tropics the cooler hours of the morning and evening are made more use of, and the mid-day interval of rest is cor- respondingly lengthened. As regards the scale of victualling, the sea- man's full allowance is: Daily: Biscuit IPbs. or soft bread 111bs., spirits pint, sugar 20z., chocolate lox., tea 31b., and, when procurable, fresh meat 11b., and vegetables ?ilb. There are weekly allowances of oatmeal, •salt beef, condi- ments and other things too numerous to men- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ROUTINE AND RELAXATION,</head>
        <p>ROUTINE AND RELAXATION,</p>
        <p>Even when in harbour the diurnal and weekly routine is maintained. , There is a. regular rotation of duties for each daS in tha week. To' take only a few random instances: on Monday morning there is "general drill, prepare for action and rig out torpedo nets." On Wed- nesday afternoon "cutlass exercise, inspection of mines, electric cables and circuits of guns, midshipmen and boys at gun, small-arm and cutlass drill." Then next morning the regula- tions prescribe "exercise landing parties, ashore if possible, drill in passing up ammunition, test hydraulic machinery, muster clothes and bed- ding." And then on Saturday is the brief but imperative week-end order, "clean 'ship." In some harbours, however, when the discipline is somewhat relaxed, the sailor's lot is at in pleasant places. Admiral Freemantle says in his autobiography: "Hong Kong is a gay place in the winter season, when the weather is Pleasant enough, the racecourse, golf links, cricket and lawn tennis, with even occasional polo, all have their attractions; while the beau- tiful semi-tropical foliage in the Botanical Gardens, in the cemetery at Happy Valley and elsewhere, make it pleasant to the eye, and to a sailor living on board it has the inestimable advantage of being a secure harbour where his ship lies close to the shore." Taking it all round, if the sailor's "lot is not happy one," it has, to set against the unvary- ing official routine, the ever-changing Vistas of iea and sky. And even in these days of gun- aristling "Dreadnoughts," of sinister sub- marines, and of forty-knot torpedo-destroyers which are semi-submarines, Jack Tar is still the same sturdy, jolly, happy-go- lucky fellow laughing at hardship and mjoying. life afloat or ashore, as he was in the lays of Nelson.</p>
        <p>FOR THE WiAc OF ODER MEANS</p>
        <p>4 6 BAYARD " as</p>
        <p>8 H.P. 4 Cylinder (GO x 120) With Two Seater Torpedo Body, Speed to 40 miles per hour, Price £197 10 H.P. 4 Seater £241.</p>
        <p>4 4 PALLADIUM " Gars</p>
        <p>1018 H.P. 4 Cylinder (65 x 130) with High Class Torpedo 3 Seater Body, Lamps, Horn, Tools etc. £250. Ditto with 4 Seater Body £271. 15/18 H.P. 4 Cylinder (83 x 140) with Hood, Screen, 5 Lamps, Horn, Tools etc. 2 Seater £225, 4 Seater £235</p>
