      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>LEATHER TRADE IN WARRINGTON AND</head>
        <p>THE LEATHER TRADE IN WARRINGTON AND</p>
        <p>DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr S. Hassall.</p>
        <p>There was last week a constant run of orders coming along from day to day, and requests for contract delivery were urgent and frequent, so that the month already is making a very fair showing, and the tanner's greatest grievance is that his goods are not coming along quickly enough. The advanced prices seem to be getting firmly established now, and I have been assured by one or two tanners that they have sold nothing since the end of the first week in January except at an advance on December rates. Bends appear to have moved v.ilh the leas' difficulty, and going, as they generally do, in comparatively small lots, it has been fairly easy to make the further fraction. Stooks appear to be very small on all hands, and there is really very little to sell, which may explain a remark overheard at Leeds Fair last week, that " tanners must have wanted a day out, for they won't sell any leather." Local firms are exceedingly nusy,and anticipate a steady continuance of the demand, for I hear privately that some very extensive purchases of raw stock is going on ; most expect the hide market to advance strongly very shortly. Very sincere grief is feit at the untimely death of Mr Sam Hassell, of the Highfield T-nuin.g Company,the whole cir umstences being so sad of a young man of such ability and promise. being taken off so sudden!:. He was connected with several social and musical institu- tions in Warrington, and the funeral, on Saturday, was attended by severe! hundred sympathisers, including Mr 11, H Posnett, and the staff and workmen from Highfield, representatives from the Union Tanneries, John H Fleming and Co., Ltd. (where Mr Hassell received his early training), and from several other tanneries.</p>
        <p>SOLE LEAEHEIL—Benda all round have had a very brisk stle ; the demand for secondary and thirds English has been urgent, and for better qualities it has been satisfactory. Continentals have had a good sale generally, though the demand for extra heavy weights seems to have quietened a little. Foreign bends of all classes have moved very quickly, with a very keen enquiry for " off "and damaged lines. Shoulders are still very scarce stock, as for all classes the demand is brisk, and bellies move off just as quickly as ready.— From the" Leather World.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>HUED IN THE POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>HUED IN THE POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>MAGISTRATE : Tbe ulaintiff says you struck him without justification.</p>
        <p>DEFENDANT: I plead "Not guilty," your Worships. We were having a chat about clothes, and I was willing to wager that the suit I am now wearing cost less than half what he paid for his, and fits me twice as well. He took my bet, and when I told him I had it made to measure for 12/11 be called me a liar.</p>
        <p>MAGISTRATE: You then knocked hitn down? DEFENDANT: That is so; he doubted my word. MAGISTRATE : From my own experienced I eould not get a suit that would fit so well under four guineas. MAGISTRATES' CLERK: Where did you have it made?</p>
        <p>DEFENDANT: At Messrs. " Suits" Ltd., Horsemarket Street, Warrington. They are clearing out all last year's suitings at below cost to make way for their new 1913 goods. They say it pays them batter to do so than to carry old stock.</p>
        <p>MAG ISTDATE (after consulting with several in the court): The plaintiff's error :s not to be wondered at: but we find that the defendant spoke the truth,as there are several members in the court each of Whom state that they are wearing suits which Messrs. " Suits " Ltd. made for them at 12/11. We think that our local firm, Messrs. " Suits " Ltd. must be very enterprising and worthy of everyone's custom. You are discharged, defendant. DEFENDANT: Thank you, sir."</p>
        <p>(LATER). As a solatium to " SUITS " LTD. for having had to bring their name into court as evidence, the defendant took all his friends • to their establishment, which is situated at Jubilee Buildings, Horsemarket-street, War- rington, to order 12/11 suits, with which, we understand, they are delighted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>CHARWOMEN'S EMPLOYERS</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Mondsy Viscount Wolmer (Newton, C) asked the Caancellor of the Exchequer whether his attention bad been called to the fact that Government inspectors are demanding that employers of charwomen, aged sixty-seven, who are employed for four hours every day and paid at the rate of 12s. per week, should fix stamps on their insurance cards to the value of sd. or 6cl. per week instead of 4d. as is provided by the National Insurance Act ; and whether the Insurance Commissioners have issued any new regulations on the subject,. Mr .Nlasterrnan : Section 19 of the Act requires the same rates of contribution to be paid in respect of persons of the age mentioned as for en ployed con- tributors of other ages. Where under the provisions altering the rates of contrhutions in respect of low- paid employees the employer ply's sd. and GA. a week, the employed person pays nothing. The rates are laid down in the Act, and are not determined by regulation of the Insurance Commissioners.</p>
        <p>BiIIMARRIAGES nt htehe Houscoursee IN of ff Commons theDISESTABLISHEDom mon debate s-0-onio ntiae C yWelshLon r dUR wCCoh l. ur Dr ce hr (Newton, C) said that the matter of marriages after the Act in the churches could easily he settled by a new clause or sub-section. The whole discussion showed the absolute impossibility of turning the Church into a voluntary association (" Why "?) This clause under dis- cussion proved that the Government themselves recognised that. It was wholly 'objectionable from beginning to end. It was a perfectly monstrous provision that any man who chose to call himself a Churchman, and desired to contract a marriage with a divorced person or a Jew or Mahomniedan should have the right to demand that tbe marriage should be solemnised in the Church of England. The contention that the judgment that Holy Communion could not be refused to a man who had married his deceased wife's sister would be binding on the disestablished Church had not been answered. The Church would be left with an Erastian marriage lasw which she wholly repudiated. Until she could make new rules for herself she should not be left in that position. If Clause 3 were left by itself the Church would have its proper liberty, but this clause withheld it. He must impress upon hon. members opposite, although they had spoken very conciliatory words that afternoon, that they did not seem to realize what they were involved in when they once set out on the career of freeing the Church. The Church must have the power to settle her marriage laws, and by the Church in this sense he meant the clergy, for the laity-had no power to settle the doctrines of the Church. Hon. members opposite were really enabling the Church to become what she , as in the middle ages—the most powerful body in the land, a sort of imperium in imperio, wielding a spiritual control over the subjects of the kingdom without any possible interference by the State. He thought the bill ought to give to the Church the power to return to her old law with regard to a person who had married his deceased wile's sister.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL BENEFIT. Those of cur kcal doctors who are on the panel for State Insurance prirposesnave for many days been inundated with those pink tickets the significance of which is now so well understood by our readers. Secretaries of approved sccieties under the Act were very glad indeed to see the intimation in the last issue of the " Examiner" that insured persons who had not already complied with the conditions should forthwith decide upon their medical man and get their tick ts signed by him without delay. These officials are just now exceedingly busy and have many perplex;t es, but, judging by the progress made during the week, order is gradually being evoked out of chaos, and all will very soon be working smoothly and satisfactorily. As t ) the indispensable tickets by-the-bye, those who, in the belief that it will do when they need the benefit, continue to neglect to make choice of a doctor, will ere long have a medical man allotted to them by the Insurance Committee. BREWSTER SESSIONS. The date of the general annual licensing meetieg draws nigh, and the Chief Constable will soon pre- sent to the justices his report as to the conditions amid which the law respecting such matters is administered in Warrington, One might pretty confidently antici- pate the contents of that document in the main. In the section dealing with convictions for drunkenness and proceedings generally in relation to the Licensing Acts, the figures necessarily vary yew by year; but the state of things so far as clubs, public-houses, , are concerned has not greatly altered during the year. Mention will of course be made of any intended applications; but as to the report as a whole,interested readers need not expect anything very sensational. A VEXED QUESTION. I just now incidentally alluded to clubs. So far as these are concsrned the publicans cherish a grievance. They view with alarm " the constant reduction of licences arid never-ceasing attacks upon our trade, whilst clubs are allo sed to increase and flourish." One points out that to obtain a licence ho must "possess such a character that even a clergyman might be proud of it." He must not permit gambling nor serve any poison under the influence of drink ; in short, he must conduct his house in a strictly moral manner. He goes on to declare that 50 per cent. of the existing clubs are " nothing less than drinking booths and gambling hells ! " BOGUS CLUBS. Now in such times as the present that is surely a hard saying. The tied club, which is so objectionable a feature of some communities, is rather the exception over a wide radius from Warrington, and the bogus clubs formerly so common in Lancashire, have been largely driven out of existence as the result of legis- lation along the lines of licensing reform. During last year's House of Commons debate upon Sir Alfred Gelder's Bill to amend the law relating to licensed clubs, it was stated that there were no fewer than 7,600 such institutions. Of these mmy clubs at which intoxicating liquor is sold, there are GOO Liberal as against 2,000 Conservative, the remainder being social clubs pretty evenly divided between the Upper, middle, and working classes. There has been a marked increase in the number of these, what time the reduction in public-houses was being effected, and it cannot be denied that some of those thus established are mere drinking places and not legitimate clubs. Some time prior to the debate Just referred to, strong support was given by the Chief Constable of Manchester, to the publicans' contention that drunkenness is due more to clubs than to licensed houses. Speaking from his own experi- ence, be did not hesitate to declare that clubs are really the cause of a large number of the cases of druekenness. In one such which he had raided in Manchester, any female could be a member for 2d a week, and any man for 31 a week. There were three dances a week, and one of them was held on Sunday afternoon. The p int I have to make of this is that such scandals would be practically impossible in this district. THE GENUINE ARTICLE. Take, on the other hand, any well conducted Iceal Workingmen's Club and lestitute. Most 9f ns know more or less intimately at least one such which systematically ministers,not only lo rational recreation and social enjoyment, but to the intellectual development and geeeral education of members I write not of those which are affliliated With and loyal to a tinier' which came into existence more than half a century ag ). As those who are able to recall the the reports of the celebration of the jubilee will be aware, the movement was started by the Rev. Henry Sony, a Christian minister, with the object of securing better meeting places for workers than the public-houses then existing. A couple of philanthropic Peers took part in the first meeting ; an Archbishop and a Bishop gave the scheme their benediction; and among prominent supporters were Queen Victoria, King Edward (then Prince of Wales of course) Professors Tyndall and lluxley,LordsFredk. Cavendish and Rosebery, Sir &amp;afford Northccte and Sir Charles Dilke. There are now affiliated with the Union some 1,500 clubs, with a membership of well- nigh half a million convalescent homes, and scholar- ships tenable at Ruskin College, Oxford. Several of those associated with the movement are naturally Proud of all this, and the conaiction that they are engaged in good and useful work in the public interest inspires and helps them in their honest and sincere endeavours to promote the welfare of their fellows. LYMII WATER QUESTION. lam not surprised to bear that, notwithstanding the practically unanswerable case made out as to this and the endorsement of the promoters' policy hy a majority of those attending the ratepayers' meeting, there are malcontents who will press to the utmost their opp ,sition to the project. I am very sorry that it should be so, but it quite accords with me experience of districts in various parts of the country. I have especially in mind the case of a Lancashire community who long depended upon a private company for their water supply, and were ultimately inaposition to acquire the works on unexpectedly and exceptionally faysurable terms, but the whole scheme Was upset by an indiecretion on the part of a small ratepayer who happened to object to the proposed arrangement. The woiks actually passed into the hands of another Corporation, and within a few sears the People thus foolishly deprived of it were obliged to Promote a much more costly scheme on their own account. The irony of the situation was the more keenly felt by reason of the fact that by that time the solitary opponent had removed to a distant .town in Yorkshire. One can only trust that in the case of I ymm wise counsels may yet prevail. WHAT A POLL INVOLVES. Is not a poll of the ratepayers a more formidable undertaking, or at all events a much more expensive 12usiness than was the case a few years ago ? As I nave previously taken occasion to explain, I spent many years in Halifax, in the West Riding of York- shire. The municipal authorities there are likewise Producing a Parliamentary Bill and a handful of ratepayers in that borough have demanded a poll, which has been fixed for to-day, between the hours of noon and 8 p.m. A dozen years ago, when similar Fircumstances arose,it was a simple matter of distribut- ing and collecting vofing papers ; but under the 1903 ?ict the opinion of the ratepayers must be ascertained by ballot, and it will lie necessary, as at a general municipal election, to establish polling booths in every ward, where householders and others on the register (including property owners who may be non-resident) can attend and register their vote! POSSIBLE FATE OF THE DINGLE. The Suggestion that, in a certain possible event which surely most of those concerned will strongly deprecate, the Dingle and lake might be turned into a 711.1 gar resort on the lines of the ordinary pleasure tfhair, reminded me of an incident which goes to show at it is by no means a far-fetched idea or an ,u,nreasonable argument that a scenic railway there o uld be Popular with a certain class. I was once revelling in one of those scenes with which in some of her moods Dame Nature ravishes the eye of the eholder who has taken the trpuble to rise betimes o climb one of the slopes of Windermere. The solitary worshipper indescribably lovely, at her shrine found the spectacle and lingered to enjoy it. His reverie was interrupted by a great hubbub of human voices that seemed a desecration. The noise was made by a party of young men and women Who had come on a day excursion in evident anticipa- -</p>
