      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <p>, . 1</p>
        <p>ICHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>THE MOST USEFUL TREE OF ALL.</p>
        <p>Last week I wrote a few lines on remark- able trees and promiseA to write a little this week upon the tree that seems to be the most useful of all.</p>
        <p>Probably the most useful tree in the world is the cocoanut palm, for every portion of it is put to good use. Let me mention very briefly the uses to which it is put. The trunk is used for building houses, for making furniture and farm implements and countless other articles; hollowed out it makes a canoe Its leaves are used for thatching, the leaf stalks for paddles and fishing lines. The blossom in bud makes preserves and pickles, besides serving as a staple vegetable. From the pith of the trunk is derived a sugar and vinegar. The ripe cocoanut is a valuable article of diet. The white kernel produces a delicious cream, a good substitute for meal, while cocoanut oil is used as a lubricant, for soap and candle making. It is also applied to counteract the stings of scorpions. The refuse of the oil, or oil cake, is valu- able as food for animals and poultry, and as fertilizer for the soil. From the shell, drinking cups, spoons, lamps, bottles, fire- wood, and even tooth powder are obtained. The husk supplies fibre for matresses and cushions, brushes and mats, ropes, cables, nets and even the harness for bullocks. The web sustaining the foot-stalks is made into strainers and torches. And last of all the tree acts as a conductor in protecting houses from lightning. Now can any other tree equal that? WINTER QUARTERS. Where's the crawling caterpillar? Sound asleep in his cocoon. Where's the bee, so bright and busy? Dreaming in the hive of June. Where's the snail and where's the turtle? Safely buried in the ground. Where's the woodchuck, where's the rabbit? In their burrows they are found. Where's the thrush and where's the robin ? Singing 'neath the S.uthern sky. Where's the bear and where's the squirrel ? In their hollow tree they lie. Where's the ant, that careful worker? In her underground abode. Where's the eighty-eyed spinning spider? In a crevice snugly st,,wed. Where's the bat that ranged at midnight? He is in his winter's sleep— In his cave he hangs head downward, And he Lever takes a peep. These and many other creatures Hide or drowse the winter through ; But when spring has once awakened, They are up and stirring, too. —SustE M. BEST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>DON'T NEGLECT THAT COLD.</head>
        <p>DON'T NEGLECT THAT COLD.</p>
        <p>Grave Chest Trouble _ Alone Prevented by</p>
        <p>PEPS.</p>
        <p>A cold on the chest cr in any part of the delicate air-passages between the mouth and lungs is distress- ing and even dangerous. if neglected it works its way to every unsuspected weak spot in the hum,in flame.</p>
        <p>Hence the danger of neglecting that slight hoarse- ness, that dry tickling cough, and that feeling of heat and soreness about the chest. If it is the membrane lining the passage from the throat to the lungs which is affected, we get bronchitis, while in this uncertain climate pneumonia and pleurisy are liable to make the weak-chested individual their prey. It is the stitch in time that saves nine; and for dealing with neglected colds and utterly routing all dangerous complications, Peps possess a special virtue not shared by any of the old-style cough medicines. Peps are the response of science to the universal and long-felt need for a cough remedy capable of treating direct the real seat of disease in the inner vissages of the throat and chest. As is well-known, no liquid can enter the lungs direct, but must pass straight down the gullet into the stomach, and there is no pissage between the stomach and the breathing organs in the chest. Hence liquid cough mixtures and the solution of cheap lozenges cannot be really effective cures for throat and chest troubles. Peps, on the contrary, contain certain rare medicincs which are set free in the form of air as the tablet dissolves in the mouth, and can then be breathed down the throat into the chest. Soreness is quickly allayed by this direct Pep, treatment; inflaination is lessened arid soon ended, phlegm easily expelled, coughing stopped, ard the weakness caused by the bronchial or chest trouble is repaired. Another advantage Peps have is that they are entirely free from all traces of opium,chloral,morphia laudanum, and all the other dangerous and nerve- depressing drugs which are in most ordinary lozenges and liquid ccugh-mixtures. Peps are not a useless medicated lozenge or jujube. Peps are a safe as well as a unique remedy, suitable alike for the aged and young children. Get a box of Peps to repair the " weak spot" in your chest, and then keep these wonderful breathable tablets handy as a safeguard and as a cure. But be sure ycii. get Peps. Watch for the name" PEP." cn the box when bu,), ing, and stamped on every tablet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>IIOW TO IMPROVE YOUR LAWN.</head>
        <p>IIOW TO IMPROVE YOUR LAWN.</p>
        <p>It is a fact that lawns usually present a better appearance in January and February than later on when the cold east winds prevail. The grass very rapidly recovers its freshness, however, when the winds became warm and showers of rain fall. Many lawns are improved by surface-dressings of rotted mspure and such material as read scrapings, which contain plenty of grit. Everyone cannot procure EGch material, but they can purchase some bone-meal, and this is one of the best and most lasting of manures for a lawn. The latter should be well brushed with a half-worn broom on a dry day; then sow the bone-meal evenly applying it at the rate of 4oz. per square yard, afterwards well rolling the grass. Do not again roll or brush the lawn until a gooa shower of rain bas fallen ; then roll several times, taking the roller in different directions each time before the surface gets quite dry again. After an application of bone-meal the grass grows freely, but not coarse ; indeed the bone-meal induces a much finer growth. This surface-dressing of bone-meal must be put on before the middle of February.—" The Garden."</p>
        <p>1F cutting his teeth makes your child fretful, get a Steeciman's Powder for him. This medicine contains no poison or narcotic, but relieves feverish heat and preserves a healthy state of the constitution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>Pope broke the spell and the pears were gathered. This festival lasted until 1810.</p>
        <p>JACK AS AN ECCLESIASTICAL HERO.</p>
        <p>As the feast fell on September 18th, it is very probable that the story of Jack and the pears was regarded as baying some bearing upon the fruit liarvest and that it was intended to remove any evil spell from the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>'ANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS.</head>
        <p>'ANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS.</p>
        <p>THE FAMOUS "HOUSE THAT JACK BUILT"</p>
        <p>AND 'THE OLD WOMAN AND HER PIG." In discussing Sally Waters we were deal- ing with a lady who appears to be entirely British. Neither Sally Waters nor Little Miss Muffet have been heard of beyond these shores. But many other of the heroes of our youth are European and hold the admiration of boys and girls of ever so many lands. Everybody is familiar with our cumula- tive nursery rhymes" The house that Jack built" and "The old woman and her pig." They are pieces which, while attractive to the youthful mind as bits of recitation, have no apparent meaning. The "House that Jack Built" tells us nothing; it has no narrative and, no moral, and suggests by its form that it is part of something rather than a whole. The versions vary very little. It is always: This is the house that Jack built, This is the malt that lay in the house that Jack built.</p>
        <p>And things are carried back as far as the cock that crows in the morn and wakes the priest all shaven and shorn. One old version, however, brings in the knife that kills the cock on the wedding morn. This is an interesting point, for the sacrifice of a cock on the wedding day was a practice that belonged to very early and pagan days,and if it be a genuine part of the rhyme gives it an antiquity of which any bit of literature might he proud.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>FOREIGN VERSIONS.</head>
        <p>FOREIGN VERSIONS.</p>
        <p>Now let us-i(Wc-,:tt the foreign versions to see if anything can be learned. In Denmark_ the children say There you have the house that Jacob built. France it is Peter that builds home This is the house aat Petcr built A rat came from the rat hole And drove the mouse to the mouse bolo.</p>
        <p>The other folks who come in the sequence are the dog, a bear, a man, a maid, the abbot, the Pope and the devil. In Germany the mouse comes into the corn-house and the rat, cat, fox, wolf, bear, man and maid follow. All these versions end abruptly like ours and convey no meaning. In Austria it is "the house of the wooden man," and introduces some powers with which we are familiar in other sequences; the cat, dog, stick, fire, water, ox, kitchen and devil. These features are so much like those in the old woman and her pig as to arouse the suspicion that the two rhymes may have a common origin, or at any rate a common meaning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>STUPID PIG.</head>
        <p>THE STUPID PIG.</p>
        <p>Let us therefore take a survey of the adventures of the old woman and her pig. In all the versions we know she finds a coin and buys a pig. But the pig won't leap the style and she despairs of getting home that night. In her despair she appeals to the dog to bite the pig, and when it refuses she asks the stick to beat the dog and the fire to burn the stick. Finally the cow gives milk to the cat, the cat begins to eat the rat who gnaws the rope, and so on through the sequence until the heroine gets the hero safely over the style. Students of greater insight may have seen meanings in all this. For ourselves we had long ago given it up as jargon. But there are other versions. Take the Scotch : The woman found two pennies and bought a kid. Coming home she tried to pull some berries from a bush but could not. Setting the kid to watch the house she went in search of a dog, then a stick, fire, water, ox, axe, smith, rope, mouse, cat, milk, etc. Each refuses to help until she appeals to the cat which cannot resist the milk. Now let us compare these berries which wont tome off the bush with some other fruits that cannot be gathered. In Switzerland a boy named Jack is sent, to knock pears off the tree but they cannot be get. The master sends the dog to bring Jack home. Then he sends fire to burn the dog and water to quench the fire and so on through the sequence of ox, butcher, hang- man and finally the devil. Jack pulls at the pears and the dog at Jack, &amp;c. An exactly similar story belongs to Swabia. In Vogtland it is the same but Jack is sent to cut oats instead of gather pears.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>ELEMENT OF WITCHERY;</head>
        <p>THE ELEMENT OF WITCHERY;</p>
        <p>Now it seems pretty clear that all these tales deal in witchery. The berries, pears and oats are bewitched and the various powers are invoked to break the spell. In some forms of the story the Abbot and Pope and then .the devil are brought in. Take another story which belongs to Languedoc. The old woman goes to her garden to pull a turnip. It resists her efforts and her husband goes out to help. He pulls at the old woman while she tugs at the turnip. Then comes the son, daughter-in- law, maid, manservant, dog, cat, rat and finally the hog who form in a procession and tug at each other. But the hog does not take hold of the last in the tug of war. He attacks the turnip with his nose from beneath and succeeds in dislodging it. Now for some other versions that bring us nearer to the old woman and her pig. In France the wolf gets into the wood and cannot be got out. The stick, fire, water, calf, butcher and devil follow in succession. In Sweden it is the pig who gets in the wood ! and remains eating acorns and cannot be got out. Elsewhere be gets in the cabbage patch. In other stories it is a kid, as in Scotland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>BEWITCHED nOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE BEWITCHED nOUSE.</p>
        <p>A possible connection between the two stories is in a foreign version in which Jack (in every country he is Jack) tries to build a house and cannot. Everything goes wrong and the dog, stick, fire and water and all the rest are sent for to help. It may easily be that our "House that Jack built" is the con- cluding part of such a story which bids us survey the house when it is finished, the couple happily married and the cock killed for the wedding. So much for the different versions.'Now let us see whether there is anything to give a clue to the age or origin. The malt of Jack's house is most probably millet, the earliest kind of grain cultivated in Europe. The pigs, oats, berries and roots are all things that belong to a very early period. This however does not tell us much. A more interesting clue lies •in the extra- ordinary fact that there is a corner in Europe where the tale of Jack and the pears was taken over by the Church. In Westphalia on the eve of the feast of St. Lambert a religious 'procession was held, and the story was recited and sung to the accompaniment of a dance. The song told how the pears were bewitched of the devil and that fire, water and everything else failed until the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>'ruit</head>
        <p>'ruit</p>
        <p>Viewed from any point of view the pro- ceeding is remarkable. The recital of Jack and the pears is clearly a pagan ritual which the Church took over, as it took over many other things in very early and ignorant days, and adapted as one of its festivals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>HEBREW PARABLE.</head>
        <p>THE HEBREW PARABLE.</p>
        <p>But we have not yet done with surprises in this matter of the sequence story. In a collection of Hebrew chants printed in Venice more than three centuries ago there is one which has all the familiar features. It is told in the first person and begins: A. kid, a kid my father bought For two pieces of money A kid, a kid. Then came the rat and ate the kid, That my father bought For two pieces of money A kid, a hid.</p>
        <p>The dog comes to eat the cat, the stick beats the dog, the fire burns the stick, the water quenches the fire, the ox drinks tho water, the butcher kills the ox, the angel of death takes the butcher and the Holy One grips the angel of death. This chant was for long a part of the Jewish liturgy and was recited in Hebrew at one of the feasts. But the Rabbis have never been agreed about its meaning. One interpretation suggests that it sets forth in a picturesque form the fact that each power in creation is kept within bounds by a power that is above it and that th 6 Almighty is above all and stronger than the angel of death. Another finds in it the suggestion that punishment awaits all wrong doing and that God has his executioners everywhere. A third interpretation is more ingenious:</p>
        <p>It finds in the Father who brought the kid Jehovah himself. .The kid is Israel; the cat is the Assyrian, the dog the Babylonian; the staff Cyrus, who conquered Babylon; the fire Rome; the butcher Titus, and so forth. Remembering the Eastern faculty for picturvsque teaching, this is a possible inter- pretation, and the chant may quite easily have been in its origin a parable of the his- tory of Israel which lee up to the consoling teaching that God is behind everything and can preserve his people from every foe. Its inclusion in Jewish ritual is an almost certain proof that it originally had a serious religious purpose.: _ • . .</p>
        <p>- Yet if this be so the curious fact is to be noted that it nowhere retains a meaning. Scotland keeps closest to the Hebrew in having the two pieces of money, the kid, dog, stick, fire, water, ox and butcher. But no nation attaches a moral. In all the narratives save the Jewish it is either Jack or an old woman to whom the adventure comes. All appear to come from a common source for all have common features such as the dog, stick, fire, water, ox and butcher, but the task of discovering the original is quite beyond us. We found " Sally Waters" to be an unblushing little pagan. It is different with the old woman who owned the stupid pig. She appears to have started as a quaint yet solemn parable, but dressed in foolish trappings has been for centuries mistaken for a nursery clown. A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>LANCASHIRE'S OLD AGE PENSIONS</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE'S OLD AGE PENSIONS</p>
        <p>Over £BOO,OOO a Year.</p>
        <p>The Clonal Secretary, opening the Liberal Club b•zaar in Rawterstall on Wednrsday, raid they could all unite in congratulation on the prosperity of trade, which he had always maintained was not always the result of any political party I,eing in power. The cotton trade had been espniilly satisfactory. The grant of £lO,OOO a year for five years from the Treasury to the British Cotton-growing Association expired during the present year, hut as the result of representations he bad personal!y pressed upon them, the Tr, asury bad agreed to extend the grant for another three yrars. Alluding t) o'd age pensions, Mr Harcourt said the figures for Lancashire showed the number of pensioners had increased from 6,000 to i 63,000, and involved an annual cost of over .000,000. It was a splendid result of the cheerful assumption by the well-to-do of the liability rightly theirs, and none who had the honour to share in the enactment would ever regret their labours. (Chee rs</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>TO MR. LLOYD GEORGE</head>
        <p>TO MR. LLOYD GEORGE</p>
        <p>"PILLAR OF A PEOPLE'S ITOPFS."</p>
        <p>A Bangor correspondent rends the following quota- tion from Tennyson's "In Memoriam," shich hq thinks may well be quoted apropos of Mr Lloyd George's fiftieth birthday, just passed : Dcst thou look back on what bath been As some divinely gifted man, Whose life in low rs'ate began And on a simple village green; Who hrialis his birth's invidious bar, Ard grasps the skirts of happy chance, And breasts the blows of circumstance, And grapples with his ea il star ; Who makes by force his merit kro And liv(s t clutch the golden keys, To mould a mighty State's decrees, Anl shape the whisper of the Throne; And moving up from high to Wgher, Becomes on Fortune's crowning slop?, The pillar of a people's hope, The centre of a world's desire.</p>
      </div>
