      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL AND LOCAL PROGRESS</p>
        <p>OF THE CAUSE.</p>
        <p>The minds of all suffragists are filled with the th ught of the Franchise Bill, which was introduced in the House of Commons yesterday. In case any readers are not clear as to the different amendments, in favour of extending the franchise to women, which are to be proposed, a brief summary may lyi useful. The Bill proposes to give the Parliamentary vote to all male persons over 21 yeats cf age. Ihe word male has been inserted for the first time, as of course there is always a question as to whether a woman is not, after all, a person. She is a peraon if it is a matter of plying taxes, but not a person when it is a matter cf the privilege of the vote. The first amendment, which will be moved by Sir Edward Grey, is to delt te the word male. This in itself will not actually give the vote to any woman, but it will clear the way for ether amendments. Suffragists of all parties can unite in supporting S. ir Edward Grey. Having opened the door by omitting the restricting word, three alternative amendments will be brought forward. The first by the Labour party who will propose to give adult suffrage instead of manhcod suffrage. In the present state of feeling in the House of Commons, this amendment has no chance of being earth d. The second amendment, known as the Dickenson amendment, is on the lines of the Norwegian franchise, and would give the vote to women householders and the wives of householders, over the age of 25. One,. could wish that th's age limit could be enfotced for men as well as for women. This amendment will enfranchise about seven million women, and it would be specially valuable as it would include married women, and thus recognise that the household is not fully represent d unless the point of view of both partters is considered With all the legislation affecting the home and the children, which is continually being brought forward, one feels that the martied woman ought not to be ignoi ed. The country needs to have at its disposal all the wisdom of its people instead of only half. Of course there is the old objection that it would lead to domestic strife ; but if husbands and wives wish to quarrel, there is never any difficulty in finding a reason ; and ti e present ccnditions are surely more conducive to bitterness, when one partner, however keenly she may feel, is debarred from contributing her vote to the party she believes in. If the Dickenson amendment is not carried there remains the Conciliation amendment, which %%ill be proposed by Lord R bert Cecil. This would give the vote to women who are on the municipal register, chiefly widows and spinsters. It would only enfranchise between one and two million worn n, whereas ten million men would have votes. But reforms in England have always came gradually, and at least the principle would be established. The annual meetings of the vat ions suffrage societies which are held at this time of the year, show an encouraging record of hard work and progress. The annual meeting of the Warrington Society was held on January 11th, when Miss Waring gave a short address. Though the vote has not ye t been won, she pointed out the great advance that the women's movement has made eluting the past fifty years. Women can now obtain a University education, they can be Mayors, town councillors, p or law guardians, doctors; and they are frequently chosen on imp ,rtant Government commissions. The passing of the White Slave 'frac Bill had been a great victory, though a long de la3ed one. The secretary of the National Vigilance Asscciation said that the passing cf the Bill was largely due to the agitation raised by the women of the county and especially the women suffragiqs. Another Ft' iking example ( f the change that was taking place was that in the reports of the Royal Commission on the Divorce Laws, the same standard was set for both men and women. This would help to do away with the idea of a double moral code for the men and women, which was the root ( f so much evil. After Miss Warirg's address, the eflicers and corn-</p>
        <p>mittee for the el sr-ling sear were elected. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Miss Ma,sy, the reliting president, and Mrs James Fairclough was elected in her stead. The vice-presidents are Mrs Dodds Miss Lucy Broadbent, Miss Crestield. The Hon, Secretary is Mrs R Pembertoa and the Hon. Treasurer Mrs Pidduck. At the annual meeting of the Man- chester Society, Catrlcillor Margaret Ashton spoke of the progress that had been made during the year and dwelt on the close relaticnsbip between politics and the home. "The woman who lived and worked at home was realizing more and more that politics had come into the home, that she was touched by politics all the way round. Although she had n3t the time to go forth and work at po'itics she had realized that the fact of bring able to come forward quietly at elections and to cast her vote for the candidate wlio would best represent her point of view, was a power which would not detach her frcm the horns, but would bring the home int) general politics." It is not always realized that the enfranchisement of woa en would be of great advantage to men as well as to women. It is in fact hardly true to speak of it as a woman's question." As an example of this we would refer to the decision of the Wages Board in Victoria, which has fixed a minimum wage for all adult typists of 45 shillings weekly. This regulation will, it is believed, lead to the dismissal of many ill equipped women typists, at present employed a an inadeouate wage, and their replacement by male typists who have been fully trained. Nevertheless women are unanimous in support of the action just taken. They value the clear recognition of the principle that wages should be regulated, not by the sex of the worker, but by the quality of the work; they are convinced that properly tamed women need fear no rivals; and they recrgnise that many women are engaged in ill p tid work, not because they would not gladly marry and work f r the community in the sphere of the home, but because the men who would gladly marry them are too poorly paid to contemplate marriage at all. The principle of equal wages for equal work is so clear that one wonders it should ever have been ignored. It is a matter of simple justice; and on lower grounds, it is at least as much men's interest as women's to et force it. Many people say they can no longer support Wcmen's Suffrage because of the pillar box outrages. We are thankful to say that these tactics have now been suspended, and we would remind our readers that the Postmaster wholestmast et destructionernGeneral,s broughtpeakit)gin bccember, said that th about by weeks of outrages on pillar boxes, was of 13 letters and seven p, st cards. It is not generally realized what a small section the militants are, and it is hardly just or reasonable to deny justice to millions of women for the sake of thirteen letters and seven post cards. The Dean of Worcester, well-known to Lancashire people as Archdeacon Wilson of Rochdale, writes as follows in the magazine of the Church League for Women's Suffrage:— " Nowhere in the whole of the New Testament is there any indication of Christ speaking of women,or to women, as if they were in any way different or inferior to men. Nowhere in the teaching of Christ is there any support to be obtained for the view that there is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
