      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>IMPORTANT INSURANCE POINT.</head>
        <p>IMPORTANT INSURANCE POINT.</p>
        <p>NSURED PERSONS WHO PAY UP ARREARS.</p>
        <p>In cases %Olen insure] persons, through unemploy- ment or illness, hive not had 26 stamps placed on their insurance cards by their employers, some societies have hesitated to pay sickness or maternity benefits e'en though the insured person himself bought up the stqmps to make up the necessary 26 %oohs. The contention was that section 10, clause 5 of the Act debarred insured persons from receiving bent fits for one month after arrears have been paid. Ihe point has, however, been definitely cleared up in response to a letter LA hich Coun- cillor W. Forshaw, of St. llcleos, sent to the Insurance Commissioners :</p>
        <p>St. Ilelens, January nth, 1911. Dear Sir,—l should esteem it a favour if you will let me hive a reply to the following question at your earliest convenience :</p>
        <p>A workman is employed until dm end of October, 1912, his employer stamping his Insurance Card up to that date. He falls ill and is. off work the remainder of the quarter. On January 19th, 1913, he buys the necessary stamps to complete his 21 payments. Co an Approved Society grant immediate sickness benefit, or will the man be debarred from Sick Pay for one mon:h under Section 10 (5) of the Act ?</p>
        <p>I submit for your consideration that Section 10 (5) only applies to arrears after the Act has beenlin operation twelve months. If it is held that this is not so, and a man takea ill after his 25th payment is going to be compelled, after paying arrears, to wait another month for benefits, it will be a real hardship in these cases. :te! An early reply will much oblige.—Ft.ith fully your 3 WM. FORSIIAW.</p>
        <p>The folio% ing is the reply teceived from the Com- missioner :</p>
        <p>Natiooll Health Insurance Commisdon (England) Buckingham Gate, London, S. W. 17th January, 1913. Sir,—ln rerly to }our letter of the 14th instant, I am directed by the National Health Insurance Com. mission (England) to inform you that an insured per- son is not entitled to Sickness Benefit unless and until 26 weeks have elapsed since his entry loto insurance, and 26 weekly contributions have been paid by, or in respect of him. It is, however, open to an insured person, in order to qualify for Sickness Benefit at the earliest possible date, to stamp his current card for weeks during which owing to sickness or unemploymont,he 'las had no employer to stamp it for hini.lf contributions were not paid during weeks in a previous quarter, the ir sured person sli(uld apply to his Society ior an arrears card, and affix the necessary stamps thereto. I am to add that the provisions of Section 10 (5) of the National Insurance Act d not apply to con- tributions in respect of weeks during the first twelve months after the commencement of the Act.—l am, sir, your obedient servant, G. Al. YOUN'O,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MOTHER'S ORDEAL,</head>
        <p>A MOTHER'S ORDEAL,</p>
        <p>Distressing Weakness was the cause of her perpetual Backache.</p>
        <p>How Dr. Williams' Pink Pills gave her New Strength.</p>
        <p>The ordeal of a brave woman who, sufferirg from irregular health and xtreme debility, strove tl do Ler duty to her family, is illus- trated in the ease of</p>
        <p>Mrs Emily Widdow-tion, who resides .ati.ll, South</p>
        <p>Street, Wakefield.</p>
        <p>conversation, recently, Mrs. Widdowson, who is the proud parent of two, charming children, said :</p>
        <p>"After my last baby was b)rn I did not</p>
        <p>regain St eng h as MRS. WIDDOWSON. quickly as I should have done. I tried to keep going for the sake of my little ones, but often had to leave them to others. I never felt well, and had awful weaknesses, with pains across the forehead. Also my back ached dreadfully at times. "My blood was p or, my colour vanished and my back felt broken. It was, in fact, as though all life and energy had been dragged out of me. "In spite of treatment, I became weaker and lost interest in everything. I could have sat the whole day through without moving only that my nerves were sO unsteady ; I was startled at almost any noise, and after the least exertion I became flurtied, while my heart palpita'ed and seemed to bump against my ribs. I turned against all solid focd and could digest next to nothing. All that I seemed to care for was a cup of tea, but as I had baby to look after, this was not enough nourishment.</p>
        <p>"I kept on taking medicines fur my blood and nerves, but as these gave me no help at all, I was soon bordering on a state of nervous collapse. " Then a friend persuaded me to try Dr. Williams Pink Pills, and kindly ( ffered me a supply to try. I took regular doses of these Pills, and the way they enriched my blood was wonderful. It was not long before I was feeling cheerful and active, and was far better able to care for my baby. Then as I continued taking Dr Williams' Pink Pills my health became regular, my appetite got keen, and I could take sub- stantial meals, which was a help to baby too. A further supply of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills banished all tree of headaahe, backache and palpita- tion. Then every trace cf weakness left me. and I was well, active and strong again." Lady readers should wiite to 46 Holborn Viaduct., London, EL'., for free lnoklet, entitled "PLAIN TALKS."</p>
        <p>Backache does not mean "kidney troubles," but debility from lack of nouri6hing blood. Dr. Williams Pink Pills make rich, red blood and banish the back aches and debility so often afflicting women, also Artemis, General Weakness, Rheumatism,lndigestion Palpitation, Neuralgia and Nervous Disorders. The Pills are sold by dealers, or direct from Dr. Williams Medicine Co., 46 Holborn Viaduct, London, E,C., post free, 2s. Od. one box, or 13s. 9,1. for six boxes. The sbopman's "something else" is useless to you; so if pestered buy elsewhere.</p>
