      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <p>The "Medical Times."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, January 25, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, January 25, 1913.</p>
        <p>which was the spirit of the age and which was essential in almost every line of industrial activity if we wore to live. He specially commended the object of the commerOal travellers' co-operative efforts, which was ' to help the children of deserving members and those travellers who fell by the roadside through sickness or other adversity. Mr Rylands spoke highly of the spirit of geniality fostered by such an association' which served the members so well in their calling, and he expressed the hope that the ‘Varrington branch would receive the cordial support of all members of the craft for many years to come. (Applause.) Mr CIENRLEs DAVENPORT responded in a speech full of convincing faots as to the usefulness and progress of the association. At the outset, he mentioned that he was a native of Burnley, and the name of Peter Rylands was closely associated with the politieal history of that town. Ha (tho speaker) remembered the time when Peter Rslands was admired and loved 1 by all the older class of politicians and,if he bad closed h,s eyes while their president was speaking, he might easily have im igined th :t he was listening to Peter , Rylands, M.P. for Burnley, in the old days. (Applause.) Referring to the mutual help aspect of the association, Mr Davenport pointed out that it provided comprehen- sive schemes of insurance and annuities on special terms and there was also an employment bureau for the pur- pose of bringing into touch the travellers who were seeking berths and firms which required their services. The fact, that the bureau had not been very largely used by members, might perhaps be a cheering sign—hat few commercial travellers were out of work. The association had lately started an approved society. It. was an unfortunate and lamentable fact that there were commercial travellers on the road who did not elm .Cl6O a year, and as they had to be insurei they thought it wise to form an association of their own, by which it would be possible for mem )era to benefit from the good health records of commercial travellers and obtain superior benefits. An impor:ant experiment was also being made in the</p>
        <p>HIGHER EDUCATION' OF COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS. They heard a gre,t deal in these days about the superior equipment for tra‘el of the Germans and the foreigners generally, and the association came to tho conclusion that one of the best ways of removing that reproach to English travellers was to encourage their members to become better qualified educationally Language classes hid been started—in several cases with considerable success but in others with c nly moderate amount—and that department of their work needed encouragement. It was desirable that commer- cial travellers seould be sufficiently educated to go into other countries and dispose of our products, just as foreign travellers came here and took our trade. -rho benefit society in c,nnection with the association was the best thing of its kind in the country, Then they had also a legal department in which the cause of their members was championed against oppressive conditions and they had not only established rulings but bad settled many cases amicably. The speaker next ton3hed upon the valuable conccseions which the association had been able to obtain from the railway companies, and strongly condensnei several C:055 of abuse of the week-end ticket which had recently come to light, The two charities which the ass tci ttion supported—the schools and benevolent institution—were dist'nct from the a seda- tion, but since its formation the association bad con- tributed upwards of .C 25,000 to those objects. Over 3,000 childree had passed through the echouls and had had berths found for them. A gratifying featare was the unbroken record of success—there was not a single absolute failure. (Applause.) At present there were 315 children in the schools and .C 9,000 per annum was required for its upkeep. The benevolent institution had pei i out £3C3,000 in annuities. 'I he number of annuitants on the bcoks to-day w;4l 500, and they were in receipt of .C 13,500 per annum The speaker instanced the case of a lady who lived to the Dee of eighty and drew £1,160 in annuities, although her age at the time of his de ith had con- tributfd only six guineas to the essociation. In con- clusion he said they were not yet satisfied with the progress of the association, He looked to the day when the members would be bound together by even stronger ties than at present and he hoped they would, as a brotherhood, be always ready to work for those who needed help and to mtiutaio the status of their calling. (Applause). Mr. G. llowetAN proposed " Warrington and District Brood), U.K.C.T.A." He sail the initiation of the local branch was dictated by the natural instinct of self-protection and that it was fulfilling a useful pur- pose was evidenced by the fact that its membership had increised by 50 per cent. The fact that there were such excellent commercial travellers in our midst must do much to account for the prosperity 9f our trade. Those who had to remain amid the smoke and din of the manufactories sometimes felt inclined to envy "the m +to on the road," as they thought of him travelling luxuriously to pleasant places. (Laughter.) Ilis work, liewever, often involved great mental strain. (Hear, Istar).„ . . - _</p>
        <p>Mi. II L TAvnort (chairman of the brancL) responded. fie said the branch began with 19 members and to-day it had over 80. (Applause.) They had their loaal benevolent society by which they helped fallen brethren, and they had also started an out-of-work fund entitling a member who had p id two years' subscrip- tions to £1 a week for thirteen weeks.l hey were deter- mined to advance still further and a proposal at present on the carpet was to pay £lO to the next of kin of any member on death. They might also be able to pay an old age pension—not of ss. but of 103. per week. (Applause). Mr W G HAYES proposed "The President." He described Mr liylands as a real live President who sympathised with all their objects and especially with their benevolent work. (Applause.) Mr RYLANDS reqaonied. Ile said they were told that "heaven helped those who helped themselves," but one always liked to see men standing shoulder t) shoulder in their rnatual interests, instead of fighting one another.</p>
        <p>Oi her toasts were " The Visitors " proposed by Mr 11 Mercer and " The Artistes " proposed by Mr W P Ashton.</p>
        <p>An excellent musical programrin was stlb.nitted, the association having b 'en fortunate in obtaining the services of several well-known local vocalists. NI iss NI E Brundrett gave artistic interpretations of " My ships" and The Temple Bells," rayon ling to an (nicer) with "My doar soul." Mr J Rabbitt wit 3 in his b2st form and sing `• Dear Heart " and " The English rose " in soulful style, being recAllei for each. Mr J W Bibby gave" Down in the deep" and "In happy moruer.ts " with his usual taste and flDish. Thera were also humorous songs by Mr A Wooley and Mr C Yearsley. Instrumental trios by Mr James Ray (violin), Mr Walter Ray (flute) and Mr Hubert J Iddon (piano) were appreciatively received. Mr II J Iddon was an efficient accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS.</head>
        <p>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLERS.</p>
        <p>KEEN COMPETITION AND CHANGED CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING SPEECHES AT ANNUAL DINNER.</p>
        <p>Our modern knights of the road—in other words commercial travellers - play a more important part in our industrial developments than perhaps many people imagine. As a local business man aptly put it, it is their constant "pegging away" which helps to keep the wheels going round in our Warrington manu- factories and makes our products a household word in distant places. Acting upon the co-operative spirit of the age, the travellers now p.)ssess their own organiza- tion for self-help and fraternal purposes. It is called the United Kingdom Commercial Traveller s' Association and Warrington boasts a very live and flourishing branch. Con,gratulation on the striking progress which has been made was the dominant note at the seventh annual dinner, held at the Lion Hotel on Saturday evening. A rich vein of comradeship and giod fellowship characterised the proceedings and, in every respect, the function was a complete success. Mr W Peter Rylauds (president of the branch) presided over a large attendance and among others present were: —The Maycr (Councillor Dr Joseph), Rev G C iggs, Messrs Charles Daven- port (lion treasurer of the-U.K.C.T.A.), 1' S Steel (boa. solicitor to the branch). J Fulton Smith, G Bowman, and the following officials of the Branch: - Chairman, Mr LI L Taylor ; vice-chairman, Mr P O'Brien ; hon. treasurer, Mr W L Walker ; hon. secretary, Mr T Percival. It was announced that apologies for non- attendance had ben received from Sir Gilbnrt Greenall, Bart., Mr Harold Smith, M.P., Mr Henry Greenall and the Mayor of Widnes (Alderman F Neil). After dinner there was the • usual speech-making, interspersed with musical items.</p>
        <p>The-PRE'SIDENT, in submitting the health of " The King," said that, as commercial traveilers,they regarded his Alajc,sty with special appreciation, because there had never been a King who had shown quite the same determination to got into cloEe touch with. the corn- mErce of the country, and to understand the various industries upon which the greatne3B of this nation dhpended. His 11.1jesty was t) visit this district for that purpose during the summer of the present year and they</p>
        <p>HOPED TO HAVE THE HONOUR OF SEEING HIM IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>(Applause.) While he was here, his Majesty would no doubt take tne opportunity of inspecting some of the leading works of the town. (Applaus,.) Mr. T. S. STEEL proposed " Tha Mayors and Cor- porations of ,Warrington and neighbouring boroughs." At the outset he paid a high tribute to the great intelligence, ability and impartiality which Councillor Dr. Joseph brought to bear upon the duties of his office. It was no easy task, in a Council composed of coon of sarying dispositions and views, to see that the rules of debate were obeyed, but his Worship did exceedingly well in that capicity. " I have often felt very sarry for him in his difficulties " aided' Mr Steel, " but he has always come out very creditably." The work of the Corpora- tion in recent years had grown very considerably and it behoved the ratepayers of a large town like War- rington to see that they appointed men who were fitted for the duties of councillors, and who could understand the business thtt was to he transacted, because matters of great importance and far-reaching effect would come t;etore them. Speaking personally, he did not think the ratepayers always appreciatei the importance of the office to which they were electing a man. Various considerations were brought to bear on elections and they did not always get the best men to repre3ent them in the Council. He believed the ratepayers would never fully appreciate the importance of voting at municipal elections until every resident who Ltd a vote was called upon to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>PAY MS OWN RATES.</head>
        <p>PAY MS OWN RATES.</p>
        <p>(Hear, hear.) However, he believed there were many men in the Council who were bringing to bear upon their work the very best that they could give to it— men of the strictest integrity, whose only desire was the improvement and benetit of their town. (Applause.) 'I he NI AYL)I2, in responding, said the amArat of work devolving upon the head of the Corporation was almost stupendous and it was only by a kind of acrobatic performance that anyone like himself, who had other duties to attend to, could hope to accomplish it. his Worship next had a few words to sly in defence of the " ambassadorial policy " which he has pursued and said that to be on frieadly terms with neighbouring boroughs would probably enable Warrington to obtain favourable terms when questions aff,cting a number of localities were uader consideration. Continuing, the Mayor expressed agreement with Conncill,r Steel in his view that the npijority of members of the Council —probably all—were actuated by an earnest desire to do their duty and in that respect they were good men. They had varied ideas of business traosictions and town i:eprovements—in fact they even held different views is to the merits and demerits of anonymous letters. (Laughter.) All present would be aware that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ANONYMOUS LETTERS</head>
        <p>ANONYMOUS LETTERS</p>
        <p>had I).2eu sent to certain members—le obably the great mi4j,arlty—of the Council and, while some spoke very vehemeir ly against them, others regarded them as real benefits and encouragement in their public woes. (Laughter.) He thought it was something new for an anonymous letter to be lauded as an absolute good —but still, it only showed the different points of view that could arise in the Council. Having dismissed this part of his address as " serio-comic," his Worship said it would be a good thing if every person felt the immediate effect of baying to pay a rate of sl much in the People would then be more likely to take more trouble to vote f)r the man who they thought was most likely to serve them. It was a pity they did not get moil who were big ratepayers in the Town Council. (Hear, heir.) Without litttery he would give as an excellent example their president (Mr ltyland,). Such gentlemen were the kind they wanted in the Ceuncil. (Applause ) He (the Mayor) had said before—and he would continue to repeat it—that one of the greatest evils operating against municipal improve- ment and progress generally was party politics. (Hear, hear.ln their proper place politics were no doubt good. Occasionally they had to be stuipmded, even at West- miuster, in the case of great national cris3s, In a municipality like Warrington they might Le considered to be always in a kind of microcosmic national crisis, because their interests were always urgent, as regarded their immediate area, and therefore it ought to be like the days which Macaulay referred to when "none was for a:party, when all were for the State." (Applause.) They ought to work together and try to get the very best men to rule over their local destinies, and then a ria7esentative who had worked for three years and done his best would not he turned out, simply becluse he happened to te of a given political colour. (Applause). A great many townspeople seemed to agree with his views on that question, but nothing was done in the matter. How- ever, he intended to drum at it until something was done. (Applause). Mr P O'BRIEN proposed "The town and trade of Warringtoa." He said they were told that the new Warrington Bridge would be one of the finekt in Lancashire, and he knew men in Warrington who were hoping to live to see its completion. (Laughter). Other alterations ought necessarily to follow in con- nection with the thoroughfare leading to Wilderspool. When residecti in Stockton Heath and district, on their way to business in Warrington, reached the level cross- ing and found the gates closed, there was a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>SAD AND SUDDEN LAPSE</head>
        <p>SAD AND SUDDEN LAPSE</p>
        <p>from that high standard of language for which Warring- ton mon were noted. (Laughter.) The necessary improvement might not be undertaken in our time, but future generations would have to face it. Referring to Warrington's commercial activities, the speaker said that when they sawsmoke rising from all the works chim no)s, they said" Where there's smoke there's money being made." (Hear, hear.) He supposed there was DO town in Great Britain which sent more of the products of its industries to all parts of tie civilized world. ‘Varriugton had also produced men who had gained dis- tinction in p ;Udell and social life, as well as in the arts end sciences and in the realm of mechanics. Warringtonians had achieved great things in the vast and he believed the young men of the present day were prepared to uphold the traditions of the town. (Applause.) As an illustration of " What Lancashire thinks to-day England thinks to-morrow," Mr O'Brien instanced the fact that when Warringtoa ele3t ed a medical man to the Mayoralty, London immediately followed its example. (Applause). Mr. J. FULTON SMITH, in responding, said that if the ctt nnrcial travellers were not BO successful in expanding the business of the local firms, Warrington would be in a sorry plight indeed. It was because of th&amp;r constant plodding and pegging away to get their wares introduced into distant parts, that they were able to keep the wheels going round in Warrington. Having touched upon the keener competition which travellers had to encounter in these days, the speaker said the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>UNFORTUNATE_ UNREST</head>
        <p>UNFORTUNATE_ UNREST</p>
        <p>which prevailed amongst workingmen must also have a deterrent effect upon their efforts, because it was undoubtedly, resulting in increased cost of produc- tion. That, of course, made it necessary to exerjse great care in the preparation of their quotations, so that they would not miss a cm- tract through want of proper estimation of the coit in the initial stage. Having argued that industrial unrest was one element in advancing the prices of commodities—as capitalists were disposed to hold back a little in view of possible ruptures in their relationships with their employees.—Mr Fulton Smith expressed the hope that the trade boom would long continue and that Warrington would maintain its premier position in many industries. (Applause). Mr. RI-LANDS propo.ed " The U.K.C.T.A., the schools and benevolent institutions." He referred to the association as an exemplification of that co-operation</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>TURTLE SAVES LIFE.</head>
        <p>TURTLE SAVES LIFE.</p>
        <p>When it is necessary for a doctor to administer a strong, vitalising food to a sinking patient, Turtle Soup is the one hope. It is quickly absorbed, and nourishes while there is yet time. Lives which roust otherwise have been lost are thus every day saved. If the patient is rich, there is no obstacle; but to pre- scribe .luttle Soup for a poor man is often felt to be like ordering a trip to the liiviera to one whose real trouble is something like starvation. Medical men have therefore learned with great satisfaction, through an article in the "Lancet," the greatest medical journal in the world, that real Turtle Soup has, by a new process of concentration, been brought within the means of every family, even if poor. The discovery is due to Mr. T. K. Bellis, for many years associated with the import trade in live turtles. Bellis's Turtle Cup, distinguished by the name on the label as the genuine product of one who has become known in the City of London as the Tuttle King, can be obtained at the grocer's in shilling bottles, ready flavoured, and mixed with the necessary meat and vegetable extracts to make Turtle Cup by the simple addition of boiling water. As a table luxury, it is delicious and, to mcst people, novel. As an invalid nutrient Bellis's Turtle Cup is a life-saver. Do not accept a substitute ; imitations are not "as god."</p>
        <p>If unobtainable at your usual grccers or stores it will be sent post free for price, direct fi om Turtle Cup (1911), Ltd., Dept. 53, 15, Bury treet, St. Mary Axe, London, E.C. Insist on baying Bellia's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>FARNI DRAINAGE IN ONTARIO.</head>
        <p>FARNI DRAINAGE IN ONTARIO.</p>
        <p>Thousands of pounds have been placed in the pockets of the Ontario farmers during the last few years owing te advantage being taken of the assistance which can be secured by installing clay tile on their farina. This is practised more particularly on heavy clay soils, but it is also being largely used on light soils, especially for fruit growing. Large areas of laud have been reclaimed which have never before given any return. It is now recognised as a constituent of good farming. It has been amply demonstrated that farres which are efficiently drained are much more productive than those which are not so. In some cases it is difficult for the iodividual farmer to decide upon the best method of draining' size of tile, depth of distance apart, and to meet this necessity the Ontario Agricul- tural College trains a number of students every year for this purpose. Experts are sent out among the ' farmers to take levels of farm drainage and to give instructions in the proper methods of installing the tiles. The expense of this work to the farmer benefited is nominal; but of course the cost of the tiles and of laying them is borne by him. These men often save farmers thousands of dollars by aiding them to instal their systems properly. The benfit to the farmer may be measured by the fact that the increase in crops under efficient drainage amounts on an average to per acre. Not only are the crops made larger, but the public health is better, due to having less standing water. This work has an educational as well as a pecuniary value. The farmer learns that he can con- trol the supply of moisture which is to .be supplied to his crops. The seasons do not influence his gross returns to such a great extent. He discovers that by scientific methods he gains more and more the control of the forces contributing to his welfare.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>The Coming of Lent.—The first of their Majesties Courts will take place on February 7th, and the spring season in London will be somewhat earlier than usual, owing to the early occurrence of Easter. Ash Wednes- day falls on February 5,,b, and the early advent of Lenten observances has had the effect of crowding most of the ciunty and hunt halls, into the first month of the year. Lent is not, as a rule, very strictly observed nowadays, but it has a considerable influence in relation to social life. Its early arrival tends to shorten winter associatiens, for we always think of Lent and the springtime together, although it brings the east wind, and not infrequently the snow blizetrd. Under the existing system of fixing the festival it is this year the earliest possible Easter that the present generation will ever se-.. Easter Sunday falls on March 23tet, which, next to March 22nd, is the earliest and the west infrequent date for the festival in the whole range of possible dates from March 22 id to April 25th. Only on four occasions has it happened on March 22nd, in the last .100 yeare, and this will not occur again until the year 2,286 It is possible that before long a reformed calend3r will intervene, providing a fixed Easter in the second or third week of April. The Marriage of Lady E iwina Ruberts.—Lord and Lady Roberts have a charming house—Englemere—at Ascot, where at All Saints, Lady Els,viva Roberts, is to be married ta Msjor Lewin on Wednesday, February 26. Cupid loves the sound of the drum, but the daughters of our Field Marshals—Lord Roberts and Lord Wolseley— appear to have been rather shy of him. Lord and Lady Roberts were married fifty-three years ago, but this will be the first wedding in the family. Of their six children only two survive Lady Aileen Roberts, who was born in 1870, and bay Edwina Roberts, who is in her thirty-eighth year. Lady Aileen is heiress te) the Earldom, or if the succession is not continued through her, the title may pass to Lady Edwina and her heirs mile. There is thus every probability of the survival of the title bestowed upon our greatest soldier. Women and the State.—Mlle. Marie Detrzar's candi- dature for the French Presidency was not, of course taken seriously, but it Berved as an opportunity for an appeal in favour of womeus' suffrage, so that, as she said, France may not know " the tr )übled hours, that the suffragists are preparing in England." There has be .n however, for the past few weeks, a welcome cessa- tion from these" troubled hours pending the fate of the women's cause under the Franchise Bill. If that fails, then, we are told militancy will be resumed with redoubled vigour. It is to he hoped tint opportunity will be afforded in the House of Corn ions for a full discussion of the various propose I schemes for the enfranchisement of women, and it, will he most unsatis- factory if the issue is again "side-tracked" by technical formalities. It would seem thet the amen lment "con- ferring the franchise upon women householders and the wives of electors," appeals most widely for sepport, and that presumably would include for the purpose of the franchise, single women who an 3 in lodgings or are tenants of roams._</p>
        <p>--Masculine Modes.—There is a decided tendency towards masculine fashions in some of this &amp;won's novelties. The new waistcoats; especially when bound with braid, are most manly in appeatance. The waist- coat is no longer simulated, but .uite a separate and important garment resembling a inaa's evening model, for it is cut very low, has a double row of handsome buttons which are often made of some semi-precious stone such as onyx, catseye, rook crystal and the like. Another shape quite as masculine in style, was formerly lee° su as the Tattersall and has a soft silk turnover collar, and may be made of short lengths of any lief) material to be found at bargain prices in the sales Waistcoats will be very much worn this spring. Revival of Coatees.—Fire, in this direction ranks the b _dere, which was launched some months ago, but not taken seriously by dressmakers, probably on account of its prolonged vogue when last worn. Fashions in this century are so ephemeral, that no sooner are they brought out and accepted by the leaders, than they are considered demod The bolero, however, is so smart and becoming, that when it has taken a firm hold on the public taste it will, like the narrow skirt, maintain its position for an indefinite period, hence the hesitation on the part of designers. There are sigr,s, hawever, that the bolero is to be a feature of many of the newest designs. The latest models show it in many instances with pouched back, and round cut away fronts. Bit it is capable of great variety. The Black Sage Gown.—Very useful at this time of year, since it can be worn under the long fur or other coat, is the little straight, Puritan, or Quaker gown or as the French call it, the Soutane. Sometimes it is made with the addition of a short smart little coat of the same material for wear on mild days. It then com- prises a useful walking suit. The latest model of this simple gown opens down the middle of the front, sufficiently to show just a glimpse of white please net, or mousseline do soie, or an apron effect is given. The smartest dresses for afternoon wear of the moment, are undoubtedly those of black serge, of the finest, softest, most supple make which lends itself admirably to com- plicated draperies the general effect of which is simplicity itself. Only a master hand, however, can successfully manipulate these seemingly simple, but really intricate draperies, and they should not be attempted by the mere amateur. The Small Hat and Uncomfortable Feather.—The present approved position of th 3 feat'aer in the modish small hat, leaves mnch to be desired, and the majority of our men folk will undoubtedly rejoice at the tidings which reach us from Paris, to the effect that fhwers instead of feathers will be donned as the spring advances. The uncurled ostrich feathers of the m ,ment take any position except artistic and comfortable ones. Some poke out at the back, others start straight out at tbe side ad are almost as great a nuisance and danger as the uproteated hat-pin. Easily Made Night Caps.—For the dainty nightcap, without which no fashionable woman nowadays retires' and which is so costly in shupland, all that is required is a piece of spotted muslin, ninon, chiffln or other thin fabric, cut in a circle 20 inches in diameter To this should be added a three inch hem (cut on the cross) and at the edge of the hem an edging of lace of any width preferred. Into the three inch hem about half a yard, or a little more, of elastic should be run and joined. A bow or two of ribbon tacked in approved positions, and the cap is complete. The removal of the elastic and bows is all that is necessary to facilitate Use easy washing and ironing process. Correct Dieting.—According to a leading medical light a big breakfast, frugal lunch, no tea and moderate, nourishing evening meal about 7 30, with a cup of miik and a dry biscuit just before retiring, is the ideal meal routia e. Be this as it may, unquestionably for the woman who would keep a smooth and comparatively unlined face. and good clear complexion, the cup of milk and biscuit before retiring is wonderfully helpful. And needless to say no woman who would keep youth- ful and good looks should touch alcohol. Plenty of water, taken hot, one hour before meals, and on rising in the morn'ng, is a habit it would be well if we were all to cultivate.</p>
        <p>DRUNK WHEN NOT SOBER. PLEA FOR POLICE coma STANDARD.</p>
        <p>A drink problem is mentioned by the Chief Constable of Chester (Mr J II Laybourin-) in his yearly report. The number of persons dealt with for drunkenness and drunk and disorderly last year was 232 ; of these 101 had no fixed abode or were non-resident in the city. The Chief Constable comments:—The number cf persons proceeded against for drunkenness is the lowest for the past fifteen years, but the figures are very high when compared with cities of a similar charactt r and population. As I stated last year, this is undoubtedly caused by the discrepancies in administration in different places. In this city public opinion requires a high standard of sobriety, and I hope this taiga standard will become far higher, so that when a man has departed in any sense from sobriety he will be called "drunk," and ntt described as "under the inflaence of drink." Drunkenness is clearly a matter of opinion ; no standird test can be applied. The circumstances of each case and the responsibility of the individual at the time must be taken into consideration. But I feel satitfinl that the recognition by the authorities during the past year of term " not drunk in a police court sense" will in future raise grave difficulties in the s'rict administration of the licc using laws throughout the country,</p>
        <p>HUNTING THE RED DEER IN NEW ZEALAND. In " Blackwood's Magazine" Mr A E Leatham dis courses most eloquently on" Stalking the Red Deer of Otago." As one who has been following that sport for ten years past—he clairila to have been the first, Englishman to Ehoot in the Hunter River Valley, New Zealand—he expresses a doubt whether anything in the way of sport can surpass it. " Gorgeous scenery ! A climate so bracing that a man can walk from sunrise to sunset without being unduly tried ! Glorious trophies in the foul of stags' horns far surpassing anything to be found in England! What more can the heart of a hunter desire ? " Mr Leatharn speaks enthusiastically of the size and quality of the heads that fall to the successful deer stalker, and declares that they are equal to any of the forest heidi of Contral Europe and far superior to those of any stags in Groat Britain at the present day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>SHIPPING.</head>
        <p>SHIPPING.</p>
        <p>Talking of comparisons leads us to our last 3ubject—Liverpool. The Mersey seaport is a port and nothing else, and no other big city in the world can omparc with it in it absolute and anal dependence upon its Shipping for its life. There .arp over a million people in the Liver- pool district, and the only trade which sup- ports them, directly and indirectly, is its</p>
        <p>shipping. Not that they haven't trlea other industries. Attempts- have been made to estab• lish cotton mills, but, though Liverpool is the cotton importing centre of the country, with- out success. Shipbuilding has had a very hard struggle to progress, and is now looking up a, bit after years of no great activity. Sugar re- fining factories and engineering works are very few in number. No, Liverpool relies altogether on. its Shipping.</p>
        <p>And whilt a Port it is! Liverpool lie; securely entrenched behind granite (lock walls which stretch in adamantine strength 'eight miles along Mersey's side. Those docks, ever being extended at a cost of several millions of pounds every three or four years, accommodate the biggest liners and the smallest coasters, speed vessels hurled by steam through the water at record rates, and sailing vessels which all the time form a stately, graceful protest against threatened gradual extinction.</p>
        <p>What Liverpool's shipping -Means to her may be gathered from the fact that in the year end- ing June 30th, 1907, the tonnage entering the port was over 17,000,000, an increase. of a million over the tonnage for the preceding year. Com- pare that with the r2turns for 1835, when the tonnage was just over a million And three- quarters. There are other facts. . Liverpool, with ships on-an average larger than those of any other port, conducts a third of the export trade and a quarter of the import trade of the United Kingdom. She owns a third of the country's total shipping, apd—think of it—a seventh of the total registered shipping of the world. One of every 10 Ships that sail the seas belongs to Liverpool, the most remarkable of the one trade tow-is in all the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>RINGWORM VERY BAD ON HEAD.</head>
        <p>RINGWORM VERY BAD ON HEAD.</p>
        <p>18, Woodside-road, Toubridge, Kent, England.— " A little over two years ago my little boy, age 91 years, had ringworm appear very bad on his head, chiefly on the top. His little bead was quite bald and thick layers of scurf kept rising. I tried some ointment but that did little or no good. I began to feel quite disheartened until I saw about the free sample of Cuticura Soap and Ointment and made up my mind to try them. To my surprise I could see a great improvement in only trying the sample of Cuticura 5( ap and Ointment and I therer,re got one box of Ointment and the same of Soap, but before I had finished using them his head was beautifully clean and even the hair began to grow thick. There is no scurf or anything now. Cuticura Soap and Ointment rid him of the trouble. _ _</p>
        <p>"My other boy, age 7, had a nasty breaking out around his mouth. I put a little Cuticnra Ointment on, two nights in succession and that quite cured his mouth." (Signed) Mrs M. Tench, June 2nd, 1912. Cuticura Soap and Ointmeht are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post- free from nearest depot: Newbery, 27, Charterhouse- square, London; R Towns &amp; Co., Sydney, N.S.W. ; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay; " Cutioura," Boston, U.S.A. Tender-faced men should shave with Cutieura Soap Saving Stick. Sample free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>DOCTORS AND THE PANELS.</head>
        <p>DOCTORS AND THE PANELS.</p>
        <p>Still a Chalice Left.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday, Mr Bowerman (Deptford, Lab.), asked whether in view of the decision arrived at last Saturday by a representative meeting of the meml.ers of the British Medical Association it ens intended to allow further time for the completion of the doctors' panels, and also if it were intended to set up local medical committees? Mr Lloyd George replied that so far as be was aware the panels were still open throughout the greater part of the United Kingdom, and doctors might send in their names for addition to them. The date of the publication of these names rested alone with the local committees. Application for recognition by local medical committees could at any time be sent to the Insurance Commissioners for approval. Mr Mastermart informed Dr Esmond (N. Tipperary, Nat) that every insured person had a right to select the practitioner on the panel by whom be wished to be attended, and the issue of any cirmilar which concealed that fact would be unjustifiable. If he had brought to his notice any actual case in which a circular had been issued stating that members were required by the society to select certain doctors who were named he would see if any action should be taken. Mr William Thorne (South-West Ham, Lab) : How are people to get medical benefit if there be no panel n_a district ?</p>
        <p>Mr Maste-rm- an : With a few tiny exceptions, I think panels are in operation in all districts. In every Case doctors are provided for insured persons. Mr Barnes (Blackfriars, Lab.), asked whether, in the case of an insured person who had been certified for sanatorium treatment, and for whom accommodation could not be found, the National Insurance Commis- • stoners had any power to render that person financial or other assistance pending his enery into a sanatorium. Mr Mastermau : Yes, sir. If there should be any such eases, both financial and other assistance would he provided for during any period between an insured Person becoming incapable of work and his actual entry into a sanatorium. He would receive domiciliary treatment, including both medical attendance and the provision of medicines, and also extra nourishment, auxiliary to the treatment,and he would also be entitled under the ordinary conditions to sickness benefit nominally of 103 a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>CARIBALDI VETEERAN.</head>
        <p>CARIBALDI VETEERAN.</p>
        <p>Mr George Goodall, J.P., 'who has just died at Farnham, Surrey, served as a captain under Garibaldi, leading the EngliFh and French companies at the battle of Solano. Ho had served in the Scots Greys and the 7th Dragoon Guards, and volunteered for service under Gordon in suppressing the Taiping rebellion.</p>
        <p>U</p>
        <p>WHIST I successf Oughtring servative "Ladies E ConFervat Of membei and dance for dancin Choice ref the evenin</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Don't "wait and see" if that cold is Coming=</head>
        <p>Don't "wait and see" if that cold is Coming— =</p>
        <p>=</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>Give</head>
        <p>Give</p>
        <p>ONE-TRADE TOWNS.</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGHT.]</p>
        <p>It is only in the natural order of things that a large proportion of the larger towns and cities of this kingdom should be associated peculiarly with one or other of the manifold industries which have made this the world': premier trading nation. In our callow days at school, when we .learned by rote the names of our counties, capitals, and chief towns, we were supposed to learn at the same time their par- ticular claims to note in the matter of produc- tive activity. Thus we informed ourselves that Nottingham is famous for lace, Paisley for shawls. Cheshire for cheese, Kidderminster for carpets, Northampton for boots, Melton Mow- bray for pies, Ormskirk for gingerbread, Bel- fast for linen, and so on. But the towns mentioned are scarcely one- trade towns. They produce in great quanti- ties the commodities for which they are famous, but those manufactures do not at all complete the sum of local industry. There are several towns, however, in this kingdom which stand or fall by the condition of one particular in- dustry. All readers have heard of Port Sun- light, the model town built near Liverpool for thousands of people who are engaged in the manufacture of Sunlight soap; and the model town of Bourneville, near Birmingham, is no less well-known as the home of the myriad workers occupied in turning out Cadbury's cocoa and chocolate. These are one-trade towns of the kind we are concerned with in this article, although they are the only two " model " towns run in a particular firm's interests that we shall deal with. STRAW PLAITING.</p>
        <p>The straw plaiting industry is the only one flourishing in a big way at Luton. It has been established there a long time, and has had periods of astonishing prosperity, as, for in- stance, in the decade which followed the year 1851, when Luton's population increased by 50 per cent. Dunstable had a cut in at the busi- ness and seriously. threatened Luton's position once, but Luton still leads by a long way, and uses all effort possible, including the teaching of the idea in the technical schools, to further extend its industry, which has a history dating from the time of James 1., and now supports a population of 30,000 persons. ENGINE BUILDERS,</p>
        <p>Crewe is a specially interesting example of the one-trade town. Were it not for the great locomotive works established by the London and North-Western Railway Company there—well, there would be no Crewe worth talking about. It was in 1843 that the great works wete estab- lished, and it was in 1901 that the 4,COOth engine was turned out there: Seven thousand to ten thousand men are employed in the numerous departments necessary to the casting of steel rails and the making of the various forms. of iron and steel work which go to the building of a railway engine. Hundreds of engines are, of course, always at Crewe being repaired, for they must always be kept in the pink of condi- tion as it were, and it is necessary to this end that they should be thoroughly overhauled fre- quently and the wear and tear of a few weeks' hard work on the iron rail made good. Crewe railway station is among the busiest in the world, and'it has one extraordinary feature :n that there are miles of rails in subterranean ways, these having been constructed to allow of the passage of the enormous quantities of goods traffic, which would otherwise hamper the fliers concerned in the widely radiating passen- ger traffic. Libraries, institutes, reading and assembly rooms, schools—all these and other in- stitutions were established at Crewe by the railway company which gives the town its vitality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>ONE TRADE AND OTHERS.</head>
        <p>ONE TRADE AND OTHERS.</p>
        <p>It is difficult to 6ay of cotton or mining or engineering that either absorbs the sole energies of the population of any town in particular. We might point to Oldham, famous for its spinning, or to Burnley, which has more looms to the square mile of population as it were than any other town; we might allude to Wigan as Coalopolis; Hartlepool or Jarrow for shipbuilding; Middlesborough or Wolverhamp- ton for ironworks. But all these places have other industries of considerable importance. Oldham has collieries and a great engineering works employing seven thousand hands.; Burnley has collieries; Wigan has several large cotton mills, and so on.</p>
        <p>Edinburg is nearly .a one-trade town, for the printing business there has attained exceedingly large proportions. Stoke and district, the pottery centre, has the one industry of manu- facturing earthenware. Woolwich is -another town which may well be included in the series. It is the Arsenal town. Latterly there has been a considerable decline in employment, but the Arsenal, in full swing, occupies the energies of 12,000 men in making all sorts of guns, military waggons, shot and shell, cartridges, bullets—everything almost for use in the work of annihilating opposing armies. It is the only Government gun factory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>GLASSOPOLIS.</head>
        <p>GLASSOPOLIS.</p>
        <p>Among our one trade towns we may include St. Helens. No, not for .pills, but for glass. There are collieries there and in the surround- ing district, where some thousinnds of men are employed, and the chemical industry is still important where once it was tremendously prosperous. But the town itself depends on its glassworks. St. Helens is the one centre in England where plate-glass i 9 made, and in the making of it, at what are known as the Cowley Hill works, belonging to the great Pilkington firm, 3,000 men or more are engaged. Then the sheet-glass works engage the attention of several thousando more, and the scenes to be witnessed there day by day are remarkable. The writer well remembers his first view of the works as he arrived in St. Helens at night and saw, from the railway embankment, great shafts of light from the furnaces shooting out into the black sky. And moving in and out among the glare were the half-clothed men, wielding their blow pipes and looking for all the world like the denizens of an underground flame country—if the reader understands what is meant. The gla,s-making industry was com- menced at S. Helens at the time that the Huguenots came over from France, but its chief growth has been within the last century, and now 50,000 of the people of the town are depen- dent upon it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>CUTLERS' TOWN.</head>
        <p>CUTLERS' TOWN.</p>
        <p>Sheffield may be said to be a one-trade town, and everybody knows what that trade is—cut- lery. Yet there are great iron and steel works there also, and sew ral collieries employing be- tween them a few thousand hands. But cutlery, employing some 23,000 men and 4,000 to 5,000 women', is in ito many forms the one trade which has made Sheffield famous throughout the world, the one town which stands as a pat- tern to the world's other cutlery towns, which flatter by nicknaming themselves after the cut- lery centre of Hallamshire. The ease of Solin- gen is one in point, for that town calls itself the "Sheffield of (lemony." Sheffield's progress in the matter of cutlery has been Birming- ham's loss, for while that wonderful Warwick- shire town of many trades fit ill produces cutlery, it does not now attempt to dispute with the Yorkshire centre its position in the matter or cutlery as it threatened to do at one time. As one might say, Sheffield cut Birming- ham out almost.completely. We have alluded to Birmingham as a town of many trades, but it might almost be said that Hi; 'many trades are ono, that of manufac- turing and dealing in a thousand and one</p>
        <p>articles of hardware. Because of this Birming- ham is another of those great British hives of industry which stand pre-eminent in this country ond•all countries. No town elsewhere can compare with it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>4 'A • c•_, • • – 0.0 ICT 4. r s 1, r –</head>
        <p>4 'A • c•_, • • - 0.0 ICT 4. r s 1, r -</p>
        <p>MR. BRIAN CROSSLEY'S ENGA GEM ENT.</p>
        <p>engf,!,ge-</p>
        <p>ment is announced of Brian, youngest son of tbe late Sir William Crossley, of Glenfield, Altrincharn, acd Pull Woods, Ambleside, ex-M.P. for the Altrincham division of Cheshire, and _Margaret Lilian, eldest (laughter of Mr J W Sidebottom, of Merlewood, Bowdon, ex-M.P. for the Hyde division of Cheshire.</p>
        <p>LADIES' HOCKEY.— TIMPERLEY V LYM3l.—The I yim Ladies visited Timperley on Saturday,and but for their sound defensive tactics would have sustained a much 111010 serious reverse than three goals to nil. Timperley have not lost a match this season, and- have not even had,a goal scored against them. The winners were best represented by Mips Skinner at outside left, and Miss C. Fitton at centre-forward, who scored two goals. Miss Smethurst also plsyed veil at half. Of the defence Miss Armstrong and Miss E. Fitton were the pi,z,k, hitting clean and hard. Result : Timperley Ladies, three goals ; Lymm Ladies, nil.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODISTS.— On Sunday the Lyrom Primitive Methodist Society were highly favoured with a visit from the President of Conference. the Rev Thomas Jackson, founder and superintendent of the Whitechapel Mission. At the morning service be captured the attention of the children with a short address on " Cleau bands," he afterwards preached a convincing missionary sermon from Acts xviii., 9 and 10. In the evening Mr Jackson preached from Acts xx., 33. He described Paul's preaching, its theme aud results. After dealing with the faith and fullness of his teaching • his humility and unselfishness ; his sympathy and tenderness, the preacher spoke on the words It is more blessed to give than to receive." The choir rendered the anthem "In Jewry is God known." Mr Jackson afterwards gave an address at the lantern service in the adjoining schoolroom on the subject of his work in the White- chapel slums- A quartette was sung by Messrs R Dixon. W Dixon, J Ellis and J Moss. There were good attendances at all the services and the visit of the President was appreciated by an who heard him. HOCKEY CLUE DANCE.--On Wednesday the Lymm Ladies' Hockey Club held their third annual dance. Mr William Skinner's Orchestra, from Altrincham, discoursed sweet music and dancing began about 8 p.m. and was kept up with brief intervals for refreshments until 2 a.m. The night was inclement, snow having fallen for some hours, but although this had a little effect upon the attendance there were present about 100 guests, who made up in enthßsiasm for any small falling off in numbers. The catering and decorating had been most effectively left in the hands of Misses NI Kirkpatrick, F Cadman, N Hurst, J Pearson, M Hazlehurst (the energetic Secretary of the club). A Guest, M Reece. N Pearson, H Goffins Boond, E Walton, and E Moore, while other helpers were Mesdames Cadman, Barnes and Guest. The M.C.s who were most indefatigable in their attention to duty were Messrs. John and Jas Pearson, and W Critchley, the Stewards being Sergeant Fitzpatrick and Messrs. W Hinton,P Holt, J Elliott, R Cnorlton, E Lecomber and Alfred Booth. The affair was most enjoyable and interest ing,and dispite the fact that dancing was almost continuously indulged in regret was general at the end that it had concluded. OUGFITRINGToN INFANT'S SCHOOL.—On Thursday an entertainment was given by the children of Oughtring- ton Infant School and was a marked success. Owing to the Rector (Rev E P Wethered) being away from home the chair was taken by Mr T Nash. The first part of the programme consisted of an "Opening speech" by Miss Ena Armstrong ; recitation "My nursery friend" by boys and girls; song "Aunt Jerusha's Cat" selected children; recitation "Grandma's Advice" Edith Wilson ; song by the Babies entitled " Chncsa- dees "• garland song and dance by girls; and fan drill r4y babies. After a short interval a play entitled "The Butterfly Queen " was given, into the ipirit of which the children entered most heartily. The characters were well sustained as follows :—Jack, a Boy Scout, Jack Marsden; 11illy, a Girl Scout, Elsie Booth: TweedlOdum and Tweedledee, Irrepressible Twins,Eric Harrison and John Bell; Wood Ogre, One of the worst, Percy Buckley; Butterfly Queen, One of the best, Hilda Jells; Fairy attendants, Ivy Tippin and Ena Armstrong; Dame Snuffy, a sharpe old lady, Gertrude Smith ; with Boy Scouts—imps—chorus of school children and fairies. The irrespressible twins received special approbation from the audience. The Chairman said special thanks were due to Miss Bowyer for the amount of time and patience which she had bestowed in order to achieve the success of the entertainment. OLD PEOPLE AND WIDOWS' TEA. AND CONCERT, OEGHTRINGTON.—AnnuaI treat to the old folks of sixty years and upwards,also widows,was given in Oughtring- ton School on Saturday evening and was most success- ful, a splendid tea and entertainment being thoroughly enjoyed. Before the concert commenced the Rev. E P Wethered (Rector) said he was sure all present would wish that a hearty vote of thanks be accorded to the members of the Men's Bible Class of St. Peter's Oughtrington, and a few friends, who had made such excellent arrangements and this was carried with acclamation. The Rector then announced that Mr T J Ridgway had kindly offered a prize of 2s nd to the oldest lady and a similar sum to the oldest gentle- man and that the Bible class had also given a sum of 5s each for the oldest lady and gentleman. These prizes were given to Mrs Newton of Hadley Hill (90), and Mr Wood of Oughtrington (88). A further prize of 2s 6d bad also been anonymously given by a friend for the oldest married couple and this was obtained by Mr and Mrs Wood, whose aggregate ages were 175 years. In addition another kind gentleman friend from Heatley presented ten dozen handkerchiefs for distribution to all present and there were very much appreciated by the old people. In the interval, in addition to an apple and orange each, the old gentlemen were presented with a packet of tobacco. The old ladies, and those old gentlemen who did not wish for tobacco, received packets of tea.A varied entertainment was afterwards given which opened with a fine pianoforte duet by Messrs W and S Jonas, and songs were excellently rendered by Miss L Bolton, and Messrs F Ingham, J Bolton, A B Starkey, and T H Peters. Miss Rushton obliged with two recitations, while Master F Rutter's violin solos w.-re given with great skill. Mr S Aukland of Warrington was a most capable humorist and his Lancashire yokel sketch in which he introduced his concertinas created great enthusiasm nod was highly appreciated. Mr 0 G Smith kindly acted as accompanist. During a short interval in the concert selected chocolates. etc., were handed round. Motor cars were employed to bring Vie old folks up to the treat and also t °take them home and on all hands expression was heard of a very enjoy- able evening having been spent. An ample share of the delicacies provided for tea and refreshments was sent to those who were unfortunately prevented through sickness from being present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>WRIST DRIVES.—The Sandy-lane Social Club held a successful whist drive in the Infant's schoolroom, Oughtrington' on Wednesday night. The Lymm Con- servative and Constitutional Association and Club " Ladies Evening " came to a happy termination in the Conservative Club on Wednesday, when a large numver Of members and friends participated in the whist drive and dance that had been arranged. Selections of music for dancing were played by Mr .T Boothroyd's Baud. Choice refreshments also added to the enjoyment of the evening. FREE CHURCH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>With the purpose of creating a greater interest in the, Work of Lymm Free Church Council, a public noseting was held in the Primitive Methooist schoolroom on Friday, the 17th inst., when the chief speaksrs were the Rev J Ll Jones thou secretary of the Cheshire Federation of Free Church Councils) and Mr F G Skenaptou, both of Chester After the singiag of a hymn and prayer bad been offered by Mr it Hewitt, the chairrean (Rev RW B Whiteway) gave his address. He Bpdke of the notion that some people had, that the Free Church Council was a political organisation. This is not so, it is for the corporating of the Churches in this , country to work for the saving ot mankind. Speaking of the similitude of methods of work and °icier of service he said that it was the aim of the council to bring the Churches closer together, for though no chuich has perhaps any distinctive work, yet unity is most necessary. "I think that our preseot divisions do ' harm and the sooner we come closer together the better we shall do our work." He said that he had done his best to foster this spirit and tliought that the services held that week had been the best they had ever had. Mr J D B Smith, tee Hon. Secretary, conveyed the regrets of the local President, Mr Wilterworth, at his being unable to attend the meeting. He spoke in eulogistic terms of Mr Whiteway's connection %%Rh the Lymm Free Church Council and expresred his sorrow that that gentlemen would shortly be leaving Lymm. On behalf of Mr Waterworth he read the treasurer's report, after which he gave that of the secretary. He said that they had arranged special services, passed resolutions on the Welsh Distablishment Bill, Peace Proposals, Education question, Sunday Rifle Practice, and White Slave Traffic Act. Though the free Church Sunday Schools had not bad their Walking Day last Year it was hoped that it would be resumed this summer, A coal strike relief tund had been organised but owing to the short time the strike lasted they bad not many cases to deal with. A registrar of marriages for Lymm had been appointed as a result of their efforts, and they had tried to obtain Sunday closing, but owing to the inadequate laws, they had been unsuccessful. He said that some people seemed to think that the Free Church Council was of no 'use, but he believed that it could do more if the men- of the Churches would take a greater interest in it, there was plenty of work to do, and were It not for the Council such a meeting as the present one could not Sieve been held. The reports were adopted on the motion of Mr S Moss, seconded by Mr S Linley. ISlr F 14 Saempton gave a lucid address on the Free Churches of this country. He spoke of the d)ing out Of the unfriendliness and suepicion that used to exist amongst the Nonconformist Churches, and said that to great extent it had come about through Mr W uladstone's protest against the Turkish atrocities, e hen the Ncneooformista in supporting him discovered what a Power they were when united. He said that their being united did not affect the rights that their forefathers had stood for. He told how he, though a Baptist, had preached in tee pulpits of other denominations. He said that though their eh.lects were not political but religious, it behoved them to band thenoelves together to fight against any °POosition that arose. For instance, the Education queation seems to be in the background at present, but Nse are only waiting our time, and now that we are laf °re United we shall achieve greater results in the ?titre. In relation to the Foie° proposals it was the "etY of Nonconformiata to raise a united voice agsinst war 00 any p,stees ; it was for such purposes as these that the Free Church Council existed. Speaking of !fork done in the foreign field be said that the various ueaorainational missionaries used to he bitter towards one another, but now they worked together, :3:1 thus the more friendly feeling that ,ar 7slS,ted amongst the Free Courches at home rt t e work of the missionaries easier. Dealing urgeeith the importance of unity the speaker that all the Free Churches of Caeshire should be osiets•county federation and these county federations Canrseireisned with the national one, it enabled the Free as was of the country to speak with one quick voice stoppedthe.o49o when the Johnson-Jeffries fight was Shall work Now that we are more banded together we ,rh „ better in the future." e "evl Ll Jones was the next speaker. He Prefaced his arbiress by explaining that the president of the Cheshire Federation. Mr .1 S Machin, of Stockport _trees unable to ha present owing to a serious illness fot expressed his pleasure at seeing such a large audience and coogratulated the secretary on his report. ate said that the Lymm Council were fortunate in Possessing such a valuable worker as Mr Smith, and hoped that all would rally round him. He then spoke at, length on the constitution rules, and object of ssthe Cheshire Federation of 'Free Church Councils. I 1 emphasised its freedom from political bias, and stated that the churches were linked together in such a WeY that each body preservsd its identity. He showed that the Federation meaet economy;a demonstration of harmony;Y and a production of greater efficiency. He said that there was a comradeship in the rsovement.and described how the weakest church was Connected eP by the federations to the Parliament. "This Union laates courtesy between the churches,subdues jealousy, !aspires hie:Alin' se, and makes us work together for the advance of the Church of Christ • it is not a nominal union for it is based on the Lord Jesus Christ s.rnd the cornmon experience of His healing power. The le.ee Church Council is one of the most spiritual and moral bodies in the world, for before a person can be _elected to it, he or she must first he a member of a Before concluding an able address the speaker said that he hoped God would bless all the efforts they PUt forth' in the work of Jesus Christ. 3 fir L Welford moved a vote of thanks to the Rev Jones Mr Shempton and Mr J D B Smith, thie NV " se °u' did cond ed by Mr J'H Holt, who however added or name of the cheirman to the resolution. This ill'orse oder' resolution was put to the meeting by Mr H S We, and heartily carried. furoVl QUEEN FESTIVAL.—For the purpcse of making the Lymm May Queen Committee held a second s_ . al .ver reduction on the deficit sustained on the last sesore !' argutitt.t in tbe Congregational schoolroom on Saturday proyasst• On this oocasion the entertainment was glom "erl by the " Musical Mountebanks, "The pro- w_ ine, which was of a varied nature was thoroughly kij°3'etir Many encores being demanded and kindly given. edvsaslinnie Porter's sweet soprano voice was heard to " yo;i2,a,ge in such pieces as the "Waltz Song," " a better ask me." " The dream of home," and gnit love o in tu o • With as ; and e operatic duet me rP W Anderson," Keys of Heaven," and "Tell acce Ptst:tt mew en.,,Miss Edith Hudson was an He:" ble mezzo soprano, her rendering of "My 4i appla,udedweetheart,' and My moon " being well burle • Mr J Bridgford, refined humorist and o Nee artiste has been aptly described as the eof the party"' whether it was in his humorous special i When father papered the parlour," in o me whist drive," and" Socks," or Mare? 73edy duets with Miss Hudson, "Single v ttred. " you love me just the same or , to_ r arm," and "The modern maid," or • O ,13roeoother item in which he took part, he was always mte f a hearty reception from the audience, his songs, Wst s r, arid actions evoking roars of laughter. Mr P • nderson h lusose . as a good bass voice and did himself Ton, „,ln the songs "Glorious Devon," and "Young thOms u Devon." Tbe party were fortunate in securing be:Cdenrvicss of Mr C M Margerison as pianiste for Ines. helos a clever accompanist he contributed the the locaidl sketches "Married v Single," and "The night chareste cow died." Mr W Hockney was a clever Carton ir inpersonater, hut items such as " Sydney Murder af‘ 1" only way,' and "Mathias" in "The cert like° the Polish Jew," were too heavy for a con- , sarieh p his. His monologue and representation of felloss. 'est) were much better. The party gave the easne:lng concerted items in a fery praiseworthy Russian "r, "Mountebanks are we," " Hey-lack-a-day " quartette, "Swank," and ` On a cold and frosty lave •t m being a capital burlesque - • •</p>
