      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>CROFT.</head>
        <p>CROFT.</p>
        <p>PRIZE DISTRIBUTION.—The prizes gained by the Sunday school scholars and members of the choir were distributed in Christ Church °a Sunday afternoon. Mr J Ball (senior warden) handed the awards to the successful scholars, for whom he had kind and encour- aging words. The Rector (Rev H I Wadeson) also took advantage of the occiston to present to Mr T Hurdus (organist), a valuable fountain pen as a slight token of the esteem in which he is held, and for his services on behalf of the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH WHILE AT WORK.-Mr. Frank Kelly, who has lived in Orford for a considerable number of years, died very suddenly on Wednesday morning, presumably from heart failure. He was employed as n lodge-keeper at the Vulcan Foundry, Newton-1e- Willows, and had to be at his work at 5.40 in order to let the men in. He was a bit late and had to hurry to</p>
        <p>. get there in time. He collapsed shortly after opening his lodge, and passed away without regaining conscious- ness. Deceased was 61 yesrs of age, and leaves a widow and grown-up family. all resident in London. The body was removed to the mortuary to await the inquest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>RISLEY.</head>
        <p>RISLEY.</p>
        <p>ODLIFELLOWS' ANNUAL MEETING,—The membPrs of the " City of Refuge " Lodge of the Loyal Order of Odd fellows held their annual meeting at the Noggin Inn on Saturday evening. Mr. H. Taylor presided.— Mr James Henshaw (secretary) submitted the balance sheet for 1012, which showed that the society is still progressing. There are 165 members, and the average amount of funds per member is .£l.l 16s 11d. The sick benefit for the year had been very heavy. 38 members having received sick pay amounting to £135 ss. The funeral benefits amounted to .1:17. The old age pension scheme, by which the net gain of the society is divided among the members aged 65 and upwards, has again been most satisfactory, the sum of £.lB 13s id having been divided among 22 members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WHIST DRIVE AND DANCE.—A very pleasant even- ing was spent at the Cottage Homes on Monday by the ofticers,and a large number of guests who were present by their invitation. The whist drive proved very enjoyabie. The ladies prize was won by Mrs Green, the consolation prize going to Mrs Hodgkinson. The victorious gentlemen being 1, Mr E Young; consola- tion, Mr Yorke. Dancing was indulged in to the accompaniments played by 'Mr Wainwright with his accustomed skill. The Superintendent Mr Lawrence making a capable M.C. Several members of the Board of Guardians were present. During the evening excellent refreshments were served under the direction of the Matron Mrs Lawrence. WESLEY GUILD.—A lecture was delivered by Dr Bennett on Monday evening, dealing with a tour which he took, in company with several local friends. in Switzerland a short time ago. The lecture was illustrated by a remarkably fine series of original slides, prepared from photographs taken by the lecturer and other mem ters of the party and shown with great distinctness , by Mr Parkinson who manipulated the lantern with his usual ability. Dr Bennett excelled himself in descrip- tive powers and his racy anecdotes lent additional interest to a subject so full of charming beauty. A vote of thanks to the lecturer was enthusiastically carried on the proposition of Mr Newton, seconded by Mr Proffitt. The President (Rev E Morgan) occupied the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>MORE "OLD WARRIit!GTON" GOING.</head>
        <p>MORE "OLD WARRIit!GTON" GOING.</p>
        <p>Photo Burrell.</p>
        <p>TENANTS OF THE SELDOM SEEN PROPERTY WHICH WILL BE DEMOLISHED TO MAKE ROOM FOR THE NEW WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Municipal improvements inevitably mean the disappearance of old landmarks. The above is a photograph of one of several houses in Fairclough's-yard, which will shortly be demolished to allow of the widening of the Knutsford-road approach to Warrington Bridge, The tenant—Mrs Ellen. Easting—has lived there for forty- eight years. Her notice to leave expires next week and she naturally regards the approaching change of environment as a severe wrench. Chatting with Mrs. Easting on Wednesday afternoon, an " Examiner " reporter found it difficult to believe that she is in her eighty-eighth year. Mentally she is remarkably alert and her physical appearance suggests a wiriness which can successfully withstand the stress of a hard life. Her only regret is that, owing to an accident which befell her eight years ago, she is except on very rare occasions, compelled to spend the whole of her time indoors. Mrs. Eastings at one time used to work in Bold-street and, while following her duties, she had the misfortune to fall from a step ladder, seriously injuring her ankles. She was a patient in the Infirmary for two months. The last time Mrs. Easting ventured out of the" yard" was on Walking Day when she went to the top of the entry to watch the procession of Sunday school children. With a twinkle of satisfaction, she recalled the incredulity of the pensions officer, on first hearing that she was over eighty years of age. The production of the care- fully treasured birth certificate was, however, indisputable evidence and Mrs. Easting is in receipt of the weekly ss. assured under Mr. Lloyd George's beneficent scheme. Mrs. Easting is a native of Birmingham but came to Warrington forty- eight years ago, along 'with her husband, the late Mr. Charles Easting who worked as a 'wiredrawer at Messrs. Edlesten's works. He died eight years ago and since then Mrs. Easting has been in the care of two of her daughters who are most devoted to her. She has had a family of ten, of whom five daughters and two sons are living. Two of the daughters are in America.</p>
