      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>WOM ENS LIBERAL Assoc lATION,—Under the auspices of this society a public meeting was held on Thursday evening in Providence schoolroom. Mrs Roberts pre- sided and a very aide and informing address was given by Mrs King. Upon the subject of Home Rule Mrs Kicg is peculiarly well fitted le speak as she is an Irish lady whose early years were spen!, in trelani and who remembered Sir Edward Carson among her child- hood acquaintances. She devoted her tvidress to the subject of Home Rule arid completely held the interest of the meeting as she sketched the history of the Celtic people across the charnel from the earliest period. Upon the motion of Mr Sherhuru supported by Mr Longland and Mrs Sherburn. Mrs Kiag was thanked for her able address.</p>
        <p>ST. THOMAS' PAROCHIAL TEA.—Man'y interesting functions are held in connection with St. Thomas' Churn)), Stockton Heat • but it is questiooable whether any are quite so delightful as the parochial tea and entertainmertt which ass held this year on Thursday evening. About 100 m.u.bers sat down to it substantial and well served tea and he atter proceedings took the form of a pleaFant enter:Rini-ilea at which the vicar, the Rev 3 Collins pret.ided. The folios big items were pleasingly given to he coomoarriments of Mrs Evans: Song " Toe appetite," Miss Evans; Recitation " 'Uhe street watchmen's s ry " Master Dunverband, coon song, "The (lamb, c loured co .ns," by the minstrel party ; song, " Valley of laughter " (t...4mnder- son) ; Miss Rodeik (p.,pit ..f Mrs Newcombe) who also rendered admiouly t h.. song "Star of Twilight (Crotty). A duol.,gue " Her lady..hin's new coat " w given most accep,a ly t., kirs G Ireland Blackburne and Miss Greenall The scene was the trying-on room at Out- ns, -street. A juvenile sketch " Red Riding, 11.. d " ...a4 also given by children of the Sunda N school, %lies Blanche Howard making it capital " Red Riding Hood " Niastor S Small acting the part (it he w ,If Toe children had been trained by the virs...e. Fawcett and Garnett. Miss Greenall rendered " slumber boat " and Miss Henshall also rec.t.d. Votr-s of thanks cencluded a pleasant evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES</p>
        <p>NEW MEDICAL OFFICER FOR WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Of six applicants who were interviewed, the Widnes Health Committee on Tuesday agreed to TPCOMIIIIO d to the Town Council the appointment of Dr Alnert Jones, of Ormskirk,tts medical offioer of health for the borough in succession to Dr S B etmer, .vtr . has been appointed to Chis, re . The salary i‘t £4OO 4 ywtr. For the last two d 3 half years Dr .les has beer, school medical officer to the Lancashire C.,unty Council and has also held tit., positiod of assist nt officer to the Ormskirk Workh,ruse, industrial sch ools, and isolation hospit I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Vk 1 DNES LIBERALS. CA.NDID.I3I:IO; MOM) VED BY J B</head>
        <p>Vk 1 DNES LIBERALS. CA.NDID.I3I:IO; MOM) VED BY J B</p>
        <p>M ARTIN DALE.</p>
        <p>The Executive Co,, mitre:. of the Wirires Division of the Liberal Association has I,en notified ny J B Martindale, of the firm of 'lestiirs Bell and C ~ woolen manufacturers, Manchester, flint he would hc:ept the candidature of the Widocs Division at the next election Mr Alexander Ur the Lord Advocate of Scotland, will address a mbeiing of Widnes Liberals in a very short time.</p>
        <p>KING TO VISI I' COTTON MILL. NOttCH-EISr LANCASUIRE itoU I'E.</p>
        <p>In view of the vi.it of the i.ug L Liteaihir‘, ot,ps were taken on vlonduy t • (I. tine the route of the Royal p•-•etetistion in N trt I.tta ca- shire. Lord De, ity's private es a. y, act', ru- panit-d by Mr Lane chief c•urtable of the county), Cant tin Or tierod deput‘-cni• f c notable) and Superintendent P.1:(1 .0, (of he lleediy Division', cot, ferreci with the p lice authorities ..t C I , Nelson, Pe.dihatn, Burlle3, And A,icringt too. Int t inaTion w, s got which will a twit of no immedia'e scheme to embrace these towns t-ieiag tormeti It is stated that wi•en a! Neitron the Rine, will inspect a tttofl ruol ds.voted to Vl.l m nutacture of sateens.</p>
        <p>PENSION ER PROSECU</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WOMAN'S BAPrIS 1/41</p>
        <p>;ER FIFICATE</p>
        <p>_ At. Runcorn on Mods, a Woman named Mary Ryan., 40, Surrey-street, was summoned for pr .ducing a fa!se certificate of baptiser, fer the put pt,se of oht aning en old age pension. Mr Gradv.ell, who defended, said that if the magis- trates thought this vas it puma face ense his client bad elected to go for trial. Mr Stocks, of Loudon, who prosvcutd, aid when the defendant made her claim Bile put in is ceriifi,iate which showed her name to be Ann ,t aria, end her age as 77. The case was that that was not her name, and that the was not that age. St;e 4.A3tained a ptnsion, but after inquiries it vas stopped. A torn} of appeal was put in by ber sun, who was a Runian Catholic priest, alleging that the Pension Comuitttett's decision vkas wrong. In July, 1904, the def,hclaut insured herself under the name of Mary Ra an, and her ago was given as 60. This corresponded with the figures on the mar iage certificate in 1876, whereupon her ag, was given as 28. Her name upon that document was Mary It her 'tau age was 77 she must have be n 41 when she was married. The case was dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>GRA 13 PENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRA 13 PENHALL.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF LIBERALS.—The annual busi- ness meeting of iLe Grappenball Liberals (Knutsford Division) was held in the Independent Methodist Coapel on Monday. Mr J Kenworthy presided over a good attendance and was supported by Mr .1 S Tomlin- son (Divisional hon. secretary), and Mr H Cliffe (Divisional secretary). A letter was read from Mr Georle Shaw resigning his position of secretary, owing to haying left the district, Mr Shaw was cordially thanked for his services to the committee in the past, Mr C Jackson Holmes was elected president, Mr J Kenworthy vice-president ; Messrs J T Rowlands and F Burrows, hon, treasurers, and Mr Frank Fox, hon. secretary. The old committee were elected en bloc, and with power to add to their number.- Ir, was decided to hold a public meeting in the Independent Methodist Chapel on March 17th next. It was also further decided to recommend to the General Council meeting that a Garden Fete be held in the district next summer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>ST. Hi- LENS.</head>
        <p>ST. Hi- LENS.</p>
        <p>STEEPLEJACK'S WIDOW GETS £3OO.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens County Court, on Wednesday, Mr Garner, Jon , applia.l for a compensation award on behalf ot Mts Janet Dean, the widow of a steeplejack, of Clarendon-road, Liverpool. He stated that the deceased man a a, employed on December 9th by Messrs Twelton and Sons on a chimney at St. Helens, when he fell on to the roof of an adjoining building, and died from his injuries. ['he case was unusual, because the respondents had failed to file any answer and tad told him that they had not insured deceased. They admitted that deceased earned £2a 5s a week, and there were five young children. The judge made an award for £3OO compensation, to be paid at £4 a month. ST. HELENS MAGISTRATES AND DRINK LICENCES.</p>
        <p>The St Helens Licensing Committee- and justices on Ttiesd3y held a meeting in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall, when the Licensing Committee was reappointed as follows :—The Mayor (Councillor J Crooks), Aldermen Martin, W W Pilkington, C J Bishop, Sir Joseph Beecham, Forster, J Green and A J Foote, and Messrs J B Leach, A H Mitchell, 3 A Collins, V J Cook, A R Pilkington, F Dromgoole ad A Rudd.</p>
        <p>Alderman Martin (chairm in) reported that since the last annual licensing meeting three beerhouses had been closed on compensation. Since 1898 tea new licences had been granted in the Borough and 82 extinguished, a gain of 72 to he magistrates. The magistrates passed resolutions recommending the Home Office to promote legislation to empower courts of summary jurisdiction to sentence youths net ween the ages of sixteon and twenty-one years, inclusive, to detention in a Borstal institution for a term of not less than one nor more than three years (1) when found guilty of committing any indictable offf,nce which may be dealt with summarily; (2) after convictiim under the Vagrancy Acts if the offender has been convicted previously by a court of summary jurisdiction for an indictable offence or any offence pu,,ishablb by imprisonment, or been dealt with under the Probation of Offenders Act 1897. At present</p>
        <p>magistrates in quarter sessions and judges of assize only have this power. Another resolution passed suggested to the Home Office that it is desirable that courts of summary jnrisdiction be empowered to order the whipping of m :le cildren under sixteen years of age with or without any other punishment. The Children's Act, 1908, has reduced the age to fourteen years. Previously the law allowed the magistrates to birch a boy up to sixteen years of age, and the St Helens bench desire to see this power restored. The justices also passed a resolutiodstating that they were of opinion that the publication of case; tried in juvenile cJurts is not in the interests of the offenders or the public and should be prohibited. If, however, reporters are allowed to be present the names and addresses of the offanders ought not be published.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>WITNESS AMR WITNESS.</head>
        <p>WITNESS AMR WITNESS.</p>
        <p>As we take up our newspaper week by week, we are struck by the hr arty, unmistakable way in which witness after witness speaks out as Mrs. Anderson does here. If these pelpio were living miles away we night take little ni,tice of them. But they are not. They ate local men and women. Their word is too easily proved to admit of ay doubt. They speak out in the hope that their experience may he a guide to others.</p>
        <p>Mrs. C Anderson, of 14, Church-street, Warrington, says : -" At one time I suffereu with very bad pains in the small of my back. I think I had taken a cold which affected my kidneys; every time I put my foot to the ground the pain was like a knife being driven into iny hack. "I was advised to use Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, and lam glad I did, for they cured me. What is more, I have been well ever since. it gives me pleasure to advise others to try Doan's Pills, for I know how excellent the medicine is for curing those terrible pains in the back. (Signed) (Mrs.) C Anderson " Price 2/9 a box, 6 boxes 13/9 of all dealers, or from Doster-McClellan Co 8 ;IV ells st.,Oxford-st., London ,TV Fon't ask for backache and kidney pills—ask DISTINCTLY for DOAN'S Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mrs. Anderson had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>LYMM WATERWORKS BILL.</head>
        <p>THE LYMM WATERWORKS BILL.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S POLL.</p>
        <p>As can readily be understood, the outstanding topic of conversation in the Lymin district during the past week has been the Lymni Parliamentary Bill. Feeling runs high, but as the time of the poll draws nearer the supporters of the Bill are becoming stronger in number many of the erroneous statements made by the opposition being exploded by the true facts of the case that are becoming more widely known. That the Council are desirous of giving all the information they can on the subject is shown by their holding public meetings in the different wards. On Wednesday successful meetings were held in the Broom Edge Mission Room and Oughtiington schools, whilst on Thursday even- ing the Lynim Ward meeting was held in the Parochial Schools: on Fiiday night the ratepayers weie addressed in the Statham Soho( ls and in Newfield Mission Room. The members of the Council attended the various meetings and spoke in support of the Bill. The opponents of the Bill held a kind of committee meeting at the Plough Hotel on Tuesday night, and an anonymous circular against the Bill is being distributed.</p>
        <p>The poll of electors with references to the resolution put to the public meeting of electors held on the Bth day of January 1913 in favour cf the promotion of the Lymm jrban Council Bill will bs taken to-day (Saturdly, January 25th, 1913), between the hours of 12 o'clock noon and S o'clock in the evening. The various polling places are as follows : Booth's Hill Ward: Zion Chapel Schoolroom, Cherry-lane, Lymm. Heatley Ward : Infants' Schoolroom, Sandy-lane, Heath y</p>
        <p>Boys' Schoolroom, Pepper-street,</p>
        <p>Lyn,m Ward: Lymm. Statham Ward: Infanta' Schoolroom, Statham.</p>
        <p>It is exi ected that the result of the poll will be declared bhortly after 9 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>MEETING AT OUGHTRINGTON PARK.</head>
        <p>MEETING AT OUGHTRINGTON PARK.</p>
        <p>Meetings in support of the Bill have been held during the week in various parts of the district, the first taking place at Broomedge on Wednesday evening where Messrs. R H Gotobed, 0 G Smith and the Rev B Thurston addressed a fair number of ratepayers. The same evening there was a large attendance in the St. Peter's schoolroom Oughtrington—which it was supposed would be one of the storm centres—but although one or two ques- tions were asked, the meeting passed off without any unpleasantness—indeed, judging by the hearty way in which votes of thanks were accorded at the close, one thought that even this supposed antagonistic part of the district, was convinced of the wisdom of the Council's proposals. The main point emphasised by all the speakers seemed to be that although members of the Council were continually at variance on other matters, they were unanimous in their opinion that for the benefit of the district as a whole, the Bill should be passed. As to what will really be the result. to-day's poll will decide. In St Peter's school, Mr T J Ridgway presided and others present during the meeting were the following members of Oa Council :—Mr W G Groves (chairman), Messrs G L Welford, 0 G Smith, J H Holt, R H Gotobed, and the Rev G B Thurston. Mr T Ridgway, jun (legal adviser), and Mr Beckett (consulting engineer ) were also in attendance.</p>
        <p>The Chairman emphasised the fact that they were met to consider the interests of the whole parish of Lymm and not one particular section. The palish was rather a curiously shaped one, for there was Lymui proper, the old Lymm, and then what were called , hamlets in the old days, Oughtrington, Reddisb, Broom- edge and Statham. The parish had grown in those different hamlets and the question now arose, whether the whole parieh was to have the waterworks or not? He hoped they would look at it in a broad light. It the Bill were not approved by the ratepayers ot Lyintn, from what he heard, it was quite likely the place might be exploited in another way. and it, might be a long time before the Lymin Council again had the chance of buying the waterworks and the bind. All water- works paid, and if the purchase was not carried through who was going to have the benefit which must arise from the waterworks? The Lymru Estates Develepment Company, as it wss called, or really Lever Bros., Ltd. would benefit if the Bill were not carried through. He quite approved of what the Council were proposing to do, and the more he had gone into the figures, the more be was convinced the Council were making a wise and prudent purchase. Mr 0 G Smith gave a lucid exposition of the Bill. At the outset he said the matter was one which, one way or another, was bound to affect Lymm for a long period of years. The very fact that Mr Ridgway approved or the Council's proposals was convincing enough to most people, because if there was a man noted everywhere for impartiality, perfect mental balance and an absence of prejudice, that man of pre- eminence was Mr Ridgway. (Hear, hear.) He (the speaker) had perhaps special claims to speak on the matter—(hear, hear)—because he happened to have been on the committee appointed to investigate that affair and meet the Dewhurst trustees. He was a member of that committee from the beginning so that anything he said was from full,intimate and accurate knowledge. He did not speak off the book. Be hoped all present had come with impartial minds and that there would be no attempt to score one way or the other. The Council did not wish to score over the ratepayers and he hoped they would not try to score over the Council. Such conduct would be open to severe reprobation. They wanted to get at the facts and he hoped they would always have one object in view—the public welfare. (Hear, hear.) The Council was the ratepayers' own choice. They were ; not self elected ;—there were no aldermen, even—and they did not get £4OO for their duties. (A Voice: "Of course not.") They all held different views socially, politically, and in other respects, but after having had access to the documents, the advantage of expert novice, and after long, exhaustive and patient investi- gation, they were—these men who differed so much in other matters—all unanimous that the Council would be guilty of a serious omission of duty if they ever thought of departing from the position they had taken up. They had agreed, that in the interests of the rate- p.yers of Lymm it was eminently wise that the water properties should be possessed by the Council. That part of the parish was a storm centre. (A Voice: "Not necessarily, it is all over.") However, continued the speaker, he found himself looked at somewhat askance by some of his friends. (A Voice : " Oh ! no.") Perhaps he was wrong—he was simply giving his impression, although he hoped he was wrong. He felt sometimes like Elijah must have felt on Carmel "I only am left and they seek my life to take it away.' (Laughter.) He had no desire to be on the Council so that he could have no personal interests to serve. Before plunging into figures, he would like to say that there was just a little desire on the part of some people to have a tilt at the Council, by getting to know what they were going to do and then oppose it. They had all heard of the story cf the irritable mother who said to her child "Mary go and see what Tommy is doing and tell him he mustn't." That seemed to him to represent, the attitude of a good many of his friends in that part of the world. (" No.") In 1874 the members of the Local Board took over the Gasworks and they were blamed like some people were going to blame the Council of to-day. %I, heo the Board bought the Gas- works, people said it was an imprudent,rash and uawise thing to do, but when he entered the district in 1880 the gas was Gs 8d per thousand, whereas to-day it was 3s per thousand—(a Voice : Largely owing to opposition ")—and 2s 9cl for power. Labour, along with the whole cost of production, was dearer, and yet they got their gas at half the price and still had a balance on the right side. If the " croakers " had been listened to, the Gasworks would not have been bought and Lymm would have been the poorer. The present Bill was not exclusively a water Bill, for it dealt with three main things. In addition to securing the water undertaking, there would be an extension of powers in respect to gas and the Bill also dealt with questions cencerning the general welfare of the district. If the Bill failed, the whole lot would go —the waterworks, increased powers tor gas, and the powers the Council were seeking for general district purposes. Mr Smith then showed how, in the long run, a saving would he effected by obtaining Parliamentary powers which allowed an extended period for the repayment of leans, rather than by making repeated application to the Local Government Board. After illustrating the fact that the rates were lower than ten years age, the speaker referred to what:he termed "a rather unfortunate letter " which appeared in the Press last week from the Rev G B Thurston relating to the legal expenses of the Bill. It stated that the legal expenses se•ould be £l,OOO. That might be, but no man could be sure as to the exact amount yet. When the legal expenses were known, they would be spread over a term of five years and the Bill specially provided that the expenses would be " whacked " out over the three interests concerned. By the provisions of the Bill the present generation would not to saddled with the whole expense, but those who came in 20, 80 or 40 years would have to bear their share. People had asked : Why not leave Warburton to work out its own destiny? If the Bill passed, it gave the Council the power— nothing more—to negotiate with .the people of Warbdrton and if they wanted it and if the people of Lymm thought they could supply the water or gas on economical terms, it would be done. Anotht r com- plaint very largely made—and to some extent he could sympathise with it—was, why were, the ratepayer; not informed earlier ? In reply to this complaint, he stated that the reason was that it was desireble that secrecy should be observed in the preliminary negotia- tions, as a lot of figures, data and documents had got out of tht INCouncil in the past and that was very dangerous. The speaker called attention to the fact that the waterworks were in the. hands of a private firm and, although they could not increase the charges</p>
        <p>it was a question as to whether they would ever decrease them. If the works came into the hands of the Council they would be worked in the interests of the ratepayers and the revenues would be used for the advantage of the people. If a person wanted a bath now it cost an extra 10s. per year. They urged people to be clean and when they kept clean they " clapped ' 10s. on their rates. That was our English way of encouraging cleanliness. If the lake got into the hands of private exploiters there was no telling what they would do with it, but if the Council com- pleted the purchase, it could be used to the advantage of the ratepayers and it would cost them nothing, because the water power was worth more than they would pay for the lake. If they ever went in for electricity they had the power there, ready, whereas if the lake passed into private hands they would have to pay heavily for it. ln conclusion he hoped they would endorse most enthusiastically the attitude of the Council. (Applause.) Questions having been invited Mr Fray, a member of the audience, said that he and others complained that they hadnot had the chance to go into the figures.(Hear hear.) He also stated that so far as the personal friendship was concerned, which Mr Smith had men- tioned he could assure the previous speaker that they would allow him to leave the room without any of them waiting for him with a bludgeon. (Laughter.) Mr Fray was referring to the price of gas having been. reduced from Gs Bd. to 3s. when the chairman pointed out that he must keep to the Bill. However, he said that if the ass was twice as good then as now, they were not effecting a great saving, Mr 1' Ridgway peiuted out that a copy of the figures of the bill could be purchased for Is. (rum the Council Offices.</p>
        <p>In reply to a question Mr Smith said he understood that the preent intention of the C,,uneil was to leave the lake as it was. The question of up-keep did not arise at present. If they had a revenue to handle, then they might be disposed to do something but he thought the present intention was not to incur any expenditure in that direction at all.</p>
        <p>Mr G L Welford agreed with that statement and in the course of a lengthy speech followed the lines adopted by Mr Smith. He remarked that if those present were ratepayers and property owners, so were the members of the Council. If the public were going to be hit by the scheme so were the members of the Council. Nc thing would happen to those present but what would happen to the councillors, They (the ceuneillofs) were all ratepayers and the majority of them were property owners while some of them were owners of cottage property in respect of whic'e they would have to pay the rates out of the rent. He stood before them as one who would be hit, and Lit hard if the scheme meant any addition in the rates. The opposition was asking the ratepayers to believe that they were going to add to the rates. Was it at, all feasible that he was going to be a party to a thing which would not be any benefit to the district and which was going to injure his pocket. In answer to the questioner who had asked why they had not been permitted to see the tlgures,MrWelford said,what right had they to publish figures in connection with the water undertaking if they were not going to buy it? They were permitted to have certain information on the understanding that they did not use it for any other purpose than for forming their own opinions as to whether it was a schema to be adopted in the interests cf the district as a whole. After possessing himself of all information he had come to the conclusion that the adoption of the scheme would be in the interests of the district. The ten members of the Council, who often differed on other subjects, had discussed the ques- tion of the tsaterworks at least 50 times. They had turned it inside out and done everything they could with it, and at the conclusion of it all, after contesting and differing at the various stages of progress, they had arrived at last, after six months of discussion and after possessing themselves of every possible kind of inform- ation, at the unanimous conclusion that they would be unfaithful to the ratepayers of the whole district if they allowed the opportunity to pass them of making the ratepayers, through the Council, the owners of the waterworks and lake, since they could not buy the waterworks without the lake. (Hear, hear.) He had not a penny of personal interests in the scheme, but in the intereste of the whole district he thought it advis- able that they should acquire the works. (Applause.) If they did not they would be placing a burden on the district for those who came in after years. Did the ratepayers prefer that the income from the waterworks should go out of the district to swell the dividends of a private firm which had no local interests whatever excepting getting money out of the con- sumer of water, or that the Council should acquire and work the soherue in the interests of all the ratepayers ? That was what the whole thing amounted to. Was it not far better to purchase the works and utilise the profits in paying off the interests and principal ? In time the works would become their own property, free from all cost. It would not be any burden on the ratepayers. If there was any extra rate at ell, it would be infinitesimal and that would only be for a short period. Were they pre- pared to let the £BOO go away when by the very money they had to pay to Lever Brothers they could be pay- ing off the purchase? He wanted that £BOO to be used by the district for their benefit. He was not there to paint pictures in the air, which had no substantial existence That £BOO would become £l,OOO if the developments proceeded in Heatley. If the waterworks were in their bands, they might be able to grant facilities to works. They were not out to prevent the development of works but to facilitate them. They were going to use the works for the public good and they were not going to place any obstacles in the way of developments. (Applause). They would increose the opportunities for workingmen to get plenty of work and good wages. They kwould be guilty of a great breach of loyalty to the ratepayers if they did not purchase the works. They were also unanimously ot the opinion that it would be a great mistake on the part of the ratepayers if they did not support the scheme and they would live to regret it. People had made mistakes of that kind and had had to pay dearly for them. They would have to pay immensely; more if they did not avail themselves of the oppor- tunity they had at. present. He appealed to them to. vote in favour of the Bill being proceeded with, so that they might complete the purchase of the water- works. (Applau3e). The Rev G B lhurston did not think there was any suspicion that the members of the Council were getting anything out of the business. He would feel he was a. traitor and worse than that, if he hesitated for a moment to come and speak to an audience of men of the district on that question. (Applause.) He was convinced in his heart of hearts that the scheme would be a good one for the benefit of the whole district. In regard to the rates, it seemed to him a grievous pity that people elected them to the Council, and when they were there doing their duty for the ratepayers, they should be regarded as people apart. He felt the matter of rates very keenly indeed. His income coming from the source it cud, meant that 25 per cent of hit income went in rates. No mau in Lymm would feel a rise in the rates more than he would. If it was a matter of advancing the rates was it commonsense that they on the Council would support it? if they had been at all indiscreet and taken the public into their confidence at an earlier stage the agreement would never have been made. For the last two or three, months Sir Willie e Lever and those wbo bad acted for him would only have been to glad to get out of the bargain which was started last May. The negotiations had been of a most delicate nature. It would have smashed the whole thing to have been indiscreet and yieloed to the public. He wished from the bottom of his heart that Mr Fray had his position on the Council. (A voice: " I dont.") If the ratepayers were not satisfied with their representatives, they had their remedy and in 1915 they could clear the whole lot out, if they desired. They had three repre- sentatives from that ward and altheugh they disagreed on other matters,they were all agreed on the one before the public now. After reading extracts from his laten- t() the Press the rev gentlemen urged all present to- vote according to their own dictates and not to be led away by the extreme members of the opposition who said that cottages would be dearer. They would not be, for the tenant did not pay the rates directly. If there was anything he was sure of in this world it was that they would make a vast blunder if they let such a splendid opportunity go by. (Applause.) The Chairman having remarked that great sanitary advantages would be secured, Mr T H Jones said that- Mr Smith referred to the paying qualities of the water- works and he (the speaker), wanted to know whether Mr Smith could tell what dividend had been paid on the capital. Mr Welford : We cannot tell. We have been allowed to see the balance sheets but we are not allowed to divulge what the Dewhurst's did with the profits. In reply to a further question, Mr Welford said that if by any chance there was a deficiency on what was purely revenue expenditure it would have to come out of the rates.</p>
        <p>Rev GB Thurston: Sopposing you had a balance</p>
        <p>in hand ?</p>
        <p>Mr Welf, rd : We Ehould keep the surplus to cover the losses if there were any. It would be worked on the same lines as the gtts works in regard to a balance or a deficit. The water rAte would be reduced if it were found that there was a surplus for a number of SPIVS. The question was asked if the tunnels,for which £1,500 were going to be paid, could be used for drinking water purposes and it \vats stated in reply that they had been used quite recently. They were underground reservoirs and were part of the waterworks property. Mr J H Holt, in proposing a vote of thanks to the chairman said that in years to come they would have boating, 3:c„ on the lake. (Applause.) Mr Fray having seconded the proposition, Mr W G. Groves supported and the Chairman briefly replied.</p>
        <p>/</p>
        <p>I'</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>NNW</p>
        <p>Britain's Land of Promise. A week from Liver- pool. Golden opportunities for competent agri- culturists who can quickly become land- lords. Dairying, cattle raising, fruit grm-i pauaarcdr:snTwanlsi roV t2es.d pmearrk et. IP acre--easy terms. Improved freehold farms can be .01.1?.. Grand bought for what climate. British farmers ..) Thousands ir,i; pay in ,130 r. • of acrec in rent. ~1,P."- peaches. Ideal edu- .o , el., cational social conditions. ‘‘.-- Bright Provects for female 0 domestics. The Government guarantee to place competent men and s women. Marvellous natural resources awaiting investors. Write Mr.N. B. Colcock, Ontario Government Agent, 263, Strand, London. Nl'</p>
