      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>SICK</head>
        <p>THE SICK</p>
        <p>The Reformation did great things for this dear and happy land of ours. The lamp of learning now burns bright ; hovels give place to noble cities; our ships sail in every ocean ; our fl,ig floats on almost every promontory of the earth and every island cf the sea ; the land is covered with our foctories and workshops ; and our population is flowing outwards in mighty waves, and peopling the ample rewiors of Canada. South Africa, Australia and New Zealand. I would invite Mr. Richardson and " Eureka," instead of scoffing at Protestants to coma out nobly and join us, and ba on the winning side. 0 brother man ! fold to thy heart thy brother ; Where pity dwells, the peace of God is there ; To worship rightly is to love each other. Each smile a hymn, each kindly deed a prayer. --I remain, &amp;c., PHIL SQUOD.</p>
        <p>B. T. PA-M.:ES AND H. OGDEN.—Your replies to Mr. Harold Smith on the Temperance question will he published next week. ED. "Es."</p>
        <p>CAST-OFF CLOTHING.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I remind your reacleis that Inspector Wainwright, National :Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, 12, Froghall-lque, Warrington, is very glad to receive cast-cif boots and clothing of any description which are a great help to him in his work. —Yours, kv,:c.,</p>
        <p>CONSTANCE BROA.DBENT, The Holhes, Latchford.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,— In the report of Mr. Bennett's speech at the Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society, which appeared in your issue of Saturday last, you state that " the Prime Minister (Mr . White) drew from him " the admission that he included food taxes &amp;c." I presume that your reporter intended to write "The Leader of the Opposition (Mr. Jarman) drew &amp;c." At the same time my impression is that Alderman Bennett stated his intentions without any hesitation, and that it was not necessary for anyone to attempt to draw admissions from him.—Yours &amp;c., E. AUBREY WHITE. 3, Egypt-street, Warrington. 20th January, 1913.</p>
        <p>ONAR LAW prescribed at Edinburgh last night as Chief Physician to the Sick Man of the Will the "food" be " food taxes." ?</p>
        <p>Roberts said that having seen the repeated devolution of duties upon corporate bodies by Acts of Parlia- ment, he confessed he seruetimes felt amazed at the viuiety and magnitude of the operations of the Town Council. There was harely a side of the individual citizen's He that vas not affected, more or less directly and ethics'., daily, by them. Councillor Roberts then ontlineu the work of the Council,especially touzhiug upon its control of the public health, the means of lighting and recreation, and the borough water supply. Teaching upon education, Councillor Roberts incidentally expressed the view that the destructi, n of School Boards which were doing a raognificent work in many centres, was a mistake. The operations of those Boards might well have been extended but in districts where th 're was no School Board, the constitution of an edncation authotity might have been made com- pulsory. lu Warrington the Education authority had 45 school departments to control. Of these only 12 were Council departments, the rest, with one exception being denominational. They had accommodation in the elementary scho)ls for 13,082 scholars, but there were 13,744 scholars on the bcoks. They employed 308 teachers, those in the Council schools being under the direct appointment of the Education Committee while those in the denominational schools were apeointed by the managers, subject to the controLing voice of the Education Committee. In the Secondary schools there were 1,656 scholars and 78 teacher; The cost of elementary education was £44,000 a year and that of secondary education 128,898. Councillor Roberts emphasised the fact that though the work of education was to a large extent delegateci to the Education Committee, the final anthority as to policy and expenditure was the Town Council. Alluding to the latest committee—the Insurar,ce Committee—Councillor Roberts said that, although they had been working very quietly for some considerable time and had had their difficulties to encounter, they had the great satisfaction of being told officially last week that the National Insurance COM- missioners looked upon Warrington as</p>
        <p>A MODEL TOWN and were extremely pleased with the progress and poEition of sffairs,so far as they had been administered. With reference to the finances of the borough, Coun- cillor Roberts pointed out that Warrington had a. capital debt of over £1,000,000 and an annual expendi- ture of about £102,000, the rates being 8s in the £. If it was necessary for a private business concern, if it valued its rrputation and desired to he successful, to get the highest ef:iciency, not merely in inanimate things but in brains, intelligence and experience,surely it was even more incumbent upon a huge, many-sided organization libe the Corporation, into uhich the personal rroprietory element did not enter to the same extent, to obtain the</p>
        <p>BEST OFFICIALS. Having Tuned Lord Rosebery and Mr Joseph Chamberlain in support of this view, Councilior Roberts said he bad no sympathy whatever with the kind of ratepayer who invariably complained when the Council advanced the salary of an official who had rendered long years of active and self-sacrificing service If thuse who knew the value of the work appreciated it at a certain figursi, it was parsimonious and detrimental to the best interests of the town to refuse to give a man what he was worth in the market. Continuing, Councillor Roberts asked his hearers whether they thought that the personnel of our Town Councils—he was not speaking of Warrington—was as good as it was twenty years ago ? (" No.") He found that question being asked up and down the country, for very serious reasons. Peisonally he had no hesitation in saying that, in his opinion, the kind of man, in the mass, who was now coming into the public service was neither educationally, socially, intellectually nor in any other way equal to the type of man who went into that work twenty years ago. Discussing the causes of the degeneration, Councillor Roberts contended that the exclusion of large commercial interests from direct representation was, in part, responsible If a man could only have a pious opinion about a question and could not give effect to it by his active support, in the shape of his vote, he could not maintain interest at fever beat very long ; he fait trammelled and that he was no use. Those whom he had in mud—the sons of men who were in the Council twenty years ago, when their con- cerns were private firms—and who would to a great extent be disposed to take up municipal work could not, because they had no qualification for nomination. It was perfectly true that a man could create a nominal qualification, but it was not everyone who would take the trouble to get himself rated in the borough for a sufficient amount to give him the vote. Of course, he (Councillor Roberts) attached no importance whatever to a man's mere social position. He did not care whero the brains came from. Ile did not care where the man who possessed them was born or what were the awkward and difficult circum- stances under which he was brought up. Any man who had conquered the difficulties of his birth and surroundings was all the more worthy of public con- fidence and 111 would rather see the Council absolutely crammed with manual workers, if they were men of intelligence,honesty, and honour, who sincerely desired to. do their best for the torn, than he would, by comparison, with any other body of men. He would as willingly trust himself, his reputation and all that he had to a representative body of workingmen as to any other class. (Applause ) The second cause of the deterioration was unquestionably the too frequent introduction of</p>
