      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>"VERY ADVANCED EDUCATION."</head>
        <p>"VERY ADVANCED EDUCATION."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your correspondent "Astonished " is labour- ing under a serious misapprehension. The Secondary School children " between the ages of 13 and 14" were not asked to write an essay on "The transmigration of my soul after death into three forms." They were to assume the possibility of the transmigration and to state the three forms they would like to become, describing their experiences in their changed conditions. Asa litersary man of some experience, I can think of nothing better fitted than such an exercise as this to stimulate the imagination or to lure the young mind into the pursuit of knowledge concerning the life- history of the creatures they choose for treatment. As the parent of one of these children of tender years," I thoroughly agree with the wise choice of the teacher who set the essay and can only lament that ill-informed and reckless critics are to be found ready to do their best to injure a splendid institution from which they do not scruple to take the many advan- tages it offers.—Yours &amp;c,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>SURPRISED ROMANISM v. PROTESTANTISM.</head>
        <p>SURPRISED ROMANISM v. PROTESTANTISM.</p>
        <p>.Sir,—I ant pleased to see my Roman critics have given me a further opportunity to reply. In regard to Mr Richardson's letter, I may say that his philo- sophical generalities are very indistinct and no-one would be able to gather from his remarks to what branch of the Church he belonged. He speaks of the Catholic Church, and this term in its modern sense is taken to imply that section of Christ's Church that has kept its faith and doctrines pure from its founda- tion. This cannot be said cf the Roman Church. The Roman Church is neither Catholic nor orthodox in having maintained the religious beliefs of the Christian Church. Take the main dogmas of her profession. Purgatory was not mentioned till the fourth century. You find no mention of the Mass till the fifth or sixth century. Auricular confession, though instituted in the fifth century, was not made compulsory till the 13th cwitury. Transub- stantiation was decreed in 1215, the Immaculate Conception in 1850, and Papal Infalli- bility in 1870. Then, as the times for reform draw near, the Roman Church became more corrupt, and she has not dared since the Reformation to hold a council or synod to review the state of her laelk,f, though she held vveral pro-Reformation councils. Turning to my friend "Eureka," I must congratulate him, on having made a graLd discovery in the Newman quotation. Let me point out that while this man was thus openly attacking Romanism he was secretly coquetting with Rome and for his equivocal services was made a Cardinal. Let me further point out that the evidence of a Church's usefulne:s does not con- sist of the amount of money spent on non-religious matters, but on the amount of piety and devotion, shown to the poor and afflicted and the dinctiou of sorrowing souls to the Blessed Redeemer. To return to Air. Richardson, I will now endeavour to sketch for him, what I think should he the aim of a true Church. That it should in the first place circulate and expound the Scriptures, teaching nothing but what is contained therein, that, while endeavouring to guide men in paths of true morality, it should eschew all matters appertaining to the State. Under such a dispensation, the human mind and the human soul would be able to wing its flight through all its fancies, and if man were true to man what a glorious state such a country would be in. Under such a dispensation, besides our holy faith, with its Sabbaths, its sanctuaries, peace in our land, virtue and love at our hearths, hope at our deathbeds, and the blessed words of inspiration dispelling the gloom about our graves, we should have every blessing life can bestow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>SMOKE AND PUBLIC HEALTH.</head>
        <p>SMOKE AND PUBLIC HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The two articles which have appeared in the " Examiner " upon the smoke nuisance fr,nn the pen of so able an authority as Professor Arthur Ruston are of practical use and interest to all those who recognise the value of plant lire in and about our towns Professor Ruston's experiments appear. to have been conducted in Leeds And the surrounding neighbourhood, and we can congratulate our- selves our local conditions are not so bad as in that great manufacturing Yorkshire town. But if our conditions are better it cannot be claimed tlqt this is in any way due to the merits of our smoke producers, manufacturing or household, or to any really vigorous and fearless use of the powers entrusted to our Health Committee. It is due entirely to the fact that compared with Leeds our town is a small one and is not so closely compacted together. It woul,;: be interesting and useful if some competent person would undertake investigations in Warrington similar to those made by Professor Ruston and others at Leeds.</p>
        <p>It must be apparent to all that the effect of smoke- pregnated atmosphere upon human life is very similar to that describ,-cl aid illustrated by Professor Ruston on vegetable life. This lowering of human vitality cannot be measured so accurately as in vegetable li.;e. Everyone living in the neighbourhood of \Varrington is about equally affected but a lowering of vital force being general is not appreciated. Perhaps the most serious consequences are experienced by the children who are in the same period of their gto:vth. Possibly the Medical Inspection going on \all over the country in our Elernenta:.y schools will in time provide figures by which useful comparisons can be made between town and country children. lam of opinion that town children will be found generally to be like the plants Professor Ruston describes and illustrates, less in both weight and stature than those in the country. It may be contended that there are other conditions prejudiciously affecting the growth of town children. This may be eo, but I doubt if any two causes are so potent in their effect upon growth as pure air and sunshine. The first step to be taken locally is a collection of reliable facts, both in regard to vegetation and human life so that comparisons can be instituted between Warrington and adjoining districts far enough removed to be outside the area appreciably affected by Warrington smoke. Sir Oliver Lodge says on this subject: " When we consider life as it is and life as it might be we are struck with the caritrast. People now are realising that life as it is is not life as it ought to be, that all this squalor, extreme plvertY, dirt and smoke, aril other things among which we are more or less content to live, are not the condi- tions that the human race ought to put, up with. but conditions which they themselves have brought about and which themselves can mend. The earth is beautiful in the extreme, in places where nobody lives. But in any place where people have decided that they wish to live in large numbers together, for social purposes, for business purposes for conveniences of all kinds, there they have taken no precautions to keep it beautiful, there they have made it ugly, there they have spoilt the f of the eir A healthy public setiment in regard to the smoke question is of slow growth in Warrington, but as Sir Oliver Lodge says " People are realising that life is not what it ought to be.' Many manufacturers are indifferent and seem scarcely to realize their obligation to their poorer neighbours who have to live in the town, and the influence a hich their indifference has upon the general health and the brightness and cleanliness of the town. But a desire for better conditions is surely, if slowly, growing, and means must be taken that this public sentiment should find practical expression through the .governing body, viz. : the Town Council.</p>
        <p>ANTI-FILTH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>WOLF ROCK LIGHTHOUSE RELIEVED.</head>
        <p>WOLF ROCK LIGHTHOUSE RELIEVED.</p>
        <p>Seven weeks after the proper date the keepers of the Wolf Rock Lighthouse were relieved on Wednesday by a Trinity steamer from the Scilly Islands. Tney had been isolated by the continuous storms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>WOMAN IN MAN'S CLOTHING.</head>
        <p>WOMAN IN MAN'S CLOTHING.</p>
        <p>Elizabeth Ford, 52, a woman Who went about in her son's clothes, instead of appearing in answer to a summons, ani denied her identity when arrested on a warrant, was sentenced to a month's hard labour for assault at London Sessions on Wednesday. She threw a glass at the son of a Kensington publican.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>TARDY TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>TARDY TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Monday, Arthur Atherton and Joseph Darlington. members of the Territorial Force, were summoned for not having put in the necessary drills. They pleaded guilty,—Atherton said that owing to the deaths of his father and uncle he was the principal support of the house, and he had to work harder.— Darlington said he was out of work at the time, and had no boots to attend drills. Atherton was let off on payment of tosts, and Darlington was lined 5s and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>PARTY POLITICS</head>
        <p>PARTY POLITICS</p>
        <p>into municipal matters. He bad, in some respect, to wear a white sheet about that, because, for many years, there was no stronger partisan than himself. That was the school in which he was reared—he thought a man ought to be first a Liberal and then a public man. He had altered his opinions about that, so far as local adminis- tration was concerned. If the Council had the oppor- tunity of administering the affairs of Warrington,not on the lines set out iii Acts of Parliament, but on lines consistent with their own views and modes of thought from a political standpoint, then he could see the desirability, and even the necessity of choosing their candidates from men of their own stamp, who would impart to their work the principles in which they believed. But that was not the case. They were left with nothing but administration in their hands and for the life of him he could not see what association there was between a man's views upon imperial political questions and the methods by which they kept their streets clean, treated cases of infectious disease, or discharged any other functions of a corporate body. It was only when they saw a party trying to gain for itself—to the exclusion of others—the honours and privileges which a Corporation occasionally had to confer, that the party spirit asserted itself. The danger was—in Warrington it</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT ALDERMAN BENNETT CARRIES HIS BILL.</p>
        <p>Alderman A. Bennett's Tariff Reform Bill was passed by the Parliamentary Debating Society on Thursday evEning by eighteen votes to nine, after a fairly interesting debate. Councillor J HAWTHORN (L. Montrose) in the absence of Mr C E Parker (L. Dundee), resumed the attack from the Opposition. In the first place they might all congratulate Alderman Bennett on his consistency in that matter and they admired his courage in bringing ton\ ard a scheme of Colonial Preference a a time V% hen such a scheme was under a cloud. (Hear, hear.) The Conservatives had practicaly deserted the nclicy of food taxes, and that, in the eyes of the Opposition, postponed Aldermen Bennett's Bill sine die. Ho was following the policy announced by Mr Chamberlain. When he broueht forward such a scheme he said that in order to carry it out there must be a tax on food. The Bill they hen before them did involve a tax on food. (Hear, hear.) NI. Chamberlain's idea was to have a self-coetained Empire and give advantages and preference to the colonies. It was to encourage manufactures in this country and agriculture in the Colonies. They had seen since that the Colonies would not be content with a scheme of that sort. They wanted to develop their (exit resources. In regard to /the proposal that Great Britain should impose ft tax on imported fond stuffs at the expense of the con- sumer, the Canadians had scouted the idea. The idea in the Bill before the House had been frustrated by the Conservatives theeselvf S. It wts based on a erect fallacy—the that tariffs were a binding factor in uait, gtheEfamnapicr);. The whole history of the world proven that was a fallacy. It was not necessary to remind members of that House that we lost the American Colonies by attempting to impose tariffs on them. Might it he far from the day when England mould break the Empire by putting taxes on the Colonies. Instead of making a ringed fence round the Colonies, that idea of tariffs would be</p>
        <p>LIKE A ROPE OF SAND , and instead of binding us together would 1 ave the , opposite effect and practically exclude 80111 e of the Colonies from the Empire. Even the small tax it was proposed to put on bacon would increase the price ore halfpenny per lb. A tax on wheat would mean one farthiug on every loaf of bread consumed in the country. Alderman Bennett had spoken disapprovingly of the Home Rule Bill. Cecil Ithodes,who had similar ideas to Alderman Bennett's on imperial questions, was in favour of the granting of Home Rule to Ireland. He wanted to see an imperial governmeet to which the Colonies would send representatives and discuss imperial matters. If we were to have that we must get rid of local questions first, concerning Ireland, Wales and Scotland, and grant them facilities so that they might have a share of local government, If the local questions were settled, Par- liament would have more time to discuss imperial matters. (Hear, hear.) In regard to defence it was .ornetirnes thought by Imperialists that we were indebted to the Colonies whereas it was the Colonit s who were indebted to us. We supplied them with the means of defence costing 113 S.:80,000A° per sear ad it was as little as they could do to return that compli- ment in the best way. (Applause.) Mr. E. ENGLAND (1., Warrington) raid that when they looked into tbe history ot the scheme of federa- tion they foucd that it sprang from the colonies, but up to now almost a deaf ear had been turned to them. On looking round we found that all the political parties in our dominions and colonies tad adsocatei a scneme of federation with this country. It was a remark- able thing that it was only the Liberal and Labour parties in this country who were opposed to any scheme of federation at all. (A VOICE : " Question.") There was no question about it. They would, however, very shortly have to toe the line in that respect. (Laughter.) He (the speaker) S% aS not in agreement with the methods adopted by the Colonial Secretary to attain his objects, although he had a certain amount of sympathy with those objects. The great leader Mr. Boner Law—(A VOICE "Whose leader ?")—had declared himself to be a free fooder. The great bulk of the Tariff Reform party in this country were tree fooders. (Lsughter)• The Bill hefore them was not a Tariff Reform scheme et ea; it sins only a question of imperial defence. He was not one of those who were afraid of the German scare. He would ask the House to adopt some system of federation but be did not for one moment urge the adoption of the Colonial Secrety'ss scheme. Mr LEA WARBoRToN (C. Wsst Bristol) said they all knew that the Bill was intended first ot all fora defence fund. (A Voice : Wisat is it worth ?) The colonies bad shown themselves willing to help in the eetripment of the ta,vy and in other ways for defensive purposes. There was at present nothing to tie the colonies to us and some scheme of federation was necessary or else they would one by one depart. Mr E WYNNE JONES (L , Chester) said they on that side of the House were in sympathy with the view that the Empire should he federated and united. (Hear, hear aud " good"). Ile was glad he had said something in that House which met with the approval of the member for East Belfast. (Laughter.) Alderman Bennett bad run up the flag and if Benar Law would run , it down, the Colonial Secretary was prepared to nail his colours to the mast. He told them he was a dreamer. ' Empires were not built up on dreams and were not made of the stuff which dreams were made of. They wanted something more practical than that. (A Voicer; "What about the Insurance Act.") Let them assume that the matters outlined in the Bill were absolutely necessary. No member spposite could prove that those things could not be accomplished without tinkering and tampering with the system of this couetry and the Colonies. Tariff Reformers were always telling them how the wonderful German Empire was federated together, yet it was federated on a Free Trade basis. (Hear. hear.) 'rhe questien should be raised above ordinary politics. ' It was not a party question and if the. Colonial Secretary would consent to amend the Bill and leave out the question of tariffs, the Liberal Party would support it. The reason people in certain protected countries had to eat horse and dog flesh—called "Spanish ruutton"—was to he totted in their Protectionist, system. (Laughter.) That statement was made be the Tariff Reform papers. Mr E A WHITE (Prime Minister) said a scheme of ' Colonial Preference was a commonsense, business one. Mr Boner Law bad said that if the Conservative Party were returned to power, be would call a meeting of Colonial premiers and discuss the question with them ; and if as a result of that meeting, it wes found by ell, that it was absolutely necessary to put a tax on food in order to bring about Colonial preference, then a small limited tax would be put on food. In conclusion he (the speaker) said that he was hoping the member for Dundee (Mr CE Parker) would have been present that night because he had recollections of seeing him at the last election with a black loaf of bread. (Laughter.) He did not know whether he had carried any dog meat about with him. (Laughter.) We should be prepared in time of war. A country at war wits in one of the elementary stages of savagery. He hoped we should enjoy immunity from it for a long time. We had been free from invasion because we had ben p•eptred to rrsist it. THE CoLONIAL SECRETARY (Alderman Bennett) made a lengthy reply in the course of which be said the referendum was a sound democratic principle to advocate. The only courses for Tariff Reformers was to go the whole bog and to do anything else was to desert the fanner and betray the Empire. He was a dreamer and many of the great statesmen of to-day had been dreamers. Unless they bad the power to look beycnd the end of their noses they could not get any states- manlike measure at all. (Applause). On a division the Bill was passed by eighteen votes to nine.</p>
        <p>There will be no meeting on Thursday next, but on Thursday week the President of the Board of Trade will move" that this House condemns the Home Rule Bill introduced by His Majesty's Government at Westminster."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>MAN</head>
        <p>MAN</p>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL CRITICISMS,</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS ON THE MODERN TYPE OF CANDIDATE. --- Politics in Municipal Lire.</p>
        <p>Councill,r H ROBERTS delivered a thoughtful and informing address tla The affairs of a Town Council" before the members of the .Warrington Junior Liberal Association at their club ri•onis in florsemarket-street on Thurrday evening. Mr E Luo ii JoNEs, who presided, said that if there was one public man who had done more than another for the borough of Warrington it was Councillor floberts. He added lustre to every department of life in which he, interested himself and no-one was better qualified ko speak on municipal affairs. If young men did not take a keen interest in the progress of their own town it was not likely that they would be able to exert much influence on the national life.</p>
        <p>1 Councillor Roeraas prefaced his address observing that, as one who could look back upon a long period of active and somewhat aggressive work in connection with Liberalism, he did not think he had ever seen a more intelligent and zealous interest manifested in the political questioLs of the day than was being taken by the young men of the present day. He could not speak for the young men who belonged to the Con- servative party—he would not desire to make any reference to them, except what would he quite friendly but the education of the last twenty-five 3 ears and the preparation of the minds cf the youth of this country for the reception of facts had developed at such a pace that he had no fear for the future of England, so far as it might he eutrustad to the Liberal party. Dealing first with the method by which the Town Council was elected, Councillor Roberts con- trasted the Parliamentary aud municipal franchises, and said the registration law was full of anomalies which sooner or later would be rectified. Why Parliament, in its wisdom or lack of it, should entrust a man with the more responsible power of voting for a man to represent him in the House of Commons and refuse him the opportunity of voting for a town coun- cillor, was one of those things he could not under- stand. L'Agers and service occupiers were entitled to vote in Parliamentary elections, yet they were denied the municipal vote. A large class of ratepayers was not represented at all on the municipal franchise. Since the Limited Liability Acts came into operation. a very large. number of firms—in fact nearly all firms of any great si %A —had been converted into limited companies. Muni they were privata concerns, it did not matter how many partners there were,as long as the rateable value cave £lO a year for each partner, every- one of them was entitled to a Parliamentary and municipal vote, providing he lived within seven miles of tbe borough borindary. The limited companies, however, were ab.olutely disfranchieed front taking any direct part by nomination or voting for any candidate for municipal honours. The same diEqualifi- cation attached to rail w iy,carrying and. canal companies. The rates of Warrington now realized over £lOO,OOO per annum, and of that amount 35 per eery; was paid by disfianchised limited companies and 15 per cent by railway and canal companies arid kindred concerns. In other words, approximately one h ilf of the entire rates of the town was paid by</p>
        <p>CONCERNS WHICH HAD NO REPRESENTATION and no voice whatever in the control of the town's affairs. such a condition of things ought, in the best interests of the communtty, to be remedied as speedily as possible. One half of the ratepaying interest of Warrington c mtrolled the whole ot the financial affairs of the town, but the representation of the town in the Council was confined to one half of its ratepaying element. In that connection he wished to refer, in the best humour, to a claim often put forward by a body of gentlemen for whom, in the main, he had the greatest possible respect, and he did not wish anything he said to be taken as reflecting upon theta. He was Fpeakiog of</p>
        <p>PROPERTY-oWNING CLASS. They had in Warrington, as in all other towns, a large number of people, many of whom had, by frugal and careful habits, saved a little money and invested it in property. There were others who had made a business of erecting property, for sale or occupation or as an investmsnt. Whenever there was an item of expenditure which was necessary for any public improvement about to be incurred and which required the promotion of a Bill, they wero always met with a great deal of opposition from particular quarters and that opposition frequently associated itself with a select number of the property owning class who constituted and called themselves the ratepayers of the town. He wanted to show them what a huge fallacy that pretension was and to dispel once and for all the claims of property owners, as such, to be considered hig ratepayers. No property owner had a vote in respect of his property, hut only in respect of the house or business premises which he occupied. He was not a ratepayer in the sense that he was the owner of 10, 20, 100 or even 1,000 cottages. He charged an inclusive rest, which covered the amount of the rates assessed upon the property of which he was the owner. He collected from the tenant in the shape of rent, the rent proper plus the rates, and be paid over every half year into the borough funds the amount of rates upon the property. He received a consideration for doing that, in the shape of a discount, and the arrange- ment was extremely convenient and mutually satisfac- tory. The great bulk of the owners of cottage and other property were honourable men and broad-minded citizens, but there was a class which was a distinct hindrance to progress but in regard to whom he did not want to be too severe. He had known cases—the last Corporation Bill was one—in which they had gone round to their tenants and said : "If you vote for this measure, this expenditure of money will lead to an increase in the amount of rates to be levied,and if tbe rates go up,your rents will go up." What, therefore, became of the claim of those people to be considered as ratepayers at all, when they told their tenants that the increase of rates would be levied upon them ? Passing to a con- sideration of the functions of the Council, Councillor</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-01-25_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>DORIC SIMPLICITY.</head>
        <p>DORIC SIMPLICITY.</p>
        <p>The Dorians in ancient Greece were of simple habits one of which was ' the partaking of common meals. This custom was known as Syssitia or Pheiditia, and prevailed also in Sparta. The men did not dine with the rest ot the family, but with;other males who formed small parties, each individual contributing a share towards the meal. The contributions were mostly in kind, the various items supplied by the diners being ore-arranged. Whenever obtainable, the Greek grape was on the table. They knew its wonderful food value and savoury properties and so do we. That is why Currant Recipes are always acceptable. Try this one.</p>
        <p>CERVANT PATTIES.</p>
        <p>loz. cornflour, 4ozs. currants, 1 lemon, 4oz. castor 3ugar, loz. butter, short-crust paste. Method.—Line some patty pans with thinly rolled out short-crust paste, providing paste covers for each. Mix the cornflour with a gill of cold water, stir in the grated rind and juice of lemon, the currants and the sugar, cook all together for five minutes, stirring all the time, and add the butter; mix well, fill the paste- lined patty pans with the preparation, wet the edges of the paste, put on the covers, brush over with sweet milk and bake 20 minutes.</p>
      </div>
