        <p>MRS. LLOYD GEORGE AND SOCIAL REFORM Mrs Lloyd George, who was the hostess at a reception by memb, ri of the Ladle.' Liberal Social Council to mei); the Liberal constituents of Hackney on Wednes- day evening, said she was glad to hear them cheer the Insurance Act. The measure had been much abused in the past, but it was now safely launched, and was going under good sail In a favourable wind, it had been said of many social reformers that they were the end of all things. No doubt they bad beard that about the Insurance Act; but said Mrs Lloyd George, England could still stand a good many more social reforms, despite the fact that it was flourishing, and that trade was keeping up: and she trusted that the working classes would continue to have social reforms better than they had ever been before. (Cheers).</p>
        <p>MR ROBERT ARTHUR'S AFFAIRS. Mr Robert Arthur, the well-known theatrical manager. who was to have appeared for public examina- tion in the London Bankruptcy Curt on Wednesday, is suffering from a nervous breakdown, and the examina- tion was adjourned. Liabilities are estimated at. £47,231, and assets at .£935. Among the debtor's expenses since July 1909 is included a sum of £25,000 for interest on borrowed money.</p>
        <p>tion of a treat of an altogether different character from the one which awaited them. They hastened up the hillside and, on reaching the eminence whence the glorious view was obtainable, one of the advance guard turned and shouted to his approaching corn- pani ns,"Oh, heck, there's nowt here.kV ! There isn't even a roundabout !" " Well, I'll goa to seeha !" was the disgusted comment, and the disappointed trippeis returned even faster than they had come. When one of their spokesmen announced his determination to "go to sea," I presume he had Blackpool in mind. with the South Sore "pleasure " ground for pt efer- ence. "EXCEPT." I note that in the course of last week's lecture, Mr Champion, B.Sc., facetiously observed that a natural history photograper took photographs of all kinds of subjects, " Except perhaps buildings and p-rsons." I once repo' ted the late Oscar Wilde. He was speaking on art in the home, and among other things of the kind he said—" Don't hag your relatives' p n.traits on the walls of your house—unless they are highly decorative." By the way, on the same occasion, denouncing the white marble mantlepiece, he added— But what are you to do with? To sell it or pass it on to some other householder would be distinctly immoral." No doubt Oscar Wilde was a judge.</p>
