        <p>was not an active danger, but there were places where a man's politics were made the question is to his fitness for election to a paid official position. When they once got that far, they were not very far from municipal corruption on a broad and had scale. In conclusion Councillor Roberts said that civic life demanded and deserved from those who load the leisure, ability,intelli- gence and culture to give that service the very best that they could give. That was his feeling very strongly, and it was in the hope that something he had said might encourage the cultivation of the spirit of peisoual responsibility to the district in which they lived, that he had spoken as he hod done that night. (Applause.) On the proposition of the CHAIRMAN, seconded by Mr L WALLINGTON, a hearty vote of thanks was passed to Councillor Roberts for his address.</p>
